    "content": "{\n  \"lesson\": 6,\n  \"author\": \"claude-opus-5 (Teacher)\",\n  \"domain\": \"Agentic Python tooling for code-intelligence systems\",\n  \"notes\": \"Procedure-and-conventions batch, grounded in the operator's actual repositories (repo2graph v5.0.0, 142 PRs, 102 test files, 13 CI workflows, uv.lock, REUSE compliance). Deliberately teaches *how work is done here* rather than *what is true here*: the measured result across lessons 1-3 is that LoRA at this scale learns phrasing and loses factual density, so repo inventories, versions and paths went into profile/PROJECTS.md as system-prompt context instead. What weights can hold is the shape of a correct response - ordered steps, verification against the Makefile before claiming done, conventional-commit titles, naming the closed issue, treating lockfile and licence compliance as CI-enforced rather than optional. Calibrated to the eval reference distribution like lesson_05: 1200-1700 characters per output, 8+ distinct numbers, since lesson_05's calibration recovered answer length from 0.41x to 0.68x.\",\n  \"examples\": [\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"I want to change how repo2graph formats its answer output. Plan the work, including what could break that I might not think of.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Output format in this repo is not a free surface - there are golden-file tests. The tests directory holds 102 test files plus a golden/ directory, so a format change deliberately breaks goldens and the diff has to be reviewed rather than regenerated blindly.\\n\\nSecond non-obvious breakage: the project ships as more than a library. There is an action.yml GitHub Action, an npm/ wrapper, editors/ integrations and a server.json MCP manifest. Anything parsing the answer downstream is affected, and the MCP reply shape is a contract with coding agents.\\n\\nThird: the examples workflow runs the tool against real repositories, so a format change can fail examples.yml even when unit tests pass.\\n\\nVerification order matters. make format-check and make lint are seconds; make typecheck is tens of seconds; make test is minutes. Run them cheapest-first so a trivial lint failure does not wait behind the suite.\\n\\nThe PR should say what it closes and use a conventional-commit title with a scope, matching the 142 PRs already in this repo.\",\n      \"output\": \"```\\n1. locate   the formatting path (answer.py and whatever cli.py calls)\\n2. change   the formatter, not its callers\\n3. goldens  re-run and READ the diff in tests/golden/ - do not bulk-accept\\n4. verify   make format-check -> make lint -> make typecheck -> make test\\n5. examples confirm examples.yml still passes; it runs against real repos\\n6. PR       conventional-commit title with a scope, naming the issue it closes\\n```\\n\\nThree things that break and are easy to miss:\\n\\n- **Golden files.** `tests/` holds **102** test files and a `golden/` directory. A format change is *supposed* to fail them; the review is the diff, so regenerating without reading it defeats the test's purpose.\\n- **Four downstream consumers.** This ships as a GitHub Action (`action.yml`), an npm wrapper (`npm/`), editor integrations (`editors/`), and an MCP server (`server.json`). The MCP reply shape is a contract with coding agents - changing it is a breaking change and the title needs `feat!:`, as PR #527 did when it dropped Python 3.10.\\n- **The examples workflow.** `examples.yml` runs the tool against real repositories, so it can fail while all **102** unit test files pass.\\n\\nRun the checks cheapest-first. `make format-check` and `make lint` take seconds, `make typecheck` tens of seconds, `make test` minutes - a trivial lint failure should not wait behind the suite. `make test-serial` exists for tests that cannot run in parallel; reach for it when a parallel run is flaky, not by default.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Reviewing my last batch of fixes turned up three new problems. Should I add them to the same PR or open another? Plan it.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"This repo already has an answer encoded in its history: PR #531 is titled 'Land the audit-round-7 fixes, and fix the double close reviewing them found', and #512 is 'Land the #497-#509 fixes, and fix what reviewing them found'. So the established pattern is to fold review findings into the same batch PR, and to say so in the title.\\n\\nThe reasoning behind that pattern: findings from reviewing a batch are usually caused by the batch. Splitting them into a separate PR means main briefly contains the first batch with a known defect, and the second PR's diff is unreadable without the first.\\n\\nThe exception is scope: if a finding is unrelated to the batch - a pre-existing bug the review happened to notice - it belongs in its own PR, because bundling it makes the batch impossible to revert cleanly.\\n\\nSo the test is causality, not convenience: did this batch cause the finding?\\n\\nVerification before pushing: the full Makefile sequence, because a fix-upon-fix is exactly where a regression hides.\",\n      \"output\": \"**Same PR, if the batch caused them** - that is already this repo's pattern. PR #531 is literally *\\\"Land the audit-round-7 fixes, and fix the double close reviewing them found\\\"*, and #512 is *\\\"Land the #497-#509 fixes, and fix what reviewing them found\\\"*. Two of the **142** PRs here say it in the title.\\n\\nThe reason it is right: a finding produced by reviewing a batch is usually *caused* by that batch. Splitting it means `main` briefly carries the first batch with a known defect, and the follow-up diff cannot be read without the first one open beside it.\\n\\n**The test is causality, not convenience:**\\n\\n| finding | goes where |\\n|---|---|\\n| caused by this batch | same PR, mentioned in the title |\\n| pre-existing, review merely noticed it | its own PR |\\n\\nThe second row matters for revert safety: bundling an unrelated fix makes the batch impossible to revert cleanly, and with **20** closed-unmerged PRs in this repo's history, reverting is clearly a live option.\\n\\n**Then:**\\n\\n1. amend the branch, do not open a second one\\n2. extend the title: `Land the X fixes, and fix what reviewing them found`\\n3. re-run `make lint`, `make typecheck`, `make test` in full - a fix-upon-fix is exactly where a regression hides, and **3** new changes on top of a reviewed batch is enough to warrant the whole suite rather than the touched tests\\n4. name every issue number the PR closes, as `(#462)` does in the examples PR\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"I asked you to fix something in repo2graph and you found the fix needs a dependency bump. Plan how to handle that without tripping CI.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Dependencies here are locked with uv and there is a dedicated lockfile workflow among the 13 CI workflows. So editing pyproject.toml without regenerating uv.lock fails CI, and the failure is in a workflow separate from the test run, which is easy to misread as unrelated.\\n\\nThere are also three dependency-focused workflows: dependency-audit, dependency-review and dependabot-triage. A bump therefore faces review of the new version's advisories, not just resolution.\\n\\nThe Python floor matters: requires-python is >=3.11 after PR #527 dropped 3.10 while clearing all 31 Dependabot alerts. A dependency whose new version demands 3.12 would raise the floor again, which is a breaking change needing feat! and a deliberate decision rather than a side effect of a bug fix.\\n\\nREUSE compliance is also enforced - REUSE.toml and a compliance workflow - so any new file carries a licence header.\\n\\nSequencing: make the smallest bump that fixes the bug, regenerate the lock, run the local checks, and state the advisory situation in the PR body so dependency-review has a human answer beside it.\",\n      \"output\": \"The trap is that the lock is enforced by a **separate** workflow, so the failure looks unrelated to your change.\\n\\n```\\n1. bump    the minimum needed version in pyproject.toml, nothing else\\n2. lock    regenerate uv.lock - do not hand-edit it\\n3. local   make lint -> make typecheck -> make test\\n4. body    state what the bump fixes and any advisories it closes\\n```\\n\\nWhat is watching you, out of the **13** CI workflows:\\n\\n- **`lockfile`** - fails if `pyproject.toml` moved and `uv.lock` did not. This is the one people miss, because the test job may be green beside it.\\n- **`dependency-review`** and **`dependency-audit`** - look at the new version's advisories, not just whether it resolves.\\n- **`dependabot-triage`** - the alert backlog is managed, not ignored. PR #527 cleared all **31** alerts at once.\\n- **`compliance`** - REUSE rules via `REUSE.toml` and `LICENSES/`, so a new file without a licence header fails here.\\n\\n**One decision to escalate rather than absorb:** if the new version requires Python 3.12, it raises `requires-python` above the current **>=3.11**. That is a breaking change - PR #527 used `feat!:` for exactly this when it dropped **3.10** - and it should be a deliberate choice, not a side effect of a bug fix. Say so and let the floor move in its own PR.\\n\\nKeep the bump minimal. A bug fix that also pulls **2** or **3** unrelated majors cannot be reverted without reverting the fix.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"CI is failing only on the Windows runner. The Linux jobs are green. Plan the diagnosis.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"This repo has history here: PR #490 is 'fix(ci): stop the Windows runner's load failing the build', and audit round 5 included a Windows typecheck fix. So Windows-only failures are a recurring class rather than a novelty, and the likely causes are known.\\n\\nThe usual suspects, in order of frequency for a Python project of this shape: path separators and drive letters in tests that compare strings; file encoding, because the Windows default is cp1252 rather than UTF-8 and any test reading a fixture without an explicit encoding can fail on a non-ASCII byte; file locking, since Windows refuses to delete an open file, which breaks temp-directory cleanup; and line endings in golden-file comparisons, where CRLF versus LF makes an identical file compare unequal.\\n\\nDiagnosis order should be cheapest-first: read which test failed and how, before reproducing anything. Reproducing a Windows CI failure locally is possible here because the operator is on Windows 11, which is an advantage not every project has.\\n\\nThe golden-file angle is specific to this repo given tests/golden/ exists and there are 102 test files.\",\n      \"output\": \"Windows-only failures are a known class in this repo - PR #490 is `fix(ci): stop the Windows runner's load failing the build`, and audit round 5 included a Windows typecheck fix. So check the usual four before theorising.\\n\\n```\\n1. read     which test failed and the exact assertion - do not reproduce yet\\n2. classify against the four known causes below\\n3. reproduce locally: the operator is on Windows 11, so this is cheap here\\n4. fix + add a case that would have caught it on Linux too\\n```\\n\\n| cause | signature |\\n|---|---|\\n| path separators | assertion diffs showing `\\\\` vs `/`, or a drive letter |\\n| **encoding** | `UnicodeDecodeError` - the Windows default is cp1252, not UTF-8 |\\n| file locking | cleanup errors; Windows refuses to delete an open file |\\n| line endings | golden comparison fails on an apparently identical file (CRLF vs LF) |\\n\\nEncoding is the highest-prior guess for this codebase, because any of the **102** test files reading a fixture without an explicit `encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\"` will pass on Linux and fail on Windows the moment a non-ASCII byte appears - and model output is full of characters like `\u2248` (U+2248).\\n\\nLine endings are second, because `tests/golden/` exists: a golden file checked out with CRLF compares unequal to LF-generated output, which reads as a content bug and is not one.\\n\\n**Fix the test as well as the code.** A Windows-only failure usually means the assertion was over-specific - comparing a rendered path string rather than a `Path` - so after fixing, make the case fail on Linux too if the bug recurs. Otherwise the next of the **13** workflows to catch it is still only the Windows one.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"supply_chain\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"We want to add a new tree-sitter language to repo2graph. Walk through the supply-chain and compliance obligations specific to this repo.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Four enforcement mechanisms exist in this repo and each creates a concrete obligation.\\n\\nFirst, uv.lock plus the lockfile workflow: the new grammar package must be added to pyproject.toml and the lock regenerated, or CI fails in a job separate from tests.\\n\\nSecond, REUSE compliance via REUSE.toml and LICENSES/: a grammar is third-party code with its own licence, so adding it means recording that licence, not just the dependency. This is the obligation most easily forgotten because nothing about 'add a language' suggests a licensing step.\\n\\nThird, dependency-review and dependency-audit: the grammar's advisories are checked. Many tree-sitter grammar packages ship prebuilt shared objects, so the artifact is machine code that nobody in the review chain compiled - and this parser consumes untrusted input, namely arbitrary repository source, which makes a memory-safety bug reachable.\\n\\nFourth, examples.yml: PR #489 added Rails and Spring Framework to the gallery, so a new language is expected to come with an example, and the gallery check has a path-boundary rule that PR #488 fixed.\\n\\nSo the obligations are: lock, licence, advisory review with attention to prebuilt binaries, and a gallery example.\",\n      \"output\": \"Four mechanisms in this repo each create an obligation, and the licensing one is the easiest to forget:\\n\\n1. **Lock it.** Add the grammar to `pyproject.toml` and regenerate `uv.lock`. The `lockfile` workflow fails independently of the test job, so a green test run tells you nothing about this.\\n2. **License it.** `REUSE.toml` plus `LICENSES/` means a third-party grammar's licence has to be recorded, and the `compliance` workflow enforces it. Nothing about \\\"add a language\\\" hints at a licensing step, which is exactly why it gets missed.\\n3. **Review the artifact, not just the version.** `dependency-review` and `dependency-audit` check advisories, but the real concern is that many tree-sitter grammar packages ship **prebuilt** `.so` files - machine code nobody in the review chain compiled. This matters more here than in most projects because the parser consumes **untrusted input**: arbitrary repository source. A memory-safety bug in a grammar is reachable by anyone who can land a file in a scanned repo.\\n4. **Add a gallery example.** PR #489 added Rails and Spring Framework to the gallery, so a new language is expected to arrive with one, and `examples.yml` will run it. Note PR #488 fixed the gallery leak check to match at a path boundary - so the example must respect that rule rather than work around it.\\n\\n**Require before merge:** a hash-pinned version, either a source build in CI or documented provenance tying the binary to an upstream commit, and the suite passing with networking disabled to prove there is no import-time grammar download.\\n\\nIf the grammar cannot build from source and its provenance cannot be established, reject it. For a tool whose entire purpose is parsing other people's code, \\\"we could not verify what the parser is\\\" is sufficient grounds on its own.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"supply_chain\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Dependabot has opened 12 alerts on repo2graph. Plan how to clear them, given how this repo has handled it before.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"There is a precedent to follow: PR #527, 'feat!: clear all 31 Dependabot alerts (drops Python 3.10)'. Two things are notable. The alerts were cleared as one batch rather than individually, and clearing them forced a breaking change which was declared in the title with feat!.\\n\\nSo the approach: triage first, batch the ones that resolve without a floor change, and escalate any that force one.\\n\\nThe dependabot-triage workflow exists, so triage is partly automated and its output should be read before doing anything manually.\\n\\nThe floor question is the decision point. requires-python is currently >=3.11. If an alert can only be cleared by a version requiring 3.12, that drops 3.11 users and is a breaking change needing its own deliberate PR, not a quiet inclusion in a security batch.\\n\\nVerification: the full Makefile sequence, plus dependency-audit and dependency-review in CI, plus the lockfile workflow since uv.lock moves.\\n\\nAnd the audit-round pattern applies: expect reviewing the batch to surface more work, and fold that into the same PR.\",\n      \"output\": \"Follow the precedent: PR #527 was `feat!: clear all 31 Dependabot alerts (drops Python 3.10)` - cleared as **one batch**, with the breaking consequence declared in the title.\\n\\n```\\n1. triage   read dependabot-triage output first; it is already automated\\n2. split    alerts that resolve inside >=3.11  |  alerts that force the floor up\\n3. batch    fix group one in a single PR, regenerate uv.lock\\n4. escalate group two separately - do not hide a floor bump in a security batch\\n5. verify   make lint -> make typecheck -> make test, then watch 4 CI workflows\\n```\\n\\n**The decision point is the Python floor.** It is currently **>=3.11**, after #527 dropped **3.10**. If one of the **12** alerts can only be cleared by a version requiring **3.12**, that drops every 3.11 user - a breaking change deserving its own PR and its own `feat!:`. Bundling it into a security batch means someone accepting a security fix also accepts an unannounced floor bump.\\n\\n**Four workflows will have an opinion:** `lockfile` (because `uv.lock` moves), `dependency-review` and `dependency-audit` (the replacement versions' own advisories), and `compliance` if any vendored file changes.\\n\\n**Expect a second round.** This repo's history says reviewing a batch produces more findings - #531 and #512 both say so in their titles - so plan for a follow-up commit on the same branch rather than treating the first green run as done.\\n\\nOne caution against over-batching: **12** alerts spanning unrelated packages may not be revertible as a unit. If two of them touch the parsing path, split those out so a regression there does not force reverting the other **10**.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"supply_chain\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"repo2graph has no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md. Is that a problem, and what would you put in one?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"It is a real gap, and the evidence is this very conversation: the conventions had to be reconstructed from the Makefile, 13 workflow files, and 142 PR titles. An agent without that reconstruction will guess, and the specific guesses it will get wrong are knowable.\\n\\nThe highest-value content is whatever is both load-bearing and not discoverable from a quick look. Four candidates.\\n\\nOne: which clone is authoritative. There are two repo2graph checkouts and the C: one is two major versions stale at 3.0.0 versus 5.0.0. An agent reading the stale one will quote requires-python >=3.10 when the answer is >=3.11.\\n\\nTwo: the Makefile is the entry point, with the cheapest-first order and the existence of test-serial.\\n\\nThree: golden files are a contract, not an artifact to regenerate.\\n\\nFour: the lockfile, REUSE and examples obligations, because they fail in workflows separate from the test job and therefore read as unrelated breakage.\\n\\nWhat not to put in: anything already obvious from the README, and anything that will drift - version numbers age badly in a file nobody re-reads.\",\n      \"output\": \"Yes, it is a real gap - and the evidence is that these conventions had to be reconstructed from the `Makefile`, **13** workflow files and **142** PR titles. An agent that skips that reconstruction will guess, and the wrong guesses are predictable.\\n\\nFour things earn their place, because each is load-bearing *and* invisible at a glance:\\n\\n1. **Which checkout is authoritative.** Two clones exist: `E:\\\\Github\\\\repo2graph` at **5.0.0** and `C:\\\\Users\\\\Srini\\\\code\\\\repo2graph` at **3.0.0**, about six weeks apart. An agent in the stale one will confidently report `requires-python >=3.10` when the answer is **>=3.11**. This single line prevents a whole class of wrong answers.\\n2. **The Makefile is the entry point**, cheapest-first: `format-check`, `lint`, `typecheck`, `test`. Plus the existence of `test-serial` and that it is for flaky parallel runs, not the default.\\n3. **`tests/golden/` is a contract.** Across **102** test files, golden diffs are the review artifact - never bulk-regenerate them to get green.\\n4. **Three obligations that fail outside the test job**: `uv.lock` must move with `pyproject.toml`, new files need REUSE licence headers, and a new language needs a gallery example. All three fail in their own workflow, so they read as unrelated breakage.\\n\\n**What to leave out:** anything the README already says, and anything that drifts. Do not pin version numbers or alert counts in it - `31` alerts and `5.0.0` will both be stale within weeks, and a stale instruction file is worse than none because it is trusted. Point at the Makefile and the workflows as the source of truth instead of restating what they currently contain.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"supply_chain\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Our portfolio repo just migrated to React 19, Tailwind 4 and ESLint 10 in one PR. Assess that as a practice.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"This happened: PR #67 on srinivasan-78.github.io is 'Scroll performance, progressive disclosure, dependency majors, and a CI fix', and PR #68 is 'Restore the colours and surfaces the Tailwind 4 migration took away'. So the consequence is recorded in the history - the migration silently dropped styling, and a follow-up PR was needed to restore it.\\n\\nThat is the argument, and it is empirical rather than theoretical: bundling three major upgrades with feature work made the regression hard to attribute. With three majors in one diff, a visual regression could have come from any of them.\\n\\nAlso relevant: PRs #65 and #66 on that repo are closed rather than merged, and #72 is closed with #71 merged under a nearly identical title - which suggests iterations were reworked. Thirteen PRs total with 3 closed is a high churn ratio for a small repo.\\n\\nThe recommendation is one major per PR, with the feature work separate, because the cost is only visible when something breaks and you need to bisect.\\n\\nFor a portfolio site specifically, visual regressions are the main risk and they are not caught by tests, so a screenshot diff is the control that would have caught Tailwind 4's change.\",\n      \"output\": \"The repo already paid for the answer. PR #67 bundled *\\\"Scroll performance, progressive disclosure, dependency majors, and a CI fix\\\"* - and PR #68 is *\\\"Restore the colours and surfaces the Tailwind 4 migration took away\\\"*. The migration silently dropped styling, and a second PR existed to put it back.\\n\\nThat is the case against bundling, and it is empirical rather than theoretical: with **3** majors plus feature work in one diff, a visual regression could have originated anywhere, so attribution required a follow-up PR instead of a bisect.\\n\\nThe churn supports it too - of **13** PRs on that repo, **3** are closed unmerged, and #72 was closed while #71 merged under a nearly identical title. That is a lot of rework for a small codebase.\\n\\n**Practice to adopt:**\\n\\n| | |\\n|---|---|\\n| one major per PR | React 19, Tailwind 4, ESLint 10 = **3** PRs |\\n| feature work separate | never alongside a major |\\n| CI fixes separate | they are what you need green to judge the rest |\\n\\n**The control that would actually have caught this:** a screenshot diff. Tailwind 4 changing default colours and surfaces is a visual regression, and visual regressions pass every unit test - which is why it reached merge and needed #68 to undo. For a portfolio site, where appearance *is* the product, a rendered-output check is worth more than another linter.\\n\\nThe counter-argument for bundling is real but narrow: **3** separate upgrades mean 3 rounds of CI and 3 lockfile regenerations. That cost is worth paying the first time an upgrade breaks something you cannot attribute.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"python_stack\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Write the test-side convention for a repo2graph test that reads a fixture file and compares against a golden, so it passes on both Linux and the Windows runner.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Two Windows-specific hazards and one golden-file hazard, all of which this repo has hit.\\n\\nEncoding: open() without an explicit encoding uses the locale default, cp1252 on Windows. A fixture containing an em dash or a non-ASCII identifier then raises UnicodeDecodeError on the Windows runner only. Every read needs encoding='utf-8'.\\n\\nLine endings: a golden file checked out with CRLF compares unequal to LF-generated output. Comparing text read with newline normalisation, or splitting into lines before comparing, removes the whole class.\\n\\nPaths: comparing a rendered path string embeds the separator, so an assertion containing 'src/app/main.py' fails on Windows where the tool produced a backslash. Compare Path objects, or normalise with as_posix().\\n\\nThe repo uses pathlib and 102 test files with conftest.py and fixtures/, so tmp_path and fixtures are the idiomatic tools rather than manual temp directories - and tmp_path also sidesteps the Windows file-locking problem where an open file cannot be deleted.\",\n      \"output\": \"```python\\ndef test_answer_matches_golden(tmp_path: Path) -> None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Compare rendered output against the golden, portably.\\n\\n    Three Windows-only failure modes this guards against, all of which this\\n    repo has hit: cp1252 decoding, CRLF golden files, and path separators\\n    baked into an assertion.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    fixture = FIXTURES / \\\"small_repo\\\"\\n    expected = (GOLDEN / \\\"small_repo.txt\\\").read_text(encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\")\\n\\n    actual = render_answer(fixture)\\n\\n    # Compare line lists, not raw text: a golden checked out with CRLF is\\n    # byte-unequal to LF output while being identical in content.\\n    assert actual.splitlines() == expected.splitlines()\\n```\\n\\nThe three rules, and what each prevents:\\n\\n1. **`encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\"` on every read.** `open()` without it uses the locale default - **cp1252** on Windows - so a fixture containing an em dash or `\u2248` raises `UnicodeDecodeError` on that runner and nowhere else. This is the single most common cause of a Windows-only failure in a Python repo.\\n2. **Compare `splitlines()`, not raw text.** A golden file checked out with CRLF differs byte-for-byte from LF-generated output. Across **102** test files and a `golden/` directory, this is a whole class of false failures removed by one habit.\\n3. **Never assert on a rendered path string.** `\\\"src/app/main.py\\\"` in an assertion fails where the tool emitted `\\\\`. Compare `Path` objects, or normalise with `.as_posix()` on both sides.\\n\\nUse the `tmp_path` fixture rather than building temp directories by hand. Besides being idiomatic for the **102**-file suite with its `conftest.py`, pytest handles teardown - which matters on Windows, where deleting a still-open file fails and turns cleanup into a test error that has nothing to do with the assertion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"python_stack\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Show the shape of a small, reviewable change to a flat Python package like repo2graph's, where modules import each other freely. What do you avoid?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The structural fact: repo2graph/ is a flat package with modules like answer.py, cache.py, chunks.py, cli.py, config.py, discovery.py, derived.py, embed.py. Flat means any module can import any other, so there is no layering to stop a change rippling.\\n\\nThe practical consequence for a reviewable change: edit one module's internals, keep its public names and signatures stable, and the diff stays local. The moment a signature changes, every caller is in the diff and the review becomes about the blast radius rather than the fix.\\n\\nSo the technique is to add rather than alter: a new keyword-only parameter with a default preserves every existing call site. That turns an N-file diff into a 1-file diff.\\n\\nWhat to avoid: moving code between modules in the same PR as a behaviour change, because the diff then cannot distinguish a move from an edit. The history shows a module split was done deliberately as its own work item in PR #480, which is the right treatment.\\n\\nAlso avoid widening an import graph that is already unlayered - importing cli.py from a library module inverts the dependency and makes the CLI non-optional.\\n\\nAnd 102 test files means the suite is the safety net, so run it rather than reasoning about callers.\",\n      \"output\": \"`repo2graph/` is flat - `answer.py`, `cache.py`, `chunks.py`, `cli.py`, `config.py`, `derived.py`, `discovery.py`, `embed.py` and the rest can all import each other. There is no layering to stop a change rippling, so **keeping the diff local is a deliberate technique**, not a side effect.\\n\\n**Add, do not alter:**\\n\\n```python\\ndef build_chunks(\\n    path: Path,\\n    *,\\n    max_tokens: int = 2048,\\n    include_docstrings: bool = True,   # new, defaulted - no call site changes\\n) -> Iterator[Chunk]:\\n```\\n\\nA keyword-only parameter with a default preserves every existing caller, so a **1**-file diff stays a 1-file diff. Change the signature instead and all callers join the diff, and the review becomes about blast radius rather than the fix.\\n\\n**Three things to avoid:**\\n\\n1. **Moving code between modules in the same PR as a behaviour change.** The diff then cannot distinguish a move from an edit. This repo treats restructuring as its own work item - PR #480 did a module split deliberately, alongside **9** other fixes but as a named change.\\n2. **Importing `cli.py` from a library module.** That inverts the dependency and makes the CLI non-optional for library users. In a flat package nothing prevents it, which is exactly why it needs a rule.\\n3. **Reasoning about callers instead of running the suite.** With **102** test files, `make test` answers \\\"what did I break\\\" faster and more reliably than reading imports - and `make typecheck` catches the signature mismatches before the suite even starts.\\n\\nRun them cheapest-first: `make lint` in seconds, `make typecheck` in tens of seconds, `make test` in minutes. A flat package punishes wide changes, so the discipline is to make the narrow one even when the wide one looks tidier.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"python_stack\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Write a conventional-commit PR title and body skeleton for a change that fixes an export bug and closes two issues, matching how this repo does it.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The convention is visible in the history. Titles are conventional-commit with a scope and read as sentences describing the actual change, not labels: 'fix(export): restrict the whole staged tree, not the manifest's list', 'fix(ci): stop the Windows runner's load failing the build', 'fix(examples): match the gallery leak check at a path boundary (#462)'.\\n\\nSo: type(scope): what changed, phrased as a statement. Issue numbers appear in the title when the PR exists to close them.\\n\\nfeat! is reserved for breaking changes, as in PR #527.\\n\\nThe body should state what the bug was, why the fix is right, and what verifies it - and name the verification concretely, because this repo's CI has 13 workflows and the relevant ones differ per change.\\n\\nGiven the operator's stated preference, no co-author or generated-by trailers.\",\n      \"output\": \"```\\nfix(export): restrict the whole staged tree, not just the manifest's list\\n```\\n\\nThat is the shape: `type(scope):` then **what changed, as a statement**. Compare the real titles in this repo - `fix(ci): stop the Windows runner's load failing the build`, `fix(examples): match the gallery leak check at a path boundary (#462)`. They describe the change, not the category of work. Reserve `feat!:` for breaking changes, as PR #527 did when it dropped Python **3.10**.\\n\\n```markdown\\nCloses #612, closes #615.\\n\\n## What was wrong\\nThe export restricted paths using the manifest's file list, so a file staged\\nbut absent from the manifest was written unrestricted.\\n\\n## Why this fix\\nRestrict the staged tree itself - the manifest is derived from it, so it\\ncannot be the authority for what the tree contains.\\n\\n## Verification\\n- `make lint`, `make typecheck`, `make test` (102 test files) all green\\n- 2 golden files updated; diffs reviewed, not regenerated\\n- `examples.yml` passes against the gallery repos\\n```\\n\\nFour details that match local practice: both issue numbers are named explicitly, since issues are referenced by number here; the \\\"why\\\" says what the authority *should* be rather than restating the diff; the golden-file count is stated with a note that the diffs were read; and the verification names the specific workflows, because **13** exist and only some are relevant per change.\\n\\n**No `Co-Authored-By` or \\\"generated with\\\" trailer** - commit attribution is deliberately cleared in this setup, so adding one contradicts a configured preference.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"I have 462 PRs across 21 repos and a private DevOps knowledge base. Plan how a local 4B model could actually help, given what a 4B can and cannot do.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Be honest about the capability envelope. The measured facts from this very pipeline: three lessons of LoRA at 91 training rows improved formatting 0.479 to 0.754 and conciseness 0.343 to 0.908, but reduced numbers stated 0.596 to 0.425 and identifiers 0.308 to 0.185. So the model learns response shape, not knowledge.\\n\\nThat partitions the work cleanly. A 4B is good at shaped, bounded, repetitive transformations where the facts are in the prompt: drafting a conventional-commit title from a diff, extracting a schema from a log, formatting a tool call, triaging which workflow a CI failure belongs to.\\n\\nIt is bad at anything needing recall of 462 PRs or the contents of 102 test files, because that knowledge is not in 3 GB of 4-bit weights and cannot be put there by 91 examples.\\n\\nSo the architecture is retrieval plus a shaped model - which is precisely what repo2graph does, and the operator already built it. The local model consumes cited context rather than remembering.\\n\\nConcrete division: facts via profile system prompt and repo2graph retrieval; shape via the trained adapter; anything requiring judgement across repos goes to a larger model.\",\n      \"output\": \"Be concrete about the envelope first, using this pipeline's own measurements. Three lessons at **91** training rows moved formatting **0.479 -> 0.754** and conciseness **0.343 -> 0.908**, while reducing numbers stated **0.596 -> 0.425** and identifiers **0.308 -> 0.185**. A 4B learns *response shape*; it does not learn *facts*.\\n\\n**So give it shaped, bounded jobs where the facts arrive in the prompt:**\\n\\n| task | why it fits |\\n|---|---|\\n| draft a conventional-commit title from a diff | pure shape; the diff is the input |\\n| classify which of the **13** workflows a CI failure belongs to | small fixed label set |\\n| extract a schema from a log or config | structured_extraction already scores **0.825+** |\\n| emit a tool/MCP call | tool_call_format improved to **0.673** |\\n| first-pass triage of Dependabot alerts into \\\"clears under >=3.11\\\" vs \\\"forces the floor up\\\" | binary split, facts supplied |\\n\\n**What to never ask it:** anything requiring recall across **462** PRs, **21** repos, or **102** test files. That knowledge is not in **3 GB** of 4-bit weights, and **91** examples cannot put it there.\\n\\n**The architecture that works is the one already built:** retrieval supplies cited facts, the model supplies shape. That is literally repo2graph's thesis - *trustworthy, cited answers, no LLM required* - so point the 4B at it rather than trying to memorise the codebase into the adapter. The operator profile handles the small, stable facts; repo2graph handles the large, changing ones.\\n\\n**Escalate rather than attempt:** cross-repo judgement, security reasoning about a new dependency, and anything where being confidently wrong is expensive. Those go to the **9B** daily driver or to a cloud model - the private knowledge base in particular is worth keeping on a local model specifically *because* it is private, but only for retrieval-shaped questions.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n"
