slug: "pennywright",
title: "Pennywright",
client: "Platform engineering",
category: "Developer tooling",
status: "Active",
teaser:
  "One front door for a coding agent: it reads what you asked for, works out what kind of work it is, and hands it to the right tool on the belt.",
tags: ["Claude Code", "Subagents", "Token cost"],
stack: ["Claude Code skills", "Codex agents", "Node.js", "Python", "Bash", "Markdown", "TOML"],
overview:
  "A utility belt for coding agents, grown out of the seven-phase build loop that started it. `/penny` is the only command you need day to day: it classifies a request from the prompt alone — no file reads, so routing is effectively free — and sends it down one of seven lanes, to a build loop, a five-member council, a bounded scout, or just does it when the request is smaller than the ceremony. Underneath sits one principle: an expensive brain plans, cheap hands do the work, and the verbose middle never reaches your context. Work is handed to a subagent whose context is discarded when it finishes, so a phase that burned 40k tokens exploring a codebase costs the main session about fifty. The same repo drives Claude Code and Codex.",
architecture: [
  {
    label: "Hard layer and soft layer",
    body: "The design knows which of its rules are guarantees. `model: haiku` is Haiku, a `tools` allowlist means no write tool exists to call, and `maxTurns` kills a run at its budget — the harness enforces those. Word caps and \"review the diff, not the summary\" are prose the model follows because the reasoning is given. The expensive guarantees are deliberately put in the hard layer.",
  },
  {
    label: "The front door, seven lanes",
    body: "REFINE sharpens an idea then re-routes, BUILD runs the loop, DECIDE convenes the council, FIND sends a scout, TUNE adjusts the agent rather than the app, DO skips the ceremony, EXPLAIN just answers. The lane is announced in one line so it can be redirected in one word.",
  },
  {
    label: "The test that does the routing",
    body: "Only REFINE versus BUILD is genuinely hard, and it has a sharp test: can three checkable acceptance criteria be written from the request alone, without inventing requirements? A thin request is not a vague one — \"add CSV export to the reports page\" is BUILD, because convention recovers the criteria and the loop's own gates catch a wrong guess. The router is biased against interrogating someone who was already clear.",
  },
  {
    label: "Refinement that sorts before it asks",
    body: "Gaps are split three ways first: a fact about the repo is scouted rather than asked, anything with an obvious default is assumed and labelled so it can be vetoed at a glance, and only a decision nobody else can make reaches you. Then one round, at most four questions, each with concrete options and a recommendation. The brief must come out sharper and shorter than the conversation that produced it — and must carry your exact words for error codes, numbers and anything you ruled out.",
  },
  {
    label: "The build loop, gated",
    body: "PLAN, TEST, IMPLEMENT, REVIEW, VERIFY, REMEMBER, IMPROVE — one subagent each, a gate between every pair, the orchestrator writing no feature code. Phases never talk; everything crosses through a single BUILD_STATE.md, with the baseline sha recorded up front because it is the only thing REVIEW and VERIFY can diff against later. Past ~1500 lines older iteration blocks collapse to their summaries, since by iteration three reading the file whole costs more than the phase did.",
  },
  {
    label: "Two rules the agents cannot talk themselves out of",
    body: "IMPLEMENT may never edit a test to make it pass — it hands back to TEST and explains which test and why, because editing a test to go green destroys the only evidence the loop has. REVIEW and VERIFY may never touch code at all, not even an obvious one-line fix: fixing it themselves hides the failure from the loop log and from the retro.",
  },
  {
    label: "The council, and its referee",
    body: "Five mandates rather than personalities — Architect on what the choice forecloses, Skeptic on how it breaks, Pragmatist on the shortest honest path, Operator on the day after, Economist on the maintenance tail nobody counts. Each writes its own file in parallel, capped at 250 words, and must state the strongest argument against its own position. One rebuttal round crosses only a ~40-line digest. A separate referee then separates verified fact from confident assertion and reduces each disagreement to a measurement.",
  },
  {
    label: "The scout",
    body: "One bounded question answered in a throwaway context, returning the answer rather than the files. Haiku, read-only, with no write tool at all, climbing a cheapest-first ladder — search, then signatures, then a range read, then the whole file only if it is small — and stopping as soon as it can answer. \"Not found\" is a valid answer, and it is told to report absence rather than widen the search until it finds something.",
  },
  {
    label: "Four tiers, and no subagent on Opus",
    body: "The orchestrator is the one place Opus earns its price; judgment work runs on Sonnet at high effort, craft at medium, and the hands on Haiku. If a subagent seems to need Opus the brief was too vague — tighten the brief, not the tier. Effort is left unset on Haiku agents deliberately, because the alias has pointed at models that reject the parameter outright and a rejected spawn costs more than the effort setting saves.",
  },
  {
    label: "Savings in order of effect",
    body: "Isolation first and by far the largest, then the PreToolUse hook that rewrites a noisy command before it runs so a 10,000-line log never becomes context, then bounded starts, then retrieval discipline — and only then model routing, because a Haiku agent carrying 150k tokens costs more than a Sonnet agent carrying 8k.",
  },
  {
    label: "Measured, not assumed",
    body: "A context report totals the always-on load and a bench script reads the usage already written to the session logs, deduplicated by request id because Claude Code writes several lines per reply. Comparisons are per-turn rather than total, since a harder task honestly costs more. Cache read share below ~80% means something is mutating the prompt prefix, and model routing is irrelevant until that is fixed.",
  },
  {
    label: "Diagnosis that reports rather than fixes",
    body: "`/penny-doctor` runs its checks in severity order, and the first section is the one that matters: the failures that make the kit look installed while doing nothing — an effort override that flattens four tiers into one, a hook on disk that settings.json never references, an unset `$AGENT_KIT` that breaks every script path, a half-pinned agent inheriting an expensive value. It ends with one highest-value fix, not a list.",
  },
],
highlights: [
  "Routing reads the prompt alone — no file reads, no tool calls — so picking the lane is effectively free",
  "A subagent's context is thrown away when it finishes, so a 40k-token exploration returns as a three-line report",
  "The expensive guarantees are enforced by the harness, not requested in prose: model, tool allowlist, turn cap, spawn depth",
  "IMPLEMENT cannot edit a test to go green, and REVIEW and VERIFY cannot touch code at all",
  "Every council member must state the strongest case against its own position, and exactly one rebuttal round is allowed",
  "Filtered commands keep the real exit status, so a suppressed failure can never read as a pass",
  "No subagent runs on Opus — two failed Sonnet attempts still cost less than one Opus subagent on a cold context",
  "Anything expensive to get wrong — schema migrations, auth, money movement, data deletion — is explicitly not delegated to a cheap tier",
  "Honest about its own cost: 18 skills advertise roughly 1.8k tokens every session, and a council spends more than deciding alone",
  "One repo drives both Claude Code and Codex: 14 agents mirrored as Codex TOML, with both model and effort pinned so neither half inherits an expensive default",
