    "old_string": "## Delegation",
    "new_string": "## Routing \u2014 do this first\n\nBefore starting work, decide what kind of work it is. Say the lane in one line,\nthen act. Do not write a paragraph justifying it.\n\n| Lane | What it looks like | Do |\n|------|--------------------|-----|\n| REFINE | An idea, not a request. No acceptance criteria are recoverable | Sharpen it first \u2014 see below |\n| BUILD | A clear change: a feature, a bug with a repro, a refactor with a goal | Run the build loop |\n| DECIDE | A choice with real trade-offs, two or more defensible options | Convene the council |\n| FIND | A question of fact about the code | Delegate to `scout_agent` |\n| DO | Small, clear, mechanical \u2014 a rename, a one-line fix, a config change | Just do it, no ceremony |\n| EXPLAIN | \"How does X work\", \"why is this like this\" | Answer directly |\n\n**The one hard call** is REFINE versus BUILD: can you write three checkable\nacceptance criteria from the request alone, without inventing requirements? Yes\nis BUILD, no is REFINE. \"Checkable\" means a machine could decide it \u2014 \"returns\n422 on an empty cart\", not \"handles bad input\". A thin but recoverable request is\nBUILD; do not interview it.\n\nTwo failure modes to avoid, equally bad: interviewing someone who was already\nclear, and building confidently on a brief nobody sharpened.\n\n### Sharpening a vague request\n\nSort the gaps before asking anything:\n\n- A fact about the code or environment \u2192 **scout it, do not ask.** More than\n  half of apparent ambiguity is answered by the repository, and asking the user\n  something the code knows is the most annoying question available.\n- Something with an obvious default \u2192 **assume it**, and label the assumption.\n- A decision only the user can make \u2192 **ask.** This pile only, and it is usually\n  two or three things.\n\nAsk at most four questions, in one round, each with concrete options and a\nrecommended default. The questions that almost always pay: who uses this\n(changes auth, hosting, rigour); what the smallest useful version is (finds the\nfirst slice); whether it is throwaway or maintained (decides if tests earn their\ncost); and any hard constraint it must work inside.\n\nDo not ask what the stack should be, whether it should have tests, or what to\ncall it. Assume, and say you assumed.\n\nThen write a brief: goal, non-goals, 3-6 numbered checkable criteria, every\nassumption prefixed `assumed:`, and open questions that did not block. Keep the\nuser's exact words for anything they were specific about \u2014 numbers, error codes,\nnegations, and things they explicitly rejected. A brief that is tidier than the\nrequest but says less has lost information, which is worse than not refining.\n\nIf the user says \"you decide\", decide. Write the assumption down and proceed.\n\n## Delegation"
  }
}
