```json
{
  "name": "Write",
  "input": {
    "file_path": "E:\\Github\\agentic-app-loop\\README.md",
    "content": "# Pennywright\n\n**The utility belt for coding agents.**\n\nOne front door. It reads what you asked for, sharpens it if it is too vague to\nact on, and hands it to the right tool on the belt \u2014 a disciplined build loop, a\ncouncil of five that argues a decision out, or a cheap scout that just answers\nthe question. An expensive brain plans; cheap hands do the work; the verbose\nmiddle never reaches your context.\n\nWorks on **Claude Code** and **Codex**, from the same repo.\n\n```\n/penny  add rate limiting to the orders endpoint\n```\n\n```\nLane: BUILD \u2014 criteria recoverable. Running the loop.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Why\n\nTwo things make agentic coding expensive, and they are the same thing twice.\n\n**Everything lands in one context.** Test output, speculative file reads, dead\nends, a 10,000-line build log. You pay for all of it on every subsequent turn,\nand the model gets worse as the window fills with noise.\n\n**Everything runs on one setting.** The model that should be weighing an\narchitecture decision is also the one grepping for a function name, at the same\nreasoning effort, at the same price.\n\nPennywright fixes both structurally rather than by nagging you to be careful. Work\nis routed to a bounded subagent whose context is thrown away when it finishes,\nand each subagent runs at the cheapest tier that can do its job. What returns to\nyour session is a three-line report.\n\n---\n\n## The belt\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---------|--------------|\n| **`/penny <anything>`** | **Start here.** Classifies the request, sharpens it if vague, routes it. |\n| `/penny-build <ask>` | 7-phase loop: PLAN \u2192 TEST \u2192 IMPLEMENT \u2192 REVIEW \u2192 VERIFY \u2192 REMEMBER \u2192 IMPROVE, one subagent per phase, a gate between each |\n| `/penny-council <question>` | Five mandates argue in parallel, rebut once, a referee adjudicates, you decide |\n| `/penny-scout <question>` | One bounded question, answered in a throwaway context |\n| `/penny-setup` | One-stop install: tiers, output filter, context diet \u2014 Claude and Codex |\n| `/penny-doctor` | Verify it all, read-only |\n| `/penny-help` | The index |\n\nPlus 15 model-invoked `miser-*` skills \u2014 the\n[tokenmiser](https://github.com/Srinivasan-78/tokenmiser) toolkit, bundled \u2014 that\nthe agent reaches for on its own: context auditing, retrieval discipline,\ndelegation contracts, model routing, hook filtering, and a measurement harness.\n\n### The front door decides between seven lanes\n\n```\nREFINE   an idea, not a request       \u2192 sharpen it, then re-route\nBUILD    a clear change to make       \u2192 the loop\nDECIDE   a real trade-off             \u2192 the council\nFIND     a question of fact           \u2192 a scout\nTUNE     about the agent, not the app \u2192 the kit\nDO       smaller than the ceremony    \u2192 just do it\nEXPLAIN  a question about what exists \u2192 answer it\n```\n\nThe one hard call is REFINE vs BUILD, and it has a sharp test: **can you write\nthree checkable acceptance criteria from the request alone, without inventing\nrequirements?** Yes is BUILD. No is REFINE.\n\nA thin request is not a vague one. \"Add CSV export\" is BUILD \u2014 the criteria are\nrecoverable. Interviewing someone who was already clear is the fastest way to\nmake a tool tiring, so the router is deliberately biased against asking.\n\nWhen it does ask, it sorts the gaps first: facts about the code get **scouted**,\nanything with an obvious default gets **assumed and labelled**, and only genuine\nuser decisions get **asked** \u2014 at most four questions, one round, each with\nconcrete options and a recommended default.\n\n---\n\n## Model policy\n\n> One expensive brain, many cheap hands.\n\n### Claude Code\n\n| Tier | Who | Model | Effort |\n|------|-----|-------|--------|\n| Orchestrator | your session | `opus` | `xhigh` |\n| Judgment | plan, review, architect, skeptic, referee | `sonnet` | `high` |\n| Craft | test, implement, verify, improve, pragmatist | `sonnet` | `medium` |\n| Hands | remember, scout, operator, economist | `haiku` | \u2014 |\n\n### Codex\n\n| Tier | Model | Effort |\n|------|-------|--------|\n| Orchestrator | `gpt-6.2-sol` | `xhigh` |\n| Judgment | `gpt-6.2-sol` | `high` |\n| Craft | `gpt-6.2-sol` | `medium` |\n| Hands | `gpt-6-luna` | `high` |\n\n**No subagent in this kit runs on Opus.** If one seems to need it, the brief was\ntoo vague \u2014 tighten the brief, not the tier. Escalation is evidence-based and the\ntest suite enforces the policy.\n\nThree things are load-bearing and easy to miss:\n\n- `effort` is deliberately **unset** on Haiku agents. Effort levels are\n  model-dependent and the alias has pointed at models that reject the parameter;\n  omitting it lets the agent launch either way.\n- `gpt-6-luna` at `high` *is* the cheap tier. Effort is not comparable across\n  models \u2014 read the model+effort pair, never the word alone.\n- Codex resolves model and effort **independently**, so an agent file that sets\n  only `model` inherits the parent's `xhigh` and stops being cheap. Every file\n  here sets both; `/penny-doctor` fails the build if one does not.\n\nFull reasoning: [`skills/penny-kit/references/model-policy.md`](skills/penny-kit/references/model-policy.md).\n\n---\n\n## Install\n\n### Claude Code \u2014 as a plugin (recommended)\n\n```\n/plugin marketplace add Srinivasan-78/agentic-app-loop\n/plugin install pennywright\n```\n\nThen, in order:\n\n```\n/penny-setup     # tiers, output filter hook, context diet, MCP prune\n/penny-doctor    # confirm it took\n```\n\n### Either runtime \u2014 via the CLI\n\n```bash\nnpx github:Srinivasan-78/agentic-app-loop install          # both runtimes it finds\nnpx github:Srinivasan-78/agentic-app-loop install --hook    # plus the output filter\n```\n\n```\npenny install --claude | --codex    one runtime only\npenny install --project             repo scope (./.claude, ./.codex)\npenny install --dry-run             print the plan, write nothing\npenny status | doctor | uninstall\npenny audit | report --since 7d     the measurement scripts\n```\n\nThen export the kit root so the skills can find the bundled scripts \u2014 the\ninstaller prints the exact line:\n\n```bash\nexport AGENT_KIT=\"$HOME/.claude/pennywright\"\n```\n\n### Codex\n\n`install --codex` writes the 14 agent definitions to `~/.codex/agents/` and the\nportable skills to `~/.agents/skills/`.\n\nIt deliberately does **not** touch `config.toml` or `AGENTS.md` \u2014 merging those\nwrongly breaks a working setup. It prints the two sample paths to merge by hand,\nor `/penny-setup codex` from Claude Code will do the merge for you, showing the\ndiff first.\n\n---\n\n## What the loop actually does\n\n`BUILD_STATE.md` at the repo root is the only channel between phases. Each\nsubagent reads it for input, writes only its own section, and reports three lines\nback. Every long diff and full test log lives in that file, not in your session.\n\n| Phase | Gate to pass |\n|-------|--------------|\n| PLAN | file-level tasks + numbered acceptance criteria + a test strategy |\n| TEST | tests exist, run, and fail **for the stated reason** |\n| IMPLEMENT | all phase tests pass |\n| REVIEW | zero unresolved blocking findings, each with file:line and a failing scenario |\n| VERIFY | full suite + lint + typecheck + build + smoke green; every criterion met with evidence |\n| REMEMBER | the non-obvious decisions written where this repo already keeps knowledge |\n| IMPROVE | retro + ranked backlog; only low-risk quick wins applied |\n\nLoop-backs are bounded: blocking review findings or a failing verify return to\nIMPLEMENT; a wrong *requirement* returns to PLAN. Four iterations without a green\nverify stops and asks. Every loop-back is logged with a reason, so IMPROVE can\nsee where the loop thrashed.\n\nTwo rules the agents cannot talk themselves out of: IMPLEMENT may never edit a\ntest to make it pass, and REVIEW and VERIFY may never touch code \u2014 their write\naccess is the state file only.\n\n## What the council actually does\n\nFive mandates, not five personalities \u2014 Architect, Skeptic, Pragmatist, Operator,\nEconomist. Each argues its brief honestly and is told explicitly not to be\nagreeable.\n\nThey run **in parallel**, each writing its **own** file (five agents writing one\nshared file corrupts it). One rebuttal round from a capped digest \u2014 never two.\nThen a referee reads all five and reports what they agree on, what is genuinely\nin conflict, and **which claims nobody verified**. The referee advises; the\norchestrator decides.\n\nHonest cost: roughly eleven agent runs. Worth it for a decision you will live\nwith for a year; absurd for one you will revisit next week. The skill says so and\nrefuses settled questions.\n\n---\n\n## Token discipline\n\nRouting is the saving. Everything below is the rest of it.\n\n| Lever | Where |\n|-------|-------|\n| Output filtered before it is context | `PreToolUse` hook \u2014 a 10k-line log becomes ~100 lines, and what you never see is never billed |\n| Bounded subagents | `omitClaudeMd`, `maxTurns`, and a `tools` allowlist on every agent |\n| No nested fan-out | spawn depth pinned to 1 |\n| Retrieval discipline | search \u2192 skeleton \u2192 range read \u2192 whole file, caps on every command |\n| Measurement | `penny report --since 7d`, baselines, A/B protocol |\n\nMeasure, do not assume:\n\n```bash\npenny audit                       # always-on context load\npenny report --since 7d           # per-turn cost, cache share, thinking share\npenny bench baseline save pre     # ... change something ...\npenny bench baseline diff pre post\n```\n\nCompare **per-turn** numbers, never totals \u2014 a bigger task legitimately costs\nmore.\n\n### Honest caveats\n\n- **The kit is not free.** 19 skills advertise their descriptions every session,\n  roughly 1.9k tokens standing cost. On a small repo with quiet tests it may not\n  pay for itself. `penny report` is how you find out rather than assume.\n- **A council spends more tokens than deciding yourself.** What it buys is a\n  defensible decision and a clean main thread, not a smaller bill.\n- **`hide_agent_reasoning` on Codex is cosmetic.** Reasoning still happens and is\n  still billed. It buys a readable transcript.\n- **`MAX_THINKING_TOKENS` does nothing on current models.** Adaptive-reasoning\n  models ignore the number, so it reads as a cap while capping nothing. Effort is\n  the real control, and `/penny-doctor` flags it if an old config still sets it.\n\n---\n\n## Repo layout\n\n```\ncommands/          7 slash commands\nagents/            14 Claude subagents, model + effort + turn-capped\nskills/\n  penny/           the front door: classify, sharpen, route\n  penny-build/     the 7-phase loop + phase contracts + state schema\n  penny-council/   the five mandates + protocol\n  penny-kit/       setup, doctor, and the model policy\n  miser-*/         15 token skills (tokenmiser, bundled)\ncodex/\n  agents/          14 mirrored agent definitions (.toml)\n  config.toml.sample\n  AGENTS.md.sample\nscripts/           context report + the bench harness\nhooks/             the tool-output filter\nreference/         the technique catalog\ntest/              structural tests \u2014 run `npm test`\n```\n\n`npm test` enforces the design: no subagent on Opus, every agent pinned and\nbounded, Codex agents never half-pinned, no dangling contract paths, and the\ndocumented counts matching reality.\n\n---\n\n## Security\n\nThe output filter hook executes shell commands with your permissions. **Read it\nbefore installing it** \u2014 `hooks/filter-tool-output.py`. It only ever filters\n*output*; a rewrite that changed which command runs would be a bug.\n\nFiltered test and build commands end with `exit ${PIPESTATUS[0]}` and a marker\nline, so a suppressed failure can never read as a pass. Turn it off for one shell\nwith `TOKENMISER_FILTER_OFF=1`.\n\nOn Codex, note that a custom agent file **cannot** sandbox an agent \u2014\n`sandbox_mode` and `approval_policy` always come from the parent session. A\n\"read-only\" Codex agent is read-only by instruction, not by enforcement. Never\ntreat an agent file as a security boundary.\n\n---\n\n## License\n\nMIT. Source files carry an `@authormark` watermark header \u2014 see\n[`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md).\n\nThe `miser-*` skills, `scripts/`, `hooks/` and `reference/` are vendored from\n[tokenmiser](https://github.com/Srinivasan-78/tokenmiser) by the same author.\n"
  }
}
