## How I did it

I wrote a purpose-built audit in the scratchpad (keeping the repo skills-only) covering 18 check families: frontmatter validity against the real Claude Code and Codex field schemas, model-policy coherence, dangling references, broken links, README TOC anchors, stale identifiers, env-var consistency, and index completeness.

Then I **validated the audit itself** by injecting four defects into a scratch copy — an Opus subagent, a Codex half-pin, a tier drift, a dangling reference path. It caught all four. So "NO ISSUES" on the real repo means something.

It took 4 passes. Passes 2 and 3 were fixing **my own audit** — the tier parser was conflating the 3-column Codex table with the 5-column Claude one, and the TODO check flagged legitimate example content like `rg -l "TODO"`.

## The significant find

**`miser-setup` was contradicting the rest of the product.** It duplicated `penny-kit`'s setup step for step, knew nothing about the subagent tier env vars that are the core of the model policy, and still recommended `MAX_THINKING_TOKENS: 8000` — the exact setting `/penny-doctor` flags as a warning.

So the kit would have installed something its own doctor complains about. That's the class of bug worth hunting for: not broken, just quietly incoherent. Removed it; repointed every reference including `reference/techniques.md`; recorded the reason in `AGENTS.md` so it doesn't get re-vendored back from upstream tokenmiser.

## Everything else fixed

| Issue | Why it mattered |
|---|---|
| Hook docstring pointed at `npx tokenmiser install --hook` | Deleted with the installer. Now names the two real steps — copying without wiring `settings.json` is the most common silent failure. |
| `commands/penny-{doctor,setup}.md` used bare `references/...` | A command is injected **before** its skill loads, so that base path was unresolved. Now fully qualified. |
| `COUNCIL.md` had `Status`/`Round`/`## Log` that nothing updated | The chair now maintains them. Also makes an interrupted council resumable — and the skipped-step lines matter, since a decision record that doesn't say round 2 was skipped reads as though it happened. |
| `state-schema.md` never said whether to commit `BUILD_STATE.md` | A real user question with no answer. Now recommends committing it as the audit trail, names the two exceptions, requires deciding once at Step 0. |
| `/penny-help` missing from its own index | — |
| `.gitignore` | `BUILD_STATE.md`, `.penny/`, `.council/` would have been committed if the workflows ran in this repo. Also dropped comments referencing the removed watermark bot and deleted `package.json`. |

