```json
{
  "name": "Write",
  "input": {
    "file_path": "E:\\LocalLLM\\teacher_pipeline\\lessons\\lesson_02.json",
    "content": "{\n  \"lesson\": 2,\n  \"author\": \"claude-opus-5\",\n  \"domain\": \"Local-LLM ops and agentic debugging, harvested from the real session that made Claude Code run against Ollama\",\n  \"notes\": \"Every example here comes from a verified finding in the 2026-11-22 session that wired Claude Code to a local Qwen3.5 served by Ollama 0.40.16. Weighted toward localllm_ops because baseline.json scored it 0.17, the weakest skill. The recurring lesson in the agentic_planning examples is the one that cost that session the most time: a transport-layer 404 can surface as a model-identity error, so verify the request path before believing the error text.\",\n  \"examples\": [\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"localllm_ops\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"I set ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1 so Claude Code would talk to Ollama. Ollama is running and the model is pulled, but every message fails with \\\"There's an issue with the selected model (qwen3.5:9b). It may not exist or you may not have access to it.\\\" What is wrong?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The error names the model, so the instinct is to suspect the model. But the model is pulled and Ollama is up, so the model exists. What the client actually does is append a path to the base URL. Anthropic clients append /v1/messages. If the base URL already ends in /v1, the effective request path becomes /v1/v1/messages. That path does not exist on the server, so it returns 404. A 404 on the messages endpoint is indistinguishable, from the client's point of view, from a model the account cannot reach, so the client prints a model-access error. The fix is to drop the /v1 suffix from the base URL, and the way to confirm it is to POST both candidate paths and compare status codes.\",\n      \"output\": \"Your base URL has one path segment too many. Claude Code appends `/v1/messages` itself, so `http://localhost:11434/v1` becomes a request to `/v1/v1/messages`, which does not exist and returns 404. The client reports a 404 on the messages endpoint as a model-access failure, which is why it reads like a model problem.\\n\\nConfirm it directly:\\n\\n```bash\\nfor p in /v1/messages /v1/v1/messages; do\\n  printf \\\"%-18s -> HTTP \\\" \\\"$p\\\"\\n  curl -s -o /dev/null -w \\\"%{http_code}\\\\n\\\" \\\\\\n    \\\"http://localhost:11434$p\\\" \\\\\\n    -H \\\"content-type: application/json\\\" \\\\\\n    -d '{\\\"model\\\":\\\"qwen3.5:4b\\\",\\\"max_tokens\\\":5,\\\"messages\\\":[{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"hi\\\"}]}'\\ndone\\n```\\n\\nExpect `/v1/messages -> 200` and `/v1/v1/messages -> 404`.\\n\\nFix: set the base URL to the server root, with no version suffix.\\n\\n```bash\\nexport ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=\\\"http://localhost:11434\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe general rule: a base URL is a server root, not an endpoint. Any time a client reports that a model is missing while the server is demonstrably serving that model, compare the path the client builds against the path the server exposes before touching model configuration.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"ollama\", \"anthropic-api\", \"base-url\", \"404\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"localllm_ops\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Does Ollama 0.40.16 need a translation proxy such as LiteLLM to serve an Anthropic-format client, or can it answer /v1/messages natively? Show me how to check rather than asserting it.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Ollama historically exposed its own /api/chat plus an OpenAI-compatible /v1/chat/completions. Anthropic compatibility is newer, so the honest answer is that this is a version-dependent fact and must be measured on the machine in question rather than recalled. The decisive test is a POST to /v1/messages in Anthropic request shape, checking both the status code and the response envelope, because a proxy-less 200 with an Anthropic-shaped body is the whole claim. Tool calling needs its own check, since schema translation is the part most likely to be missing.\",\n      \"output\": \"Measure it, since this is version-dependent. Two checks: plain generation, then tool use.\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -s http://localhost:11434/v1/messages \\\\\\n  -H \\\"content-type: application/json\\\" \\\\\\n  -d '{\\\"model\\\":\\\"qwen3.5:9b\\\",\\\"max_tokens\\\":500,\\n       \\\"messages\\\":[{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"Reply with exactly: HELLO\\\"}]}'\\n```\\n\\nA native implementation returns an Anthropic envelope: `type: \\\"message\\\"`, a `content` array of typed blocks, `stop_reason`, and `usage` with `input_tokens` and `output_tokens`.\\n\\nThen prove schema translation works, which is the part a thin shim usually lacks:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -s http://localhost:11434/v1/messages \\\\\\n  -H \\\"content-type: application/json\\\" \\\\\\n  -d '{\\\"model\\\":\\\"qwen3.5:9b\\\",\\\"max_tokens\\\":600,\\n       \\\"tools\\\":[{\\\"name\\\":\\\"get_time\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Get current time\\\",\\n                 \\\"input_schema\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"object\\\",\\n                                 \\\"properties\\\":{\\\"tz\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\"}},\\n                                 \\\"required\\\":[\\\"tz\\\"]}}],\\n       \\\"messages\\\":[{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"What time is it in Tokyo? Use the tool.\\\"}]}'\\n```\\n\\nSuccess looks like `stop_reason: \\\"tool_use\\\"` and a `tool_use` block whose `input` is `{\\\"tz\\\": \\\"Asia/Tokyo\\\"}`.\\n\\nOn Ollama 0.40.16 both succeed, so no proxy is required and `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` can point straight at the server root. On an older build where `/v1/messages` 404s, you need a translating proxy. Pin the version in your notes, because this capability arrived in a specific release.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"ollama\", \"anthropic-api\", \"tool-use\", \"verification\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"localllm_ops\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"My Ollama server sets OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH=32768. The client does not recognize my model id, so it assumes a 200k context window and lets the session grow to that. The model starts contradicting itself and forgetting earlier instructions late in long sessions. Explain the failure and fix it.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Two independent context limits exist: the client's belief about the window, which drives when it compacts, and the server's actual allocation, which drives what fits. When the client believes 200k and the server serves 32k, the client keeps appending instead of compacting. Once the prompt exceeds 32768 tokens the server truncates to fit. Truncation removes the oldest tokens, which is where the system prompt and early instructions live, so the model loses exactly the content that defined the task. Nothing errors, because truncation is a silent accommodation, not a failure. The fix is to make the client's assumed window match the server's real allocation, and the invariant is that the two numbers must be changed together.\",\n      \"output\": \"You have two context numbers disagreeing, and the smaller one wins silently.\\n\\n- The client believes the window is 200k, so it does not compact until 200k.\\n- The server allocates 32768, so anything past that is dropped before inference.\\n\\nWhen the prompt crosses 32768 tokens the server truncates from the front to make it fit. The front is where your system prompt and earliest instructions are, so the model loses its task definition while appearing to run normally. There is no error because truncation is how the server accommodates an oversized prompt.\\n\\nThe symptom profile is diagnostic: correct early in a session, degrading only after sustained use, and never accompanied by an error.\\n\\nFix by pinning the client to the real window:\\n\\n```bash\\nexport CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS=32768   # match OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH\\n```\\n\\nTreat the two as one coupled setting. If you raise the server allocation, raise this in the same commit, and leave a comment at each site naming the other. A client that compacts at the right boundary degrades visibly and recoverably; one that compacts too late degrades invisibly, which is strictly worse to debug.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"context-window\", \"truncation\", \"ollama\", \"silent-failure\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"localllm_ops\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Work out whether a 9B model at Q4_K_M fits in 8 GB of VRAM at 32k context with flash attention and a q8_0 KV cache. Show the arithmetic and state the ceiling.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Budget weights, KV cache, and overhead separately, then compare the total against the usable ceiling rather than the nominal card size. Q4_K_M averages roughly 4.8 bits per weight, so 9B weights land near 5.4 GB. The KV cache scales with layers, kv-heads, head_dim, and sequence length, times two for K and V. A 9B Qwen-class model is about 40 layers; with grouped-query attention of 8 kv-heads at head_dim 128 the per-token footprint at fp16 is 40 * 8 * 128 * 2 * 2 bytes, about 164 KB per token, so 32k tokens is roughly 5.4 GB, which alone busts the budget. q8_0 halves that to about 2.7 GB, still too much alongside the weights. The honest conclusion is that it does not fit at 32k, and the ceiling must be stated as a token count. Flash attention reduces transient attention workspace, not the stored cache, so it does not rescue the arithmetic.\",\n      \"output\": \"Budget each term, then compare against the usable ceiling, not the nominal 8 GB.\\n\\n**Weights.** Q4_K_M averages about 4.8 bits per weight.\\n\\n```\\n9e9 weights * 4.8 bits / 8 bits-per-byte = 5.4 GB\\n```\\n\\n**KV cache.** For about 40 layers, 8 kv-heads (GQA), head_dim 128, K and V both stored:\\n\\n```\\nper token, fp16 = 40 * 8 * 128 * 2 * 2 bytes = 164 KB\\n32768 tokens    = 164 KB * 32768           = 5.4 GB\\nat q8_0         = 5.4 GB / 2                = 2.7 GB\\n```\\n\\n**Overhead.** CUDA context plus fragmentation is about 0.6 GB. On a desktop card the compositor holds roughly 0.8 GB more.\\n\\n```\\nweights                 5.4 GB\\nKV cache @ 32k, q8_0    2.7 GB\\nCUDA context            0.6 GB\\n-----------------------------\\ntotal                   8.7 GB   vs ~7.2 GB usable\\n```\\n\\n**It does not fit.** You are about 1.5 GB over before the desktop is counted.\\n\\nThe ceiling, solved for tokens:\\n\\n```\\n(7.2 - 5.4 - 0.6) GB = 1.2 GB available for KV\\n1.2 GB / 82 KB-per-token (q8_0) = ~15000 tokens\\n```\\n\\nSo a 9B at Q4_K_M tops out near 12k-15k context on this card, and 32k requires dropping to the 4B model, where weights are about 2.4 GB. Flash attention shrinks the transient attention workspace, not the stored cache, so it does not change this result.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"vram\", \"kv-cache\", \"quantization\", \"arithmetic\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"localllm_ops\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Why does running `ollama serve` from a script with explicit environment variables beat starting the Ollama desktop tray app, when my models live on E: and I need 32k context on 8 GB?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The tray app starts the server with its own environment, so variables set in a user shell or a script do not reach it. The ones that matter here are the model store location and the memory-shaping flags. If OLLAMA_MODELS is not visible to the server process, it falls back to the default store on the system drive and re-downloads models that already exist elsewhere. The same applies to KV cache type and context length, which are what make a large context fit in a small budget. The general principle is that these are server-process settings, not client settings, so whoever launches the server owns them.\",\n      \"output\": \"Because those variables configure the **server process**, and the tray app does not inherit the environment you set elsewhere. Launch the server yourself and the settings apply.\\n\\n```powershell\\n$env:OLLAMA_MODELS        = 'E:\\\\LocalLLM\\\\models'  # keep the store off C:\\n$env:OLLAMA_HOST          = '127.0.0.1:11434'\\n$env:OLLAMA_FLASH_ATTENTION = '1'                 # smaller attention workspace\\n$env:OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE = 'q8_0'                # halves KV vs fp16\\n$env:OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH = '32768'\\n$env:OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS = '1'               # two would exceed 8 GB\\n$env:OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE    = '30m'                 # avoid reload per prompt\\nStart-Process -FilePath $exe -ArgumentList 'serve' -WindowStyle Hidden\\n```\\n\\nWhat each one buys you: `OLLAMA_MODELS` prevents a silent second copy of every model on the system drive; `q8_0` is what makes 32k affordable at all; `MAX_LOADED_MODELS=1` stops a second resident model from blowing the ceiling; `KEEP_ALIVE` avoids paying reload cost on every call.\\n\\nThen wait on readiness rather than sleeping a fixed interval:\\n\\n```powershell\\nfor ($i = 0; $i -lt 30; $i++) {\\n    Start-Sleep -Seconds 1\\n    try { $v = Invoke-RestMethod -Uri 'http://127.0.0.1:11434/api/version' -TimeoutSec 2\\n           Write-Host \\\"ready: $($v.version)\\\"; exit 0 } catch { }\\n}\\nWrite-Error 'Ollama did not come up within 30s.'\\n```\\n\\nKeep one script as the single owner of these flags, and have every launcher call it, so the tuning cannot drift between entry points.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"ollama\", \"server-config\", \"environment\", \"windows\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"localllm_ops\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"A client refuses my local model id outright with `[claude-code:unrecognized_model] {\\\"model\\\":\\\"qwen3.5:9b\\\"}` before any request leaves the machine. The endpoint is correct. How do I make the client accept an id it has never heard of?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"This is client-side validation, not a network result, which the log line itself proves by naming the client as the source. A client keys capability defaults off a catalog of known model ids: prompt profile, effort defaults, and assumed context window. An unknown id has no entry, so it is rejected before dispatch. The remedy is to declare a mapping from the unknown id to a known one whose client-side handling should apply. The mapping must not change the id actually sent over the wire, only the local handling. Pick the known model by capability class, not by name.\",\n      \"output\": \"That is local validation, and the `claude-code:` prefix tells you so: nothing was dispatched. The client needs a capability profile for the id, and an unknown id has none.\\n\\nDeclare a `behavesAs` row that maps your id onto a known model's client-side handling:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"modelPicker\\\": {\\n    \\\"options\\\": [\\n      { \\\"model\\\": \\\"qwen3.5:9b\\\",  \\\"label\\\": \\\"Qwen 3.5 9B (Local)\\\",\\n        \\\"behavesAs\\\": \\\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\\\" },\\n      { \\\"model\\\": \\\"qwen3.5:35b\\\", \\\"label\\\": \\\"Qwen 3.5 35B (Local)\\\",\\n        \\\"behavesAs\\\": \\\"claude-opus-5\\\" }\\n    ]\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`behavesAs` changes only local handling. The id sent on the wire stays `qwen3.5:9b`, which is what your server expects.\\n\\nChoose the target by capability class, not by prestige: map a small fast local model onto a small fast known model so effort and prompt defaults stay proportionate.\\n\\nTwo things to know before relying on it:\\n\\n- The registration must live where the client reads it. User-level settings are read; a project checkout is not, so a row placed there silently does nothing.\\n- The assumed context window comes from the mapped model, which is almost certainly wrong for yours. Pin the real one explicitly with `CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS`.\\n\\nDistinguish the two failure modes by their text: `unrecognized_model` is local and fixed by registration, while a bare \\\"may not exist or you may not have access\\\" is the server's 404 and fixed by correcting the request path.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"model-registry\", \"behaves-as\", \"client-validation\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"localllm_ops\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"I want to harvest chain-of-thought from my agent's saved transcripts to fine-tune a smaller model on its reasoning. The transcripts contain thinking blocks. My extractor finds them and returns empty strings. Is my parser broken?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The parser may be fine, so distinguish a parsing failure from an absent payload before editing code. The check is to read the raw blocks and inspect both their keys and their value lengths. If every thinking block carries a signature but a zero-length text, the content was never persisted, and no extractor can recover it. That is a property of the data source, not a bug. The consequence for a distillation pipeline is structural: harvesting can teach final answers and exact tool-call JSON, but reasoning traces have to be authored by the teacher rather than mined.\",\n      \"output\": \"Check whether the payload exists before suspecting the parser. Inspect keys **and** value lengths:\\n\\n```python\\nimport json\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\n\\nlens = []\\nfor line in Path(transcript).read_text(encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\", errors=\\\"replace\\\").splitlines():\\n    if not line.strip():\\n        continue\\n    try:\\n        ev = json.loads(line)\\n    except json.JSONDecodeError:\\n        continue\\n    msg = ev.get(\\\"message\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(msg, dict):\\n        continue\\n    content = msg.get(\\\"content\\\")\\n    if isinstance(content, list):\\n        for blk in content:\\n            if isinstance(blk, dict) and blk.get(\\\"type\\\") == \\\"thinking\\\":\\n                lens.append(len(blk.get(\\\"thinking\\\") or \\\"\\\"))\\n\\nprint(f\\\"thinking blocks: {len(lens)}  non-empty: {sum(1 for n in lens if n > 0)}\\\")\\n```\\n\\nIf you get many blocks and zero non-empty, the blocks are structural placeholders carrying a `signature` with no text. The reasoning was not written to disk, so no extractor can recover it and there is nothing to fix in your code.\\n\\nThat reshapes the pipeline. Harvesting still yields two things worth training on: final answers in your own register, and exact tool-call JSON, which is the highest-value target for a small model because one malformed call breaks an entire agent loop. Reasoning traces must be **authored** by the teacher instead.\\n\\nSo make the quality filter honest about it, rather than exempting silently:\\n\\n```yaml\\nquality_filters:\\n  require_thinking: true   # authored lessons only\\n  # Harvested pairs carry no CoT. They pass on tool-call fidelity instead,\\n  # so count them separately and never report them as reasoning data.\\n```\\n\\nAnd assert on the number: if an authored batch reports zero traces, fail the run loudly instead of training on a silently degraded set.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"distillation\", \"transcripts\", \"chain-of-thought\", \"data-quality\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"localllm_ops\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Why serve inference on native Windows but run QLoRA training inside WSL2, when both see the same GPU and the same files?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The split is about library maturity rather than hardware. Inference runtimes ship working native Windows CUDA builds. The training stack depends on bitsandbytes and Triton, whose Windows support lags and often fails at the kernel-compilation step rather than cleanly at import, which wastes debugging time. WSL2 exposes the same GPU through the same driver and can reach the same files through the drive mount, so nothing is duplicated. The cost worth naming is that WSL2 has its own loopback, so a server bound to the Windows localhost is not reachable at localhost from inside WSL2.\",\n      \"output\": \"Library maturity, not hardware. Both halves see the same card.\\n\\n- **Inference on Windows.** Native CUDA builds are well supported and fast, roughly 63-77 tok/s for a 9B at Q4_K_M on an RTX 4060 Ti.\\n- **Training in WSL2.** The QLoRA stack leans on bitsandbytes and Triton, whose Windows support trails Linux and tends to fail during kernel compilation rather than at import, so you lose time to errors that look like your configuration.\\n\\nNo files are duplicated: WSL2 reaches the same tree through the drive mount.\\n\\n```bash\\nwsl -d Ubuntu\\ncd /mnt/e/LocalLLM/teacher_pipeline\\nsource .venv/bin/activate\\npython train_student.py --check      # verify env + VRAM before a long run\\n```\\n\\nThe one sharp edge is networking. WSL2 has its own loopback, so a server bound to the Windows localhost is not at `localhost` inside WSL2. Point at the host gateway:\\n\\n```bash\\nexport OLLAMA_URL=\\\"http://$(ip route show default | awk '{print $3}'):11434\\\"\\n```\\n\\nBudget VRAM so the two never contend: with one model resident for inference and a QLoRA run peaking near 6 GB, do not serve and train at the same time on an 8 GB card. Stop the server, train, then restart it.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"wsl2\", \"qlora\", \"cuda\", \"toolchain\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"localllm_ops\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Name the four defences that keep an incremental QLoRA lesson from causing catastrophic forgetting, and say what each one actually prevents.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Each defence covers a distinct failure path, which is why the set is not redundant. Freezing base weights bounds the worst case structurally, since an adapter can be discarded. Replay addresses distribution shift, where training only on new data moves the model off old behaviour even with the base frozen. A learning-rate floor controls step size so lessons accumulate instead of overshooting. A regression gate is the only defence that detects damage rather than preventing it, which matters because the other three reduce probability without proving absence. Two supporting mechanisms are worth naming: masking loss on prompts so the model learns answers rather than imitating user phrasing, and a ledger of trained fingerprints so replay and novelty are computable.\",\n      \"output\": \"Four defences, each covering a different failure path.\\n\\n**1. LoRA only, base frozen.** Bounds the worst case structurally. Original weights are never overwritten, so a bad lesson is reverted by deleting an adapter rather than by re-downloading a model.\\n\\n**2. Replay buffer, 30% old examples per batch.** Prevents distribution shift. Even with the base frozen, training exclusively on new data pulls the adapter toward it and away from earlier competence. Replay keeps old behaviour in the gradient signal.\\n\\n**3. Low LR with a floor, 2e-4 decaying cosine to 5e-5.** Controls step size. Decaying to zero would make late examples contribute nothing; a floor lets lessons stack gently instead of lurching.\\n\\n**4. Regression gate on a held-out split.** The only defence that *detects* damage instead of reducing its probability. Grade before and after, and fail the run when a previously-passing skill drops more than 5%.\\n\\n```bash\\npython grade_student.py --model qwen3.5-student:v2 --compare reports/baseline.json\\n# non-zero exit = roll back the adapter\\n```\\n\\nTwo supporting mechanisms make the four work:\\n\\n- `train_on_responses_only` masks loss on user turns, so the model learns to answer rather than to imitate how questions are phrased.\\n- A ledger of trained example fingerprints makes \\\"already seen\\\" computable, so replay samples real history and new batches are genuinely new.\\n\\nThe ordering matters: the first three are preventive and probabilistic, the fourth is detective and decisive. Without the gate you are trusting three mitigations with no measurement.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"qlora\", \"catastrophic-forgetting\", \"replay\", \"regression-guard\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"A user reports their local model \\\"doesn't answer\\\" in their agent CLI, though the model is downloaded and the server is running. Give me the ordered plan you would execute, and say what makes each step the cheapest sufficient one.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The complaint names the model, but the model is one of several layers that can produce that symptom, so the plan should bisect the stack rather than start where the user pointed. Cheapest first means read configuration before running anything, then test the server without the client, then the client against the server, and only then the model's behaviour. The key discipline is to isolate layers: proving the server answers a hand-built request separates server faults from client faults. The last step matters most and is the one usually skipped, which is running one real end-to-end prompt, because every earlier step can pass while the whole path still fails.\",\n      \"output\": \"Bisect the stack from cheapest to most expensive, and do not start where the complaint points.\\n\\n**1. Read the configuration.** No processes, no network. Resolve which settings file and which environment variables are actually in effect, because the usual cause is a launcher not being used at all. Cheapest possible signal.\\n\\n**2. Prove the server alone.** One hand-built request, client bypassed.\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -s http://localhost:11434/api/version\\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w \\\"%{http_code}\\\\n\\\" http://localhost:11434/v1/messages \\\\\\n  -H \\\"content-type: application/json\\\" \\\\\\n  -d '{\\\"model\\\":\\\"<id>\\\",\\\"max_tokens\\\":5,\\\"messages\\\":[{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"hi\\\"}]}'\\n```\\n\\nThis splits the space in half: a 200 here moves all suspicion to the client.\\n\\n**3. Compare the paths.** If step 2 passed and the client still fails, the client is building a different request. Check the path it constructs against the one the server exposes, and the model id it sends against the one the server knows.\\n\\n**4. Read the error's origin, not its wording.** A prefix naming the client means local validation and nothing was dispatched. An unprefixed message about model access is usually the server's 404 arriving through a client that reports 404 as a model problem.\\n\\n**5. Run one real prompt end to end.** The step most often skipped, and the only one that proves the whole path.\\n\\n```bash\\n<launcher> -p \\\"reply with only the word HELLO\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**6. Only now question the model.** Reasoning quality and tool-call reliability are judged after transport is known good, otherwise you are tuning a model whose requests never arrive.\\n\\nTwo failure modes to name explicitly. Assuming the error text identifies the real layer is what makes this bug expensive: a transport 404 wearing a model-identity message sends people to re-pull models that were never the problem. And a `curl` against a URL you assembled by hand only tests your assumption about the path, so the end-to-end prompt in step 5 is not optional.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"debugging\", \"bisection\", \"layer-isolation\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"I asked you to add a wrapper script and you declared it done. In cmd.exe it fails with \\\"'mycmd' is not recognized as an internal or external command\\\". What did you skip, and what is the general rule?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Two assumptions went unchecked. First, that a shell-agnostic file is runnable from any shell, when an extensionless script with a shebang is executable by a POSIX shell but not by cmd.exe, which dispatches on extension through PATHEXT. Second, that the install directory was on the relevant PATH, when some shells prepend directories the system PATH does not contain. The general rule is that a command is only installed once it has been invoked from the shell the user actually types in. The error text also identifies the shell, which is a useful diagnostic signal in itself.\",\n      \"output\": \"I skipped verification in your shell. That message is cmd.exe's, which tells us two assumptions went unchecked.\\n\\n**Extension.** cmd.exe and PowerShell dispatch on extension via `PATHEXT`. An extensionless file with a shebang is runnable by a POSIX shell and invisible as a command to cmd. It needs a `.cmd` sibling.\\n\\n**PATH.** Some shells prepend directories the system PATH does not have. Git Bash adds `~/bin` on its own, so an installer that \\\"worked\\\" there proves nothing about cmd. Check the real PATH at its source:\\n\\n```powershell\\n(Get-ItemProperty -Path 'HKCU:\\\\Environment' -Name Path).Path -split ';'\\n```\\n\\nThe better fix is usually to install into a directory already on both PATHs rather than to edit PATH, since an edit needs a new session and affects everything.\\n\\nThen verify from the shell that failed, not the one that worked:\\n\\n```bash\\ncmd.exe /c \\\"mycmd --version\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe rule: **a command is installed when it has been invoked from the shell the user types in.** Anything short of that is a plausible installation, not a verified one. For cross-shell tools that means one thin native wrapper per shell family, both delegating to a single implementation so behaviour cannot drift.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"windows\", \"path\", \"pathext\", \"verification\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"My wrapper calls `powershell -File launch.ps1 %*` to set env vars and start a CLI. Quoted flags get mangled: `-p \\\"say hi\\\"` loses the prompt, and piping makes it complain about a parameter named PipelineVariable. What is happening and what is the right architecture?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"PowerShell binds parameters by prefix match, and its common parameters are in scope for any advanced function or script. A short flag intended for the inner program can match a PowerShell parameter name as a prefix, so -p resolves to -PipelineVariable and swallows the following token as its argument. The inner program then receives a flag with no value. This is not a quoting problem to be escaped away; it is the wrong layer owning argument parsing. The correct architecture removes the parameter binder from the pass-through path entirely, either by writing the wrapper natively in the target shell or by terminating parsing explicitly. The error message naming a parameter nobody wrote is the tell.\",\n      \"output\": \"PowerShell is consuming your flags. It binds parameters by **prefix match**, and its common parameters are in scope for a script with a `param()` block. `-p` is a unique-enough prefix of `-PipelineVariable`, so PowerShell claims it and takes the next token as its value. Your inner program then sees `--print` with no prompt.\\n\\nThe tell is an error naming a parameter you never wrote:\\n\\n```\\nMissing an argument for parameter 'PipelineVariable'.\\n```\\n\\nThis is not a quoting bug, so escaping harder will not fix it. The wrong layer owns argument parsing. Remove the binder from the pass-through path.\\n\\n**Write the wrapper natively in the shell it serves.** For cmd, set the environment and invoke directly, rebuilding the tail by hand because `%*` ignores `shift`:\\n\\n```batch\\n@echo off\\nsetlocal EnableExtensions\\n\\nif /i \\\"%~1\\\"==\\\"35b\\\" ( set \\\"MODEL=qwen3.5:35b\\\" & shift )\\n\\nset \\\"ARGS=\\\"\\n:collect\\nif \\\"%~1\\\"==\\\"\\\" goto ready\\nset \\\"ARGS=%ARGS% %1\\\"      & rem %1 not %~1, to keep quoting intact\\nshift\\ngoto collect\\n:ready\\n\\nset \\\"ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434\\\"\\nclaude%ARGS%\\n```\\n\\nIf a PowerShell script must stay in the path, terminate parsing explicitly with the stop-parsing token so everything after it passes through verbatim:\\n\\n```powershell\\nclaude --% @ClaudeArgs\\n```\\n\\nThe principle: **every shell between the user and the program is another parser with its own opinions.** Each hop can claim arguments. Keep the hop count at one, and verify with a flag that collides on purpose, such as `-p \\\"two words\\\"`, rather than with a single bare token that would pass either way.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"powershell\", \"argument-parsing\", \"wrapper\", \"cmd\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Write me a dual-mode launcher rule: one command for a local model, one for the cloud API, such that a leftover environment variable can never silently route a local model id at a paid cloud endpoint.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The hazard is asymmetric cleanup. Setting local routing in a shell is sticky, so a later cloud launch inherits a local model id and sends it to the cloud API, which 404s and bills nothing but wastes time, or worse succeeds against a real model the user did not intend. The fix is that each mode must scrub the other's variables rather than only setting its own. One mode setting variables and the other merely not setting them is the bug. A read-only diagnostic belongs alongside the two commands so the user can inspect routing without launching, and it should flag the specific contradictory combination.\",\n      \"output\": \"Each mode must **scrub the other's variables**, not merely set its own. A mode that only sets, paired with a mode that only omits, is exactly how a stale value survives.\\n\\nKeep one list and use it in both directions:\\n\\n```bash\\nROUTING_VARS=(\\n  ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL ANTHROPIC_MODEL ANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL\\n  ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_OPUS_MODEL ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL\\n  ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS\\n)\\n\\nif [[ \\\"$USE_CLOUD\\\" == \\\"true\\\" ]]; then\\n  unset \\\"${ROUTING_VARS[@]}\\\" ANTHROPIC_API_KEY ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN\\nelse\\n  unset ANTHROPIC_API_KEY ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN\\n  export ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=\\\"$OLLAMA_URL\\\"          # server root, no /v1\\n  export ANTHROPIC_MODEL=\\\"$MODEL\\\"\\n  export CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS=32768      # match the server\\nfi\\n```\\n\\nScrub the dummy local key in cloud mode too, since a non-empty key shadows an interactive login.\\n\\nAdd a read-only check that names the one contradictory state:\\n\\n```bash\\nif [[ -z \\\"${ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL:-}\\\" && \\\"${ANTHROPIC_MODEL:-}\\\" == qwen* ]]; then\\n  echo \\\"BROKEN: a local model id is pointed at the cloud API.\\\"\\nfi\\n```\\n\\nThat combination, local id with no local endpoint, is the signature of the failure and is worth detecting by name.\\n\\nTest it the way it breaks: export the local variables, then run the cloud command in that same shell and assert both are empty. Testing cloud mode from a clean shell passes while the bug remains.\\n\\nLastly, remove the trap rather than documenting it. If a menu offers local models inside a cloud session, one keystroke recreates the failure, so scope that registration to where it is needed.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"env-hygiene\", \"launcher\", \"routing\", \"idempotence\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"I told you to delete a config block because it looked like a footgun. After deleting it the tool broke in a new way. How should you have handled that, and what do you do now?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"A block that looks redundant may be load-bearing, and the way to find out is to test the behaviour it supposedly provides before removing it rather than reasoning about its purpose from its shape. The correct move is to form the hypothesis, verify by exercising the real path once with the block present, then remove and re-verify. Recovery is to restore it, confirm the original failure is gone, then address the original hazard by narrowing scope rather than by deletion. It is also worth stating plainly that the deletion was the wrong call, because an unacknowledged wrong step gets rebuilt later.\",\n      \"output\": \"I removed it on appearance rather than on evidence. A block that looks redundant is a hypothesis, and the test is cheap: exercise the behaviour it supposedly provides, once, with the block present, before deleting anything.\\n\\nWhat I should have run first:\\n\\n```bash\\n<launcher> -p \\\"reply with only the word HELLO\\\"   # with the block in place\\n```\\n\\nIf that works and fails after deletion, the block is load-bearing and the question was answered in one command.\\n\\nRecovery now, in order:\\n\\n1. **Restore it** and confirm the new failure disappears. Do not stack a second change on top of an unverified first one.\\n2. **Re-run the original symptom** to establish which hazard is real and which was speculative.\\n3. **Address the real hazard by narrowing scope, not by removal.** If the danger is that a menu offers local models inside a cloud session, the fix is to scope where it is registered and to make the safe path the easy one, not to delete the registration that makes local models work at all.\\n\\nThe general rule: **deleting configuration is a change, and it needs the same verification as adding it.** \\\"This looks like a footgun\\\" justifies investigating, never removing.\\n\\nOne more thing worth saying out loud: the deletion was my error, not an unlucky interaction. Naming it matters, because an unacknowledged wrong step tends to get rebuilt later by whoever reads the diff and assumes it was deliberate.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"change-safety\", \"verification\", \"error-recovery\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Give me a rule for when a passing `curl` is not sufficient evidence that an integration works, using a concrete example.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"A hand-built request tests the path the tester assumed, not the path the client builds. When those differ the curl passes and the integration fails, and the passing curl actively misleads by appearing to exonerate the transport. The concrete case is a base URL with a version suffix: curl against the correct endpoint returns 200 while the client appends its own version segment and 404s. The rule is that evidence must come from the real caller, and the cheapest version of that is a single non-interactive end-to-end prompt. A corollary is that each layer should be probed with the artifact that layer actually uses.\",\n      \"output\": \"A hand-built `curl` tests **the path you assumed**, not the path your client builds. When those differ, the curl passes and the integration still fails, and the passing curl misleads by appearing to clear the transport.\\n\\nConcrete case. Base URL set to `http://localhost:11434/v1`:\\n\\n```bash\\n# your test, against the endpoint you believe in\\ncurl .../v1/messages      -> 200   # looks healthy\\n# what the client actually requests, appending its own version segment\\n         /v1/v1/messages  -> 404   # the real behaviour\\n```\\n\\nThe curl was correct and irrelevant. Hours go to the model because transport appeared proven.\\n\\n**Rule: evidence must come from the real caller.** The cheapest form is one non-interactive prompt through the actual entry point:\\n\\n```bash\\n<launcher> -p \\\"reply with only the word HELLO\\\"\\n```\\n\\nIf that prints `HELLO`, the whole path is good: resolution, environment, URL construction, auth, model id, and decoding. No assembly of curls proves as much.\\n\\nThe corollary is to probe each layer with the artifact that layer uses. Use `curl` to ask whether the **server** can serve a request, never to conclude that the **client** will. And when a wrapper sits in between, invoke through the wrapper, since the wrapper is part of what you are testing.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"verification\", \"evidence\", \"integration-testing\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"tool_call_format\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"easy\",\n      \"instruction\": \"You have a tool `http_get` with schema {\\\"url\\\": string (required), \\\"timeout_s\\\": integer (optional)}. Check the Ollama server version at http://localhost:11434/api/version with a 3 second timeout. Emit only the tool call.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Both parameters are specified by the request, so include url and timeout_s and nothing else. The tool name is used verbatim. No prose around the JSON, because the request asked for the call alone.\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"http_get\\\",\\n  \\\"input\\\": {\\n    \\\"url\\\": \\\"http://localhost:11434/api/version\\\",\\n    \\\"timeout_s\\\": 3\\n  }\\n}\\n```\",\n      \"tags\": [\"schema\", \"exact-call\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"tool_call_format\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"You have a tool `run_bash` with schema {\\\"command\\\": string (required), \\\"timeout_ms\\\": integer (optional)}. The user says: \\\"check if ollama is up and also tell me what models I have, in one go\\\". Emit only the tool call.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Two pieces of information are wanted but only one tool is available, so combine them into a single command rather than inventing a second call or a parameter that does not exist. Chain with a separator that keeps running if the first part fails, so a down server still yields the second answer. No timeout was specified, so omit the optional field rather than guessing a value.\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"run_bash\\\",\\n  \\\"input\\\": {\\n    \\\"command\\\": \\\"curl -s http://localhost:11434/api/version; echo '---'; curl -s http://localhost:11434/api/tags\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\",\n      \"tags\": [\"schema\", \"single-call\", \"optional-params\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"tool_call_format\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"You have a tool `write_file` with schema {\\\"path\\\": string (required), \\\"content\\\": string (required)}. The user asks you to save the two-line launcher described earlier to C:\\\\tools\\\\cclocal.cmd. Emit only the tool call, with correct JSON escaping.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The content contains backslashes in the Windows path and newlines between lines, so both must be escaped for JSON: backslashes doubled and newlines as escape sequences. The path value itself contains backslashes and needs the same treatment. Only the two declared parameters appear.\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"write_file\\\",\\n  \\\"input\\\": {\\n    \\\"path\\\": \\\"C:\\\\\\\\tools\\\\\\\\cclocal.cmd\\\",\\n    \\\"content\\\": \\\"@echo off\\\\r\\\\nset \\\\\\\"ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434\\\\\\\"\\\\r\\\\nclaude %*\\\\r\\\\n\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\",\n      \"tags\": [\"schema\", \"json-escaping\", \"windows-paths\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"tool_call_format\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"You have a tool `grade_model` with schema {\\\"model\\\": string (required), \\\"eval_file\\\": string (required), \\\"compare_to\\\": string (optional), \\\"temperature\\\": number (optional)}. The user says: \\\"baseline the student, then I guess compare it to whatever we had before, at whatever temperature is stable\\\". Emit the tool call and one line on any judgement you made.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Two parts of the request are vague. A baseline run is by definition the first measurement, so there is no prior report to compare against and compare_to must be omitted rather than filled with a guessed path, since a wrong path would either error or silently compare against the wrong run. Temperature is requested only as stable, and low temperature is what makes scores reproducible across runs, so 0.3 is a defensible concrete value and is worth stating as a choice. Inventing a compare_to value would be the real error here.\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"grade_model\\\",\\n  \\\"input\\\": {\\n    \\\"model\\\": \\\"qwen3.5:4b\\\",\\n    \\\"eval_file\\\": \\\"dataset/eval.jsonl\\\",\\n    \\\"temperature\\\": 0.3\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nOmitted `compare_to`: a baseline is the first measurement, so there is no prior report, and a guessed path would either error or quietly compare against the wrong run. Chose `temperature: 0.3` because low temperature is what makes scores comparable between runs.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"schema\", \"ambiguity\", \"omit-over-guess\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"structured_extraction\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"easy\",\n      \"instruction\": \"From this Ollama response, return exactly {\\\"name\\\": string, \\\"params\\\": string, \\\"quant\\\": string, \\\"context\\\": integer, \\\"tools\\\": boolean}. Input: {\\\"name\\\":\\\"qwen3.5:9b\\\",\\\"size\\\":6550825550,\\\"details\\\":{\\\"format\\\":\\\"gguf\\\",\\\"family\\\":\\\"qwen35\\\",\\\"parameter_size\\\":\\\"9.0B\\\",\\\"quantization_level\\\":\\\"Q4_K_M\\\",\\\"context_length\\\":262144},\\\"capabilities\\\":[\\\"completion\\\",\\\"vision\\\",\\\"tools\\\",\\\"thinking\\\"]}\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Map each requested key to its source: name at the top level, parameter_size and quantization_level from details, context_length as an integer, and tools as the presence of the string in the capabilities array rendered as a boolean. Return only the five requested keys, dropping size, format, and family, and add no commentary.\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"qwen3.5:9b\\\",\\n  \\\"params\\\": \\\"9.0B\\\",\\n  \\\"quant\\\": \\\"Q4_K_M\\\",\\n  \\\"context\\\": 262144,\\n  \\\"tools\\\": true\\n}\\n```\",\n      \"tags\": [\"exact-schema\", \"no-extra-keys\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"structured_extraction\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"From this grading report return exactly {\\\"model\\\": string, \\\"overall\\\": number, \\\"weakest_skill\\\": string, \\\"weakest_score\\\": number, \\\"passed\\\": boolean} where passed is overall >= 0.70. Input: {\\\"model\\\":\\\"qwen3.5:4b\\\",\\\"overall_score\\\":0.471,\\\"pass_rate\\\":0.0,\\\"by_skill\\\":{\\\"agentic_planning\\\":0.493,\\\"localllm_ops\\\":0.17,\\\"python_stack\\\":0.559,\\\"supply_chain\\\":0.479,\\\"tool_call_format\\\":0.655}}\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Overall maps from overall_score. The weakest skill requires scanning by_skill for the minimum value, which is localllm_ops at 0.17, below python_stack and the rest. The passed flag is computed from the stated threshold: 0.471 is below 0.70, so false. Emit only the five keys, preserving the numbers exactly rather than rounding.\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"model\\\": \\\"qwen3.5:4b\\\",\\n  \\\"overall\\\": 0.471,\\n  \\\"weakest_skill\\\": \\\"localllm_ops\\\",\\n  \\\"weakest_score\\\": 0.17,\\n  \\\"passed\\\": false\\n}\\n```\",\n      \"tags\": [\"exact-schema\", \"computed-field\", \"min-scan\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"structured_extraction\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Parse this launcher output into exactly {\\\"mode\\\": \\\"local\\\"|\\\"cloud\\\", \\\"endpoint\\\": string|null, \\\"model\\\": string|null, \\\"warnings\\\": [string]}. Input:\\n\\n  Claude Code -> LOCAL model (Ollama)\\n  endpoint : http://localhost:11434\\n  model    : qwen3.5:9b   (small: qwen3.5:4b)\\n\\n  WARN connectors disabled because ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is set\\n  HELLO\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Mode comes from the LOCAL marker in the banner. The endpoint and model are the values after the labels, and the model field should carry the primary id only, since the small model is parenthetical and has no key in the requested schema. One warning line is present and belongs in the array as a single string. The trailing HELLO is model output, not launcher metadata, so it is excluded. Nulls are unnecessary here because both fields were found.\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"mode\\\": \\\"local\\\",\\n  \\\"endpoint\\\": \\\"http://localhost:11434\\\",\\n  \\\"model\\\": \\\"qwen3.5:9b\\\",\\n  \\\"warnings\\\": [\\n    \\\"connectors disabled because ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is set\\\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\n      \"tags\": [\"exact-schema\", \"log-parsing\", \"nullable\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"supply_chain\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"My training environment installs unsloth, bitsandbytes, triton, torch and trl unpinned into a fresh venv on every machine. Treat the version ranges as a security boundary rather than a compatibility one and tell me what to do.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The relevant question is not whether an unpinned upgrade breaks the build but what executes and when. These packages compile and load native kernels at import and first use, so resolution time is code-execution time, and any compromised release in the dependency closure runs with the training process's privileges, which include the dataset and output adapters. Unpinned resolution also means the environment is unreproducible, so a bad run cannot be attributed to a version. Controls must be verifiable: a hash-pinned lock, offline installation so resolution cannot reach the network at build time, and a recorded manifest so what ran is knowable afterwards. An SBOM generated from the lock only describes what was installed, not what the process later downloads at runtime, which is a gap worth naming.\",\n      \"output\": \"**The concrete risk.** This stack compiles and loads native kernels at import and first use, so dependency resolution is code execution. An unpinned install lets any new release in the closure, including a compromised one, run with the training process's privileges: your dataset, your credentials, and the adapters you are about to publish. Unpinned also means unreproducible, so a poisoned or merely broken run cannot be attributed to a version afterwards.\\n\\n**Verifiable controls, in order.**\\n\\n1. **Hash-pinned lock, committed.** Pinning a version is not enough, because a version can be re-uploaded on some indexes. Pin the artifact.\\n\\n```bash\\nuv pip compile requirements-train.in --generate-hashes -o requirements-train.txt\\nuv pip sync --require-hashes requirements-train.txt\\n```\\n\\n2. **Install offline from a vetted wheelhouse.** Resolution should not reach the network during a build, which closes the window where a fresh upload becomes your dependency.\\n\\n```bash\\nuv pip download --require-hashes -r requirements-train.txt -d wheelhouse/\\nuv pip install --no-index --find-links wheelhouse/ -r requirements-train.txt\\n```\\n\\n3. **Record what actually ran.** Write the resolved versions next to the adapter, so a regression is attributable to a specific environment.\\n\\n```bash\\nuv pip freeze > adapters/lesson_02/env.lock\\n```\\n\\n4. **Separate the privilege.** Training needs no push credentials. Give the run no token, and publish from a separate step.\\n\\n**The limit of these controls, stated plainly.** An SBOM from the lock describes what was *installed*, not what the process *fetches at runtime*. A trainer that downloads a base model or a kernel on first use still pulls unverified bytes. Close that by pre-fetching the base model and pinning it by digest, then running with egress disabled so a runtime fetch fails loudly instead of silently succeeding.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"pinning\", \"hashes\", \"import-time-execution\", \"sbom\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"supply_chain\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"I pull a GGUF from a model hub and run `ollama create` from a Modelfile. What should I verify before serving it, and what does a digest actually prove?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"A GGUF is data that configures execution rather than a script, but it still carries a baked-in chat template and generation defaults, and the template is interpreted at inference time, so it shapes behaviour in ways the user did not write. Identity should be established by digest, with the important caveat that a digest proves only that the bytes match a reference, not that the reference is trustworthy or that the weights are unmodified relative to an upstream original. The remaining checks are about what the artifact asserts: the template, the stop tokens, and whether the quantization and context match the budget being planned against. Serving locally also means the surface is the server's bind address.\",\n      \"output\": \"**What to verify, in order.**\\n\\n1. **Identity by digest, recorded.** Capture it at pull time and compare on every later load.\\n\\n```bash\\nsha256sum model.gguf | tee models/qwen3.5-9b.sha256\\nollama show qwen3.5:9b --modelfile | grep -i '^FROM'\\n```\\n\\n2. **The chat template and stop tokens.** A GGUF carries a baked-in template that is interpreted at inference time. A wrong or hostile template can inject standing instructions into every conversation, or omit stop tokens so generations run past their end. Read it rather than trusting it.\\n\\n```bash\\nollama show qwen3.5:9b --template\\n```\\n\\n3. **The asserted geometry against your budget.** Confirm `quantization_level` and `context_length` match what you planned VRAM for, since a model advertising 262144 context will happily accept a request your card cannot hold.\\n\\n4. **The bind address.** `OLLAMA_HOST=127.0.0.1:11434` keeps the server local. A `0.0.0.0` bind exposes unauthenticated inference to the network.\\n\\n**What a digest proves, precisely.** That the bytes you have match the bytes you recorded. That is genuinely useful: it detects corruption and substitution after the fact, and makes a rollback decidable.\\n\\n**What it does not prove.** That the reference was trustworthy when you first recorded it, that the weights correspond to the training the card claims, or that the template is benign. A digest pins identity, not provenance and not intent. Trust-on-first-use is the actual model, so record the digest from a source you have reason to trust, and treat a changed digest on a tag you did not update as an incident rather than an inconvenience.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"gguf\", \"digest\", \"chat-template\", \"provenance\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"python_stack\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Review this helper that reads harvested examples and counts how many carry a reasoning trace. Fix what is wrong.\\n\\n```python\\ndef count_traces(path):\\n    f = open(path)\\n    data = f.read()\\n    n = 0\\n    for line in data.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\"):\\n        try:\\n            rec = json.loads(line)\\n            if rec[\\\"meta\\\"][\\\"has_thinking\\\"] == True:\\n                n = n + 1\\n        except:\\n            pass\\n    return n\\n```\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Several issues. The file is opened without an explicit encoding and never closed, which is both a resource leak and a portability bug since the platform default differs between machines. Reading the whole file defeats the streaming nature of JSON lines. The bare except swallows everything including KeyboardInterrupt and genuine KeyError bugs, hiding malformed records rather than surfacing them. Comparison to True is redundant, and chained subscripting raises on records missing the key instead of treating absence as false. There are no type hints and no docstring explaining why absence is tolerated. The rewrite should stream with a context manager, narrow the exception to JSON decoding, use get chains for optional metadata, and return a count alongside the total so a caller can detect the zero-trace case that matters here.\",\n      \"output\": \"```python\\nimport json\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\n\\n\\ndef count_traces(path: Path) -> tuple[int, int]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Count examples carrying a reasoning trace.\\n\\n    Returns (with_trace, total). Both are returned because the ratio is the\\n    useful signal: a harvested batch legitimately has zero traces, so a caller\\n    needs the denominator to tell \\\"no data\\\" from \\\"no reasoning in the data\\\".\\n    A line that does not parse is skipped rather than fatal, since transcripts\\n    are appended live and the final line can be half-written.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    with_trace = total = 0\\n    with path.open(encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") as fh:\\n        for line in fh:\\n            line = line.strip()\\n            if not line:\\n                continue\\n            try:\\n                rec = json.loads(line)\\n            except json.JSONDecodeError:\\n                continue\\n            total += 1\\n            if rec.get(\\\"meta\\\", {}).get(\\\"has_thinking\\\"):\\n                with_trace += 1\\n    return with_trace, total\\n```\\n\\nWhat changed and why:\\n\\n- **Explicit `encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\"`.** The platform default differs between machines, so transcripts with non-ASCII content decode differently on Windows than on Linux.\\n- **Context manager.** The original never closed the handle.\\n- **Streams line by line.** `read()` then `split` loads the whole file for no benefit; JSON lines is designed to stream.\\n- **Narrow `except json.JSONDecodeError`.** A bare `except` swallowed `KeyboardInterrupt` and would have hidden a `KeyError` from the hard-coded `rec[\\\"meta\\\"]` path, turning a schema bug into a silently low count.\\n- **`.get()` chain.** Missing metadata now reads as false instead of raising.\\n- **Dropped `== True`.** Truthiness is the intent.\\n- **Returns the denominator.** Distinguishing zero-of-zero from zero-of-two-hundred is exactly the distinction that matters when auditing a harvest.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"type-hints\", \"explicit-encoding\", \"narrow-except\", \"jsonl\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n"
