Done. PR #531 is green and mergeable — 32 checks pass, 1 skipping, `mergeable_state: clean`.

Two things were wrong with it.

**1. It was conflicting with `develop` (`mergeable: false`).** `develop` had moved 6 commits ahead — the 5.0.0 release plus two Dependabot bumps. Oddly, git merges those two SHAs textually clean; GitHub reported `dirty` anyway, and no test job had run at all because of it. Merging `develop` in and pushing cleared both.

But there was a real conflict git couldn't see. The 5.0.0 release moved the `## [Unreleased]` heading down and inserted a released section under it. Since this branch's audit entries were written under `### Fixed`/`### Security` headings that now belong to `[5.0.0]`, the auto-merge silently filed all ~20 audit-round-7 entries **inside the released 5.0.0 section** and left `[Unreleased]` empty — i.e. it would have claimed shipped-in-5.0.0 for work that hasn't shipped. I moved them back under `[Unreleased]` and verified the `[5.0.0]` section and everything below it is now byte-identical to `develop`. The 25 version sites take `develop`'s 5.0.0; I left the version derivation alone.

**2. Once the suite could finally run, 8 jobs failed — and it was a real bug in this branch's own SEC-01 fix.** All four Ubuntu and all four macOS test suites failed `test_unredacted_artifacts_are_owner_only`; every Windows job passed.

`_restrict_if_unredacted` applied its `chmod 0600` by iterating `written`, the manifest's list of artifacts. But `write_local()` adds `local.json` and `.gitignore` to the index root *after* the manifest is written, so neither could ever be in that list — both stayed at 0644, world-readable, exactly when the operator passed `--secret-policy off/warn-only`. `local.json` carries the source root and the machine marker. The fix walks the staged tree instead (0600 files, 0700 dirs), which also covers any artifact added later instead of a list someone has to keep in sync.

Worth noting why this slipped through: the mode-bit tests are `skipif(os.name == "nt")`, because `chmod` on Windows only moves the read-only bit. A Windows-only run — which is what I can do locally, and reports 2784 passed — can never see it. I installed the package under WSL to confirm properly: the published test fails on the old helper with `{'local.json': '0o644', '.gitignore': '0o644'}` and passes on the new one.

One thing I did **not** change, flagging it since it's your call: `develop`'s released 5.0.0 CHANGELOG already describes the file-descriptor double-close fix as shipped, but `develop`'s `repo2graph/graph.py` still has the buggy two-close version — the code fix only exists on this branch. So 5.0.0 shipped a changelog claim ahead of its code. This PR makes it true on merge, but the 5.0.0 release notes are inaccurate as published.

