I'll look at your OBS configuration.

Now the scene collection and plugin configs — the audio routing and nested-encoder details matter most.

I read your whole OBS config — active profile, all four profiles, the scene collection's audio routing, plugin configs, and the last 20 logs. Hardware: Ryzen 7 5700X / RTX 4060 Ti 8 GB / 64 GB, single 1080p240 display, OBS 32.2.2.

Short version: the architecture is genuinely good (advanced mode, 6-track audio with a DMCA-safe VOD track, per-process audio capture, source-record clean feeds, profile fallbacks). Performance is clean — 0 lagged frames in every log but one (1 frame, 0.0%), graphics thread 99.96% under 16.67 ms, NVENC submit median 0.2 ms. So there's no perf fire. The problems are routing gaps and a few settings that aren't doing what the file says.

## Real bugs — fix these first

**1. Minecraft and The Finals have no game audio on stream.**
`Desktop Audio` is routed to **zero tracks** (`DesktopAudioDevice1` mixers=192 → no track bits set), so all game audio depends on your per-process captures. But `AUD - Game` targets only `::VALORANT-Win64-Shipping.exe`. There is no audio source for `javaw.exe` (Minecraft) or `Discovery.exe` (The Finals), and none for `CAP - Any Game`. Scene `2 - Minecraft` includes `AUD - Game`, which will stay silent.

Fix: duplicate `AUD - Game` into `AUD - Game (Minecraft)` → `javaw.exe` and `AUD - Game (The Finals)` → `Discovery.exe`, both on tracks 1, 2, 3 (mixers 199) to match. Then add `Desktop Audio` back to scenes on tracks 1+2+3 but **muted** — a one-click rescue when a new game isn't covered, with no double-audio risk while it's muted.

**2. Your recording encoder isn't using the preset you set.**
`recordEncoder.json` has `"preset2": "p6"`, but the log for that same session shows `preset: p5`. Every other key from that file applied correctly (cqp 18, multipass qres, profile main, bf 2, lookahead false) — only the preset didn't, so the stored value isn't being read. Open Settings → Output → Recording, set the preset in the UI, and confirm the next log says `preset: p6`.

**3. The `Archive - High Quality` and `Stream Only - No Record` profiles stream 12000 kbps CBR H.264 to plain Twitch RTMP.** Enhanced Broadcasting is off (`EnableMultitrackVideo=false`), so Twitch will accept the connection and then you'll bleed dropped frames and get no transcodes. Either drop those to 8000 or enable Enhanced Broadcasting on them.

## Worthwhile quality wins

**4. Your stream encoder is leaving quality on the table, for free.** `gpu_encode_frame` median is 0.2 ms against a 16.67 ms budget — the 4060 Ti's NVENC is ~99% idle. In `streamEncoder.json`, change `"multipass": "qres"` → `"fullres"` and `"preset2": "p6"` → `"p7"`. At 6000 kbps on Valorant that's a visible reduction in smearing during fast flicks, at effectively no cost on this card.

**5. Enable Twitch Enhanced Broadcasting.** This is the single biggest image-quality lever available to you: it unlocks multiple encodes and higher aggregate bitrate well past the 6000 kbps you're capped at now (`IgnoreRecommended=true`, `EnableMultitrackVideo=false`). Your card does AV1 (`AV1 supported: true`) and has the headroom. Main cost is upload bandwidth — worth a test stream before committing.

**6. You have NVIDIA Audio Effects 1.6.1.2 installed and loaded, but unused.** Your mic chain is Noise Suppression (RNNoise/Speex) → Gate → EQ → Compressor → Limiter. Swapping the first filter for NVIDIA Noise Removal is a clear step up on a Brio 100's mediocre mic, and your GPU can absorb it.

**7. `ProcessPriority=Normal`, and OBS isn't running as admin** — the log shows `D3D11 GPU priority setup failed (not admin?)`. Setting priority to Above Normal and running OBS elevated is the standard fix for frame drops when a game saturates the GPU. You have no drops today, so this is insurance, not a cure.

**8. No stream delay** (`DelayEnable=false`, `DelaySec=20` preconfigured). For ranked Valorant, a 20 s delay is cheap anti-stream-sniping. Your call — it hurts chat interaction.

## Smaller things

- **StreamElements alert widget is partly broken**: the log shows a blocked script (`widgets.streamelements.com/z/i.js` served as `text/html`) plus a CSP violation. You also run `ALR - Gotham` alongside it in every scene and in the `DSK - Alerts` downstream keyer. Verify SE alerts actually fire; if Gotham covers it, drop the SE source.
- **`input-overlay` server listens on `0.0.0.0:1608`** and obs-websocket on all interfaces at 4456. Websocket auth is on (`auth_required: true`) — good. Bind both to 127.0.0.1 unless you drive them from another machine.
- **Old stream keys on disk**: `service.json.bak-prekey` exists in three profiles. If you rotated your key, delete those; they hold the old one in plaintext.
- **Clutter**: `Main.json.pre-bgr-removal`, `.pre-cam-patch`, `.pre-se-disable`, `.renumber-bak-*`, `ZZ_*_Maintenance.json.bak`, `user.ini.bak-predock-*`. Harmless but it's ~8 stale copies of a 250 KB scene file.
- **Up to 6 concurrent NVENC sessions possible**: stream H.264 + replay-buffer HEVC + four source-record HEVC filters. Only the running game's capture is active, so in practice it's 3 — fine, just don't add more.
- **Clean-recording filters specify no rate control**, so they run at source-record's defaults rather than your CQP 18. Set them explicitly if those files matter.
- **Webcam is 30 fps on a 60 fps canvas** — the Brio 100 caps at 1080p30, so this isn't fixable, just expect slight cam judder.
- **Default transition is Move at 1000 ms** — long enough to feel sluggish between scenes; 300–400 ms reads better.
- Cosmetic: leftover `FPSInt=30`/`FPSNum=30` in `basic.ini` (unused, `FPSCommon=60` is active) and `FirstRun=true` in `user.ini`.

One thing I'd call out as a deliberate good decision, so you don't "fix" it: music is on tracks 1 and 6 only, excluded from the VOD track 2. Keep that. Note it *is* in track 1, which is the mixed track in your recordings — if you re-upload those, pull the mix from tracks 3/4/5 instead.

