    "content": "{\n  \"lesson\": 1,\n  \"author\": \"claude-opus-5 (Teacher)\",\n  \"domain\": \"Agentic Python tooling for code-intelligence systems\",\n  \"notes\": \"Batch 1 of 25. Laddered easy->hard across 7 skills. Every example carries an explicit reasoning trace in `thinking`; the Student is trained to produce that trace before answering.\",\n  \"examples\": [\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"tool_call_format\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"easy\",\n      \"instruction\": \"You have a tool `read_file` with schema {\\\"path\\\": string (required), \\\"start_line\\\": integer (optional), \\\"end_line\\\": integer (optional)}. Read lines 40-80 of src/repo2graph/cli.py. Emit only the tool call.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The user wants a single tool call, nothing else.\\n\\n1. Tool name: `read_file` - use it verbatim, no pluralisation or casing changes.\\n2. Required param: `path`. The path given is src/repo2graph/cli.py - pass it as-is, do not absolutise it since the schema says nothing about absolute paths.\\n3. Optional params: start_line=40, end_line=80. Both are integers, so they must be bare numbers, not strings.\\n4. The instruction says \\\"emit only the tool call\\\" - so no explanation before or after. Prose around a tool call is the most common way a small model breaks an agent loop, because the harness tries to parse the whole message.\\n\\nI will not invent fields like `encoding` or `limit` that the schema does not declare.\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"read_file\\\",\\n  \\\"input\\\": {\\n    \\\"path\\\": \\\"src/repo2graph/cli.py\\\",\\n    \\\"start_line\\\": 40,\\n    \\\"end_line\\\": 80\\n  }\\n}\\n```\",\n      \"tags\": [\"mcp\", \"schema\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"tool_call_format\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"easy\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Tool `grep_code` schema: {\\\"pattern\\\": string (required), \\\"glob\\\": string (optional), \\\"case_sensitive\\\": boolean (optional, default true)}. Find every call to `get_parser` in Python files, case-insensitively. Emit only the tool call.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Mapping the request onto the schema:\\n\\n1. `pattern` - they want calls to get_parser. A literal substring `get_parser(` is more precise than a bare name, since it excludes imports and string mentions. The schema does not say the pattern is regex, so I keep it a plain literal and avoid regex metacharacters that might be interpreted. A bare `(` is not a metacharacter problem in a literal, but if it were treated as regex it would be an unclosed group - so I escape conservatively by just using `get_parser` plus the glob filter, and note nothing extra. Actually precision matters more: I will use `get_parser(` and accept it, because if the pattern is regex the unescaped `(` would error. Safer: use `get_parser` and let the caller refine. I will pass `get_parser(` escaped as `get_parser\\\\\\\\(` only if regex is declared - it is not declared, so a literal is the correct reading.\\n2. `glob` - Python files only: `**/*.py`.\\n3. `case_sensitive` - the request says case-insensitively, so pass false explicitly. The default is true, so omitting it would be wrong.\\n\\nI pass a boolean false, not the string \\\"false\\\".\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"grep_code\\\",\\n  \\\"input\\\": {\\n    \\\"pattern\\\": \\\"get_parser\\\",\\n    \\\"glob\\\": \\\"**/*.py\\\",\\n    \\\"case_sensitive\\\": false\\n  }\\n}\\n```\",\n      \"tags\": [\"mcp\", \"schema\", \"booleans\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"tool_call_format\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Tool `query_graph` schema: {\\\"symbol\\\": string (required), \\\"direction\\\": enum[\\\"callers\\\",\\\"callees\\\",\\\"both\\\"] (required), \\\"max_depth\\\": integer 1-5 (optional, default 2), \\\"budget_tokens\\\": integer (optional)}. Find everything that calls `build_index`, three levels deep, capped at 4000 tokens. Emit only the tool call.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Schema has an enum and a bounded integer, so both need checking.\\n\\n1. `symbol`: \\\"build_index\\\".\\n2. `direction`: they want what *calls* build_index, i.e. inbound edges, which is \\\"callers\\\". Not \\\"callees\\\" (that would be what build_index itself calls) and not \\\"both\\\". Choosing the wrong enum member here is a silent semantic error - the call succeeds and returns the wrong graph.\\n3. `max_depth`: 3. Valid, since the range is 1-5. If they had asked for 8 I would have to clamp to 5 and say so.\\n4. `budget_tokens`: 4000.\\n\\nAll four fields are declared, so no invented keys. Enum value must be the exact lowercase string from the schema.\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"query_graph\\\",\\n  \\\"input\\\": {\\n    \\\"symbol\\\": \\\"build_index\\\",\\n    \\\"direction\\\": \\\"callers\\\",\\n    \\\"max_depth\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"budget_tokens\\\": 4000\\n  }\\n}\\n```\",\n      \"tags\": [\"mcp\", \"enum\", \"bounds\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"tool_call_format\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Tool `edit_file` schema: {\\\"path\\\": string (required), \\\"old_string\\\": string (required), \\\"new_string\\\": string (required), \\\"replace_all\\\": boolean (optional, default false)}. In config.py, change the timeout from 30 to 120 everywhere it appears as `timeout=30`. Emit only the tool call.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Exact-match replacement, so the strings must be byte-exact.\\n\\n1. `path`: config.py.\\n2. `old_string`: `timeout=30`. I must not add spaces around the `=` - the user wrote `timeout=30` and an exact matcher would fail on `timeout = 30`.\\n3. `new_string`: `timeout=120`, preserving the same spacing style.\\n4. `replace_all`: the user said \\\"everywhere it appears\\\", so true. This matters: with the default false, an exact-match editor typically errors when the string occurs more than once, so omitting it would make the call fail rather than do half the job.\\n\\nOne risk worth noting mentally: `timeout=30` is also a prefix of `timeout=300`, so a naive replace would corrupt `timeout=300` into `timeout=1200`. The schema gives me no word-boundary option, so the call is still correct as specified, but that is the kind of thing to verify after editing.\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"edit_file\\\",\\n  \\\"input\\\": {\\n    \\\"path\\\": \\\"config.py\\\",\\n    \\\"old_string\\\": \\\"timeout=30\\\",\\n    \\\"new_string\\\": \\\"timeout=120\\\",\\n    \\\"replace_all\\\": true\\n  }\\n}\\n```\",\n      \"tags\": [\"mcp\", \"exact-match\", \"replace_all\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"tool_call_format\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Tool `rag_answer` schema: {\\\"question\\\": string (required), \\\"repo\\\": string (required), \\\"cite\\\": boolean (optional, default true), \\\"budget_tokens\\\": integer 500-16000 (optional, default 4000), \\\"allow_network\\\": boolean (optional, default false)}. A user asks: \\\"summarise how caching works in repo2graph, with citations, and keep it cheap - I'm on a metered connection\\\". Emit the tool call and one sentence explaining your parameter choices.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"This one has a trap: \\\"metered connection\\\" is about network, \\\"keep it cheap\\\" is about tokens. They are two different parameters and I need to not conflate them.\\n\\n1. `question`: restate it cleanly as \\\"How does caching work in repo2graph?\\\"\\n2. `repo`: \\\"repo2graph\\\".\\n3. `cite`: they explicitly asked for citations, so true. It is also the default, but passing it explicitly is harmless and makes the intent legible.\\n4. `budget_tokens`: \\\"keep it cheap\\\" means lower than the 4000 default. 1500 is a reasonable frugal value and is inside the 500-16000 range.\\n5. `allow_network`: \\\"metered connection\\\" means do NOT fetch over the network. The default is already false, so the safe thing is to leave it false - and here passing it explicitly is worth it, because it is the parameter the user's constraint actually targets.\\n\\nThe request also permits one sentence of explanation, so unlike the earlier examples prose is allowed here. I must still keep the JSON block clean and separate.\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"rag_answer\\\",\\n  \\\"input\\\": {\\n    \\\"question\\\": \\\"How does caching work in repo2graph?\\\",\\n    \\\"repo\\\": \\\"repo2graph\\\",\\n    \\\"cite\\\": true,\\n    \\\"budget_tokens\\\": 1500,\\n    \\\"allow_network\\\": false\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nI set `budget_tokens` to 1500 (below the 4000 default) to honour \\\"keep it cheap\\\", and pinned `allow_network` to false because \\\"metered connection\\\" is a network constraint rather than a token one.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"mcp\", \"ambiguity\", \"defaults\"]\n    },\n\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"python_stack\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"easy\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Review this function and fix what's wrong:\\n\\n```python\\ndef load_config(path):\\n    f = open(path)\\n    data = json.load(f)\\n    return data\\n```\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Four defects, in rough order of severity:\\n\\n1. The file handle is never closed. On CPython refcounting usually saves you, but it is not guaranteed and leaks under PyPy or on exceptions. Use a context manager.\\n2. No explicit encoding. On Windows `open()` defaults to the locale codepage (cp1252 here), so a UTF-8 config with non-ASCII content decodes wrongly or raises. This is a real cross-platform bug, not a style nit - and this project targets Windows and Linux both.\\n3. No type hints, and the project supports 3.11+ so modern syntax is available.\\n4. `path` as a bare string is weaker than accepting a path-like; `Path | str` is the idiomatic signature.\\n\\nI will not add exception handling: swallowing JSONDecodeError here would hide a malformed config, and the caller is better placed to decide. Saying that explicitly is part of the review.\",\n      \"output\": \"```python\\nimport json\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\nfrom typing import Any\\n\\n\\ndef load_config(path: Path | str) -> dict[str, Any]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Load a JSON config.\\n\\n    The encoding is explicit because Python's default is the locale codepage on\\n    Windows, which silently mangles non-ASCII config values.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return json.loads(Path(path).read_text(encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\"))\\n```\\n\\nWhat changed and why:\\n\\n1. **Leaked handle** - the original never closed the file. `Path.read_text()` opens and closes in one call, so there is nothing to leak.\\n2. **Implicit encoding** - added `encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\"`. Without it, the same config file parses differently on Windows and Linux.\\n3. **Type hints** - `Path | str` in, `dict[str, Any]` out, using 3.10+ union syntax.\\n4. **Deliberately no try/except** - a malformed config should raise `JSONDecodeError` loudly rather than be swallowed into a default here.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"review\", \"encoding\", \"pathlib\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"python_stack\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"easy\",\n      \"instruction\": \"This helper is slow and memory-hungry on large repos. Improve it:\\n\\n```python\\ndef find_py_files(root):\\n    results = []\\n    for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(root):\\n        for fn in filenames:\\n            if fn.endswith('.py'):\\n                results.append(os.path.join(dirpath, fn))\\n    return results\\n```\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Two separate problems.\\n\\nMemory: it materialises every path into a list before the caller sees one. On a large monorepo that is hundreds of thousands of strings held at once. A generator streams them instead, so the caller can process incrementally or stop early.\\n\\nSpeed: the real cost on a repo is almost never the `.py` check, it is descending into `.git`, `node_modules`, `.venv` and `__pycache__`. `os.walk` lets you prune by mutating `dirnames` in place, which stops recursion into those trees entirely. That is the change that actually matters - repo2graph would otherwise walk 471 `.pyc` files' worth of `__pycache__` for nothing.\\n\\nI will use `pathlib.Path` for the yielded values since the rest of a modern codebase expects Paths, and `Iterator[Path]` as the return type. Mutating `dirnames` requires the slice-assignment idiom `dirnames[:] = ...`; rebinding the name would not affect the walk.\",\n      \"output\": \"```python\\nimport os\\nfrom collections.abc import Iterator\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\n\\n# Pruned at the directory level, so os.walk never descends into them.\\n_SKIP_DIRS = frozenset({\\\".git\\\", \\\".venv\\\", \\\"venv\\\", \\\"node_modules\\\", \\\"__pycache__\\\", \\\".mypy_cache\\\"})\\n\\n\\ndef find_py_files(root: Path | str) -> Iterator[Path]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Yield every .py file under `root`, skipping vendored and cache trees.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(root):\\n        # Slice assignment is required: rebinding `dirnames` would not prune\\n        # the walk, because os.walk reads the list it handed us.\\n        dirnames[:] = [d for d in dirnames if d not in _SKIP_DIRS and not d.startswith(\\\".\\\")]\\n        base = Path(dirpath)\\n        for fn in filenames:\\n            if fn.endswith(\\\".py\\\"):\\n                yield base / fn\\n```\\n\\nTwo independent wins:\\n\\n1. **Streaming** - returning an `Iterator` means the caller never holds every path at once, and can stop early.\\n2. **Pruning** - skipping `.git`, `.venv`, `node_modules` and `__pycache__` at the directory level is where the actual time goes. This is far more impactful than micro-optimising the extension test.\\n\\nNote `dirnames[:] = ...` rather than `dirnames = ...` - only in-place mutation affects the traversal.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"generators\", \"os.walk\", \"performance\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"python_stack\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Why is this dangerous, and what should it be?\\n\\n```python\\ntry:\\n    result = parse_source(path)\\nexcept:\\n    result = None\\n```\",\n      \"thinking\": \"A bare `except:` catches `BaseException`, not just `Exception`. That means it swallows:\\n\\n- `KeyboardInterrupt` - Ctrl-C stops working, which is infuriating in a CLI that walks thousands of files.\\n- `SystemExit` - `sys.exit()` inside the call gets eaten.\\n- `MemoryError`, `GeneratorExit` - states you cannot meaningfully continue from.\\n\\nBeyond the class being too wide, assigning `None` destroys the diagnostic. In a tree-sitter parsing pipeline you genuinely do want to continue past one unparseable file, so the intent is defensible - but you need to know *which* file failed and why, or you ship a tool that silently under-reports the graph.\\n\\nSo the fix has three parts: narrow the exception class to what parse_source can actually raise, keep the control flow, and log enough to debug. I should also mention that `logger.exception` captures the traceback, which `logger.error` does not.\",\n      \"output\": \"```python\\nimport logging\\n\\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\\n\\ntry:\\n    result = parse_source(path)\\nexcept (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, SyntaxError) as e:\\n    # Skipping one unparseable file is intended; silently losing it is not.\\n    logger.warning(\\\"could not parse %s: %s: %s\\\", path, type(e).__name__, e)\\n    result = None\\n```\\n\\nThree problems with the original:\\n\\n1. **`except:` catches `BaseException`** - that includes `KeyboardInterrupt` and `SystemExit`. Ctrl-C stops working, which is a serious usability bug in a CLI that walks a whole repo.\\n2. **The error is destroyed** - `result = None` keeps no record. A parser that silently skips files produces an incomplete graph that looks complete, which is worse than crashing.\\n3. **No signal about which file** - with thousands of files you cannot reproduce the failure.\\n\\nIf you want the traceback too, use `logger.exception(...)` instead of `logger.warning(...)`; `logger.error` alone does not include it. And catch only what `parse_source` can actually raise - if it can raise something else, that is a bug worth seeing, not worth hiding.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"exceptions\", \"logging\", \"review\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"python_stack\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Write a `SymbolRef` type for a code graph: a file path, a symbol name, a 1-indexed line, and an optional end line. It must be hashable so it can go in a set, immutable, and reject a line number below 1.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Requirements map cleanly onto a frozen dataclass:\\n\\n- hashable + immutable -> `@dataclass(frozen=True)`, which synthesises `__hash__` and blocks attribute assignment.\\n- `slots=True` is worth adding (3.10+): these objects appear by the hundred-thousand in a code graph, and slots cuts per-instance memory noticeably by dropping `__dict__`.\\n- validation -> `__post_init__`. With `frozen=True` I cannot assign in `__post_init__`, but I only need to *raise*, not mutate, so that is fine.\\n- `end_line` optional -> `int | None = None`. If present it must not precede `line`, which is worth checking too since an inverted range is a real bug source in span highlighting.\\n- 1-indexed is the kind of thing that gets confused constantly, so it belongs in the docstring and in the error message.\\n\\nI will use `ValueError` rather than `assert` - asserts vanish under `python -O`, so they are the wrong tool for validating data.\",\n      \"output\": \"```python\\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\n\\n\\n@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)\\nclass SymbolRef:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"A reference to a named symbol at a location in a file.\\n\\n    Lines are **1-indexed**, matching what editors and tree-sitter report.\\n    Frozen so it can be used as a dict key or set member; slotted because a\\n    code graph holds these by the hundred-thousand.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n    path: Path\\n    name: str\\n    line: int\\n    end_line: int | None = None\\n\\n    def __post_init__(self) -> None:\\n        if self.line < 1:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"line is 1-indexed, got {self.line} for {self.name!r}\\\")\\n        if self.end_line is not None and self.end_line < self.line:\\n            raise ValueError(\\n                f\\\"end_line {self.end_line} precedes line {self.line} for {self.name!r}\\\"\\n            )\\n\\n    @property\\n    def span(self) -> tuple[int, int]:\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Inclusive (start, end) line span; a single line if end_line is unset.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n        return self.line, self.end_line if self.end_line is not None else self.line\\n```\\n\\nDesign notes:\\n\\n1. **`frozen=True`** gives both requirements at once - immutability and a synthesised `__hash__`, so instances work in sets and as dict keys.\\n2. **`slots=True`** drops the per-instance `__dict__`. At code-graph scale that is a material memory saving, not a micro-optimisation.\\n3. **`ValueError`, not `assert`** - assertions are stripped under `python -O`, so they must never guard data validity.\\n4. **Inverted spans rejected too** - an `end_line` before `line` is a bug that otherwise surfaces much later as a broken highlight.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"dataclass\", \"validation\", \"hashable\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"python_stack\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"This cache looks fine but has a subtle correctness bug on a long-running MCP server. Find it:\\n\\n```python\\nfrom functools import lru_cache\\n\\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\\ndef parse_file(path: Path) -> Tree:\\n    return parser.parse(path.read_bytes())\\n```\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Two bugs, and the second is the one being asked about.\\n\\nFirst, `maxsize=None` means the cache grows without bound. On a long-running MCP server indexing large repos, every parsed Tree is retained forever. Tree-sitter Trees hold the source bytes, so this is a slow memory leak measured in hundreds of megabytes.\\n\\nSecond, and this is the correctness bug: the cache key is the `Path` only. `Path` hashes by its string value, so if the file *changes on disk*, the key is unchanged and the stale Tree is returned forever. On an MCP server serving a developer's working tree, files change constantly - that is the whole point. So the server would serve answers about code that no longer exists, with citations pointing at line numbers that have moved.\\n\\nThe fix is to include a staleness token in the key: mtime_ns plus size is the cheap standard choice. You cannot do that with lru_cache on the Path alone, so the shape has to change - an inner cached function keyed on (path, mtime_ns, size) and an outer function that stats the file. Stat is a single syscall, far cheaper than reparsing.\\n\\nAlso worth flagging: an unbounded cache keyed on mtime is *worse* than one keyed on path, because every edit adds a new entry and never evicts the old one. So bounding maxsize is not optional once the key includes mtime.\",\n      \"output\": \"The headline bug is **stale cache entries**: the key is the path alone, so when the file changes on disk the cached `Tree` is returned forever.\\n\\nOn an MCP server that is serving a live working tree, that is the common case, not an edge case - the server answers questions about code that no longer exists, and the citations point at line numbers that have since moved.\\n\\n```python\\nimport os\\nfrom functools import lru_cache\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\n\\n\\n# Bounded, and keyed on a staleness token rather than the path alone.\\n@lru_cache(maxsize=512)\\ndef _parse_cached(path: Path, mtime_ns: int, size: int) -> Tree:\\n    return parser.parse(path.read_bytes())\\n\\n\\ndef parse_file(path: Path) -> Tree:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Parse `path`, reusing a cached Tree only if the file is unchanged.\\n\\n    The cache key includes mtime and size, so an edit on disk produces a new\\n    key and forces a reparse. One stat() per call is far cheaper than the\\n    reparse it avoids.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    st = os.stat(path)\\n    return _parse_cached(path, st.st_mtime_ns, st.st_size)\\n```\\n\\nTwo defects, both load-bearing:\\n\\n1. **Stale results** - `Path` hashes by its string, so an edited file keeps its key. Including `st_mtime_ns` and `st_size` makes an edit produce a different key. Size is included because some filesystems have coarse mtime granularity, so a fast successive write can land in the same timestamp.\\n2. **Unbounded growth** - `maxsize=None` never evicts, and tree-sitter `Tree` objects retain their source bytes. Once the key includes mtime this gets *worse*, since every edit adds an entry, so bounding `maxsize` becomes mandatory rather than merely tidy.\\n\\nIf the server must also notice changes made by other processes mid-request, a stat-based key is still the right primitive - you would add a filesystem watcher to invalidate proactively, not change the key.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"caching\", \"correctness\", \"mcp\"]\n    },\n\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"static_analysis\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Write a tree-sitter query that captures every Python function definition's name, and explain how to also catch methods inside classes.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"In the Python grammar, a function is a `function_definition` node whose name is an `identifier` child under the `name` field. The query syntax uses `(node_type field: (child) @capture)`.\\n\\nThe key insight for the second half: tree-sitter queries match anywhere in the tree by default, they are not anchored to the root. A method inside a class is still a `function_definition` node - just nested under `class_definition` -> `block`. So the same query already catches methods. That surprises people who expect to need a separate pattern.\\n\\nIf you want to *distinguish* them, you anchor the pattern by writing the enclosing context explicitly. And to tell a method from a plain function at the capture site, you capture the class name too.\\n\\nI should also mention `async def`, which is still a `function_definition` in the Python grammar (with an `async` child), so it is covered - that is a real gotcha worth stating since some grammars use a distinct node type.\",\n      \"output\": \"```scheme\\n(function_definition\\n  name: (identifier) @function.name)\\n```\\n\\n**Methods are already included.** Tree-sitter patterns match at any depth, not just at the root, and a method is still a `function_definition` node - it simply sits under `class_definition` \u2192 `block`. So the query above captures module-level functions, methods, and nested closures alike.\\n\\nTo *distinguish* methods, anchor the pattern to its enclosing class:\\n\\n```scheme\\n(class_definition\\n  name: (identifier) @class.name\\n  body: (block\\n    (function_definition\\n      name: (identifier) @method.name)))\\n```\\n\\nThree things worth knowing:\\n\\n1. **`async def` is covered.** In the Python grammar an async function is still a `function_definition` (carrying an `async` child), not a separate node type. Some grammars do split this, so it is worth checking per language.\\n2. **Nested functions match too.** If you only want top-level definitions, anchor on `(module (function_definition ...))`.\\n3. **Decorated functions nest one level deeper.** A decorated definition is wrapped in `decorated_definition`, so if you anchor to `module` you will miss decorated functions unless you also match `(module (decorated_definition (function_definition ...)))`.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"tree-sitter\", \"queries\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"static_analysis\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"I'm walking a tree-sitter tree recursively and it's slow, and it misses some nodes. What's the standard way to traverse correctly?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Two issues to separate.\\n\\nMissing nodes: if they are iterating `node.children`, they get only *named* children plus anonymous ones depending on the API, but the common mistake is using `named_children` and losing punctuation/operator nodes, or conversely walking `children` and being surprised by anonymous tokens. The deeper usual cause of \\\"misses some nodes\\\" is recursing only into the first child, or forgetting that a cursor-based walk needs the goto_next_sibling loop.\\n\\nSlowness: creating Python-level objects for every node is the cost. Tree-sitter provides `TreeCursor`, which walks without materialising a Python object per node. That is the intended API for full traversals and it is substantially faster.\\n\\nThe canonical cursor walk is a specific loop shape that people get wrong: descend with goto_first_child; when that fails, goto_next_sibling; when that fails, goto_parent and retry sibling, terminating when you return to the start depth. I should write that out correctly, because the subtle part is the inner while loop that unwinds multiple levels.\\n\\nAlso worth noting: if they only want specific node types, a query is faster than any manual walk, because it is executed in C.\",\n      \"output\": \"Use a `TreeCursor`, not recursion over `node.children`. The cursor walks the tree without allocating a Python object per node, which is where the time goes in a manual walk.\\n\\n```python\\nfrom collections.abc import Iterator\\n\\n\\ndef walk(tree) -> Iterator:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Yield every node in document order using a cursor.\\n\\n    The unwind loop is the part that is easy to get wrong: when a node has no\\n    next sibling we must climb parents until one does, and stop when we climb\\n    back out of the subtree we started in.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    cursor = tree.walk()\\n    while True:\\n        yield cursor.node\\n\\n        if cursor.goto_first_child():\\n            continue\\n        if cursor.goto_next_sibling():\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Dead end: climb until a parent has an unvisited sibling.\\n        while True:\\n            if not cursor.goto_parent():\\n                return                      # back at the root; done\\n            if cursor.goto_next_sibling():\\n                break\\n```\\n\\nOn the missed nodes - two likely causes:\\n\\n1. **`named_children` vs `children`.** `children` includes anonymous nodes (punctuation, operators, keywords); `named_children` does not. If you were filtering to named children you legitimately lost tokens like `=` and `:`.\\n2. **A recursion that never unwinds more than one level.** The nested `while` above is the fix: after a dead end you may need to climb several parents before finding a sibling, and a single `goto_parent()` silently truncates whole subtrees.\\n\\nAnd if you only need particular node types, do not walk at all - run a query. Query execution happens in C, so it beats any Python-level traversal:\\n\\n```python\\nquery = language.query(\\\"(function_definition name: (identifier) @name)\\\")\\nfor node, capture in query.captures(tree.root_node):\\n    ...\\n```\",\n      \"tags\": [\"tree-sitter\", \"traversal\", \"performance\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"static_analysis\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"In a code graph, how do you resolve `from .utils import helper` to the actual definition, and what cases break naive resolution?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Resolution has a mechanical part and a hard part.\\n\\nMechanical: a relative import's leading dots count levels up from the *current module's package*, not the filesystem cwd. One dot = current package, two dots = parent. So for module `pkg/sub/mod.py`, `from .utils import helper` resolves to `pkg/sub/utils.py`, and `from ..utils import` to `pkg/utils.py`. Then you look for a top-level binding named `helper` in that module.\n\nThen `.utils` might be a package rather than a module, in which case it is `pkg/sub/utils/__init__.py`.\n\nThe hard cases are where naive resolution silently gives wrong edges:\n\n1. Re-exports: `utils/__init__.py` does `from .core import helper`. The definition is in core.py, not utils. You have to follow the chain, and chains can be several deep or circular.\n2. `import *` - makes the binding set dynamic and dependent on `__all__`.\n3. Conditional imports under `if TYPE_CHECKING:` or try/except ImportError - two different definitions for the same name.\n4. Shadowing: a local assignment `helper = something` after the import rebinds the name.\n5. Namespace packages (PEP 420) with no `__init__.py`, so directory existence is not enough signal.\n6. `__all__` controlling what the re-export surface actually is.\n7. Aliasing: `from .utils import helper as h` - the graph edge should be keyed on the local name but point at the original definition.\n\nFor a code graph the honest engineering answer is: resolve statically, record confidence, and do not pretend dynamic cases are resolved. Emitting a low-confidence or unresolved edge is better than a confidently wrong one, because downstream citations inherit the error.\",\n      \"output\": \"**The mechanical part.** Leading dots count packages up from the importing module's own package, not from the working directory:\\n\\n```\\npkg/sub/mod.py:  from .utils  import helper   ->  pkg/sub/utils.py\\npkg/sub/mod.py:  from ..utils import helper   ->  pkg/utils.py\\n```\\n\\nThen `.utils` resolves to either `utils.py` or `utils/__init__.py`, and you look for a module-level binding named `helper` there.\\n\\n**The cases that break naive resolution** \u2014 each one produces a confidently wrong edge, which is worse than no edge because citations inherit the mistake:\\n\\n| Case | Why it breaks |\\n|---|---|\\n| **Re-export** | `utils/__init__.py` does `from .core import helper`. The real definition is in `core.py`. Chains can be several hops deep, and can be circular. |\\n| **`import *`** | The binding set is determined by `__all__` (or by every public name if absent), so it is not statically obvious. |\\n| **`if TYPE_CHECKING:`** | Two different definitions for one name \u2014 one for type checkers, one at runtime. |\\n| **`try/except ImportError`** | Optional-dependency fallbacks give the same name two possible origins. |\\n| **Shadowing** | A later `helper = ...` or a parameter named `helper` rebinds the name; the import is then not the definition in that scope. |\\n| **Namespace packages (PEP 420)** | No `__init__.py`, so \\\"directory exists\\\" is not sufficient evidence of a package. |\\n| **Aliasing** | `import helper as h` \u2014 the edge must key on the local name `h` but point at `helper`'s definition. |\\n\\n**What to actually build.** Resolve statically, follow re-export chains with a visited-set to survive cycles, and attach a confidence to every edge:\\n\\n- `resolved` \u2014 found a unique module-level definition.\\n- `ambiguous` \u2014 several candidates (conditional imports, `import *`).\\n- `unresolved` \u2014 dynamic, or the target is outside the indexed tree.\\n\\nDo not collapse the last two into a guess. For a system whose whole promise is *cited* answers, an unresolved edge the user can see is honest; a fabricated one quietly corrupts every answer built on it.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"imports\", \"resolution\", \"code-graph\"]\n    },\n\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"structured_extraction\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"easy\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Extract to JSON with exactly these keys: tool, version, exit_code, duration_seconds, files_indexed. Use null for anything absent. Output only the JSON.\\n\\nLog:\\nrepo2graph v4.1.0 starting\\nindexing /home/srini/work/api\\nindexed 1,284 files in 12.4s\\ndone (exit 0)\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Walk the required keys one at a time against the log.\\n\\n- tool: \\\"repo2graph\\\" - the name without the version.\\n- version: \\\"4.1.0\\\". The log says \\\"v4.1.0\\\"; I strip the `v` prefix since the key is `version` and the value is the version number. Keeping \\\"v4.1.0\\\" would be defensible but less canonical.\\n- exit_code: 0. It is an integer, so a bare 0, not \\\"0\\\". This is the field most often wrongly stringified.\\n- duration_seconds: 12.4 - a float, from \\\"12.4s\\\".\\n- files_indexed: 1284. The log writes \\\"1,284\\\" with a thousands separator; JSON numbers cannot contain commas, so it must be 1284. Emitting 1,284 would be a syntax error and \\\"1,284\\\" would be the wrong type.\\n\\nNothing is absent, so no nulls needed. The instruction says JSON only, so no commentary and no code-fence prose around it. Keys in the order given.\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"tool\\\": \\\"repo2graph\\\",\\n  \\\"version\\\": \\\"4.1.0\\\",\\n  \\\"exit_code\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"duration_seconds\\\": 12.4,\\n  \\\"files_indexed\\\": 1284\\n}\\n```\",\n      \"tags\": [\"extraction\", \"types\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"structured_extraction\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Extract to JSON with exactly these keys: package, installed, required, satisfied. `satisfied` is a boolean. Output only the JSON array, one object per line of input.\\n\\ntree-sitter 0.23.1 (need >=0.23)\\ntree-sitter-language-pack 1.2.0 (need >=0.7,<1.0)\\npyyaml (not installed, need >=6.0)\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Three rows, one object each, and the third row is the interesting one.\\n\\nRow 1: tree-sitter, installed 0.23.1, required \\\">=0.23\\\". Is 0.23.1 >= 0.23? Yes. satisfied true.\\n\\nRow 2: tree-sitter-language-pack, installed 1.2.0, required \\\">=0.7,<1.0\\\". 1.2.0 satisfies >=0.7 but violates <1.0. So satisfied is false. This is the trap - a careless reading sees \\\">=0.7\\\" and says true. Both clauses of the specifier must hold.\\n\\nRow 3: pyyaml is not installed. `installed` must be null, not \\\"\\\" and not \\\"not installed\\\". And satisfied must be false - an absent package cannot satisfy a requirement. Some would argue null for satisfied, but the schema says satisfied is a boolean, so false is the correct typed answer.\\n\\nOutput is an array, objects in input order, no commentary.\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n[\\n  {\\n    \\\"package\\\": \\\"tree-sitter\\\",\\n    \\\"installed\\\": \\\"0.23.1\\\",\\n    \\\"required\\\": \\\">=0.23\\\",\\n    \\\"satisfied\\\": true\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"package\\\": \\\"tree-sitter-language-pack\\\",\\n    \\\"installed\\\": \\\"1.2.0\\\",\\n    \\\"required\\\": \\\">=0.7,<1.0\\\",\\n    \\\"satisfied\\\": false\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"package\\\": \\\"pyyaml\\\",\\n    \\\"installed\\\": null,\\n    \\\"required\\\": \\\">=6.0\\\",\\n    \\\"satisfied\\\": false\\n  }\\n]\\n```\",\n      \"tags\": [\"extraction\", \"version-specifiers\", \"nulls\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"structured_extraction\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Extract to JSON: {\\\"findings\\\": [{\\\"severity\\\": one of \\\"high\\\"|\\\"medium\\\"|\\\"low\\\", \\\"file\\\": string, \\\"line\\\": integer, \\\"issue\\\": string}], \\\"summary\\\": {\\\"high\\\": int, \\\"medium\\\": int, \\\"low\\\": int}}. Output only JSON.\\n\\nAudit output:\\nsrc/parser.py:88 - WARNING bare except swallows KeyboardInterrupt\\nsrc/cache.py:12 - CRITICAL unbounded lru_cache on long-running server\\nsrc/io.py:45 - WARNING open() without explicit encoding\\nsrc/cli.py:203 - NOTE consider a generator here\\nsrc/parser.py:88 - WARNING bare except swallows KeyboardInterrupt\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Two mapping decisions and one dedup decision.\\n\\nSeverity mapping: the log uses CRITICAL/WARNING/NOTE but the schema enum is high/medium/low. So CRITICAL->high, WARNING->medium, NOTE->low. I must emit the enum values, not the source labels - emitting \\\"WARNING\\\" would violate the schema.\\n\\nDeduplication: line 1 and line 5 are byte-identical (src/parser.py:88, same issue). Should the array have it twice? The schema gives a findings list, and a duplicate finding is one finding reported twice by the tool. Including it twice would double-count in the summary and misrepresent the audit. I will emit it once and make the summary consistent with the array - internal consistency between `findings` and `summary` matters more than faithfully echoing a repeated line, and a summary that disagrees with its own array is clearly wrong.\\n\\nSo: 1 high (cache.py), 2 medium (parser.py, io.py), 1 low (cli.py). Total 4 findings, summary sums to 4. Lines are integers.\\n\\nIssue text: keep the descriptive part, drop the redundant severity token.\",\n      \"output\": \"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n      \\\"file\\\": \\\"src/cache.py\\\",\\n      \\\"line\\\": 12,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"unbounded lru_cache on long-running server\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n      \\\"file\\\": \\\"src/parser.py\\\",\\n      \\\"line\\\": 88,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"bare except swallows KeyboardInterrupt\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"medium\\\",\\n      \\\"file\\\": \\\"src/io.py\\\",\\n      \\\"line\\\": 45,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"open() without explicit encoding\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"low\\\",\\n      \\\"file\\\": \\\"src/cli.py\\\",\\n      \\\"line\\\": 203,\\n      \\\"issue\\\": \\\"consider a generator here\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"summary\\\": {\\n    \\\"high\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"medium\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"low\\\": 1\\n  }\\n}\\n```\",\n      \"tags\": [\"extraction\", \"enum-mapping\", \"dedup\"]\n    },\n\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"localllm_ops\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"I have 8 GB of VRAM. Can I run a 9B model at Q4_K_M with a 32k context window? Show the arithmetic.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Budget the three consumers, then compare against usable VRAM rather than nameplate VRAM.\\n\\nUsable VRAM: an 8 GB card reports 8188 MiB ~= 8.0 GiB. On Windows the desktop compositor holds roughly 0.4-0.6 GiB, and you want fragmentation headroom. So plan against ~7.2 GiB, not 8.0.\\n\\nWeights: a 9B at Q4_K_M is about 4.7 bits/weight effective including the unquantized bits, giving ~5.7 GB in practice.\\n\\nKV cache: this is the term people forget. Per token you store K and V for every layer: 2 * n_layers * n_kv_heads * head_dim * bytes. With GQA the n_kv_heads is much smaller than n_heads, which is what makes 32k feasible at all. For a 9B-class model with ~40 layers and 8 KV heads of dim 128, at fp16: 2 * 40 * 8 * 128 * 2 bytes = 163,840 B/token ~= 160 KiB/token. At 32,768 tokens that is ~5.4 GB. Way too much.\\n\\nAt q8_0 KV it halves to ~2.7 GB. Still too much on top of 5.7.\\n\\nHmm, that suggests 32k does not fit. Let me reconsider the head count - many 9B models use fewer KV heads or smaller head_dim. If n_kv_heads*head_dim = 512 (i.e. 4 heads of 128), fp16 gives 2*40*512*2 = 81,920 B/token = 80 KiB/token -> 2.6 GB at 32k fp16, ~1.3 GB at q8_0. That fits: 5.7 + 1.3 + 0.4 compute buffer = 7.4, right at the edge.\\n\\nSo the honest answer is: it depends on the GQA config, it only works with a quantized KV cache, and the way to settle it is to measure rather than trust the estimate. I should give the formula, give the measured outcome on this machine, and give the levers.\\n\\nMeasured on this machine: qwen3.5:9b loads at 100% GPU with 32768 context, ~6.5 GB resident. So it does fit with flash attention plus q8_0 KV.\",\n      \"output\": \"Yes, but only with a quantized KV cache \u2014 and the KV cache is the term that decides it, not the weights.\\n\\n**Step 1 \u2014 usable VRAM, not nameplate.**\\n```\\n8 GB card            = 8188 MiB  ~= 8.0 GiB\\n- Windows desktop/compositor      ~0.4 GiB\\n- fragmentation headroom          ~0.4 GiB\\n-----------------------------------------\\nusable                            ~7.2 GiB\\n```\\n\\n**Step 2 \u2014 weights.** A 9B at Q4_K_M averages ~4.7 bits/weight once you include the higher-precision tensors it keeps:\\n```\\n9e9 * 4.7 / 8 / 1e9  ~= 5.3-5.7 GB\\n```\\n\\n**Step 3 \u2014 KV cache.** Per token you store K and V for every layer:\\n```\\nbytes/token = 2 * n_layers * n_kv_heads * head_dim * bytes_per_elem\\n```\\nWith ~40 layers, 4 KV heads \u00d7 128 dim (grouped-query attention \u2014 this is what makes long context affordable at all):\\n```\\nfp16:  2 * 40 * 512 * 2 = 81,920 B/token  = 80 KiB\\n  -> 32,768 tokens = 2.6 GB\\nq8_0:  half that                          = 40 KiB\\n  -> 32,768 tokens = 1.3 GB\\n```\\n\\n**Step 4 \u2014 add it up.**\\n```\\nweights          5.7 GB\\nKV @32k (q8_0)   1.3 GB\\ncompute buffer   0.4 GB\\n----------------------\\ntotal            7.4 GB   vs ~7.2 GiB usable  -> right at the edge\\nsame with fp16 KV: 8.7 GB -> does not fit\\n```\\n\\n**Measured on this machine**, which is the only number that settles it: `qwen3.5:9b` loads at **100% GPU, 32768 context, ~6.5 GB resident** with flash attention and a q8_0 KV cache. So it fits \u2014 with the settings in `start-ollama.ps1`:\\n\\n```\\nOLLAMA_FLASH_ATTENTION=1\\nOLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE=q8_0\\n```\\n\\nYour levers if it ever spills to CPU (you will see it as a sudden drop to single-digit tok/s): drop context to 16k, move to q4_k_m KV, or step down to a 4B. Note that KV cost scales **linearly** with context, so halving the window buys back exactly half the cache.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"vram\", \"kv-cache\", \"arithmetic\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"localllm_ops\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"Explain why a 35B MoE model with 3B active parameters can be usable on an 8 GB GPU with 64 GB of system RAM, when a dense 35B model would be unusable.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The key distinction is that generation speed is bound by memory *bandwidth* over the bytes you must read per token, not by total parameter count.\\n\\nDense 35B at Q4 is ~20 GB. Every token requires reading essentially all of it. If it lives in system RAM over a dual-channel DDR4 bus at ~51 GB/s, the ceiling is 51/20 ~= 2.5 tok/s before overheads, so realistically 1-2 tok/s. Unusable.\\n\\nMoE 35B with 3B active: still ~22 GB on disk/RAM, so it does not fit in 8 GB VRAM either. But per token the router activates only a couple of experts, so you read ~3B params worth ~1.8 GB at Q4. 51/1.8 ~= 28 tok/s theoretical, realistically 12-18 after routing overhead and attention.\\n\\nThe second part: you can split intelligently. Attention layers and shared/dense parts are used for every token, so put those on the GPU. The expert FFN weights are used sparsely, so park them in RAM. That is exactly what llama.cpp's --n-cpu-moe / Ollama's automatic MoE offload does. So the GPU does the per-token-hot work at 288 GB/s and RAM serves the cold sparse work.\n\nSo the answer: same footprint, radically different per-token read volume. Bandwidth per token is the metric that matters.\n\nI should give the arithmetic and the measured expectation, and be honest that it is reading-speed, not interactive-speed.\",\n      \"output\": \"Because generation speed is bound by **bytes read per token**, not by total parameter count \u2014 and an MoE reads a small fraction of itself for each token.\\n\\n**Dense 35B at Q4** is ~20 GB, and essentially every weight is touched for every token. Over your dual-channel DDR4 bus:\\n```\\n51 GB/s / 20 GB per token  ~= 2.5 tok/s   (theoretical ceiling)\\nrealistically                   1-2 tok/s   -> unusable\\n```\\n\\n**MoE 35B with 3B active** occupies the same ~22 GB, but the router selects only a couple of experts per token, so you read ~3B params \u2248 1.8 GB at Q4:\\n```\\n51 GB/s / 1.8 GB per token ~= 28 tok/s    (theoretical ceiling)\\nrealistically                  12-18 tok/s  -> reading speed\\n```\\n\\nSame footprint. **11\u00d7 less traffic per token.** That is the whole trick.\\n\\n**And the split is not arbitrary.** The two kinds of weight have different access patterns, so they belong in different places:\\n\\n| Weights | Used | Put them |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Attention, embeddings, shared/dense layers | every token | **GPU** (288 GB/s) |\\n| Expert FFN blocks | sparsely, router-selected | **System RAM** (51 GB/s) |\\n\\nThat is what `--n-cpu-moe` in llama.cpp (and Ollama's automatic MoE offload) does: the per-token-hot path runs at GPU bandwidth, and the cold sparse path is served from your 64 GB.\\n\\n**Two honest caveats.** This gets you reading speed, not the ~40 tok/s of a fully resident 9B \u2014 so it suits considered single questions rather than long agentic loops. And it needs ~24 GB of free system RAM while loaded, which you have, but it is not free.\\n\\nThe general rule worth carrying: **estimate tok/s as bandwidth \u00f7 bytes-read-per-token.** It predicts local LLM performance far better than parameter count does.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"moe\", \"bandwidth\", \"offload\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"localllm_ops\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"I'm fine-tuning a 4B model with QLoRA on 8 GB VRAM and hitting OOM at sequence length 2048, batch size 2. Rank the fixes by how much memory they save per unit of quality lost.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"I should order by the ratio of memory saved to quality cost, not just by memory saved. Let me think about what each knob actually does.\n\nActivation memory scales roughly with batch_size * seq_len * hidden * n_layers. So batch and seq_len are the big levers.\n\n1. Gradient checkpointing (if not already on): recomputes activations in backward instead of storing them. Saves the most activation memory - often 50-70% - at the cost of ~20-30% slower training. Zero quality cost. This is strictly the best trade if it is off. Unsloth's variant also offloads to CPU.\n\n2. Reduce per_device_batch_size 2->1 and double grad_accum 8->16: halves activation memory, keeps effective batch identical at 16. So the optimizer sees the same thing. Quality cost essentially zero (tiny difference in batchnorm-like stats, irrelevant here). Slightly slower. Excellent trade.\n\n3. adamw_8bit instead of adamw_torch: saves ~2 bytes/param of optimizer state. For LoRA params only (a few million), this is small in absolute terms - maybe 100-200 MB. Negligible quality cost. Good but low magnitude since LoRA already has few trainable params.\n\n4. Reduce seq_len 2048->1024: halves activation memory. But this one DOES cost quality if your examples are longer than 1024 tokens - they get truncated, teaching the model to stop early. So quality cost depends entirely on data length distribution. Should be measured, not assumed.\n\n5. Reduce LoRA rank 16->8: saves very little memory (LoRA params are tiny relative to activations) but directly reduces capacity. Bad ratio - lots of quality risk for little memory. Should be last.\n\n6. Target fewer modules: similar - modest savings, real capacity loss.\n\nAlso: close GPU apps. Browsers can hold 1+ GB. Free, zero quality cost - arguably #0.\n\nSo the ranking: free stuff first (close apps, checkpointing), then batch/accum swap, then 8-bit optim, then seq_len (data-dependent), then rank last.\n\nI should also give the actual numbers for this config and the concrete config edits.\",\n      \"output\": \"Ranked by **memory saved per unit of quality lost** \u2014 which is a different order from \\\"memory saved\\\".\\n\\n**0. Free up what you already own** (saves 0.5\u20131.5 GB, zero quality cost)\\nClose browsers and GPU-accelerated apps first. A Chrome window with hardware acceleration routinely holds over a gigabyte. Check with `nvidia-smi` before blaming the config.\\n\\n**1. Gradient checkpointing** (saves 50\u201370% of activation memory, zero quality cost)\\nRecomputes activations during the backward pass instead of storing them. Costs ~20\u201330% wall-clock, costs nothing in final quality. If it is off, nothing else is worth trying first:\\n```yaml\\nuse_gradient_checkpointing: unsloth   # also offloads some activations to CPU\\n```\\n\\n**2. Batch 2\u21921, grad-accum 8\u219216** (saves ~50% of activation memory, ~zero quality cost)\\nActivation memory scales with `batch \u00d7 seq_len`. Halving the micro-batch while doubling accumulation keeps the **effective batch at 16**, so the optimizer sees an identical update:\\n```yaml\\nper_device_train_batch_size: 1\\ngradient_accumulation_steps: 16\\n```\\nThis is the highest-leverage edit after checkpointing, and it is nearly free.\\n\\n**3. 8-bit optimizer** (saves ~0.1\u20130.2 GB here, negligible quality cost)\\n```yaml\\noptimizer: adamw_8bit\\n```\\nWorth having, but note the modest magnitude: QLoRA already trains only a few million parameters, so optimizer state was never the bulk. The big win from 8-bit optimizers applies to full fine-tuning.\\n\\n**4. Sequence length 2048\u21921024** (saves ~50% of activation memory, **data-dependent** quality cost)\\nHalves activation memory, but truncates any example longer than 1024 tokens \u2014 which teaches the Student to stop mid-answer. Measure before choosing:\\n```bash\\npython -c \\\"import json;ls=[len(json.loads(l)['messages'][-1]['content'])//4 for l in open('dataset/train.jsonl',encoding='utf-8')];print('p50',sorted(ls)[len(ls)//2],'p95',sorted(ls)[int(len(ls)*.95)],'max',max(ls))\\\"\\n```\\nIf p95 is under 1000 tokens, this is nearly free. If p95 is 1800, you would be corrupting your best examples.\\n\\n**5. LoRA rank 16\u21928** (saves very little, direct capacity loss) \u2014 **last resort**\\nLoRA parameters are a rounding error next to activations, so this buys almost no memory while measurably reducing what the adapter can learn. Bad ratio; do the four above first.\\n\\n**For your exact config**, steps 1 and 2 alone should take peak from ~8+ GB to ~4.5 GB:\\n```\\nbase 4-bit weights            2.4 GB   (fixed)\\nLoRA params + grads           0.1 GB\\nadamw_8bit states             0.2 GB\\nactivations @2048, batch 1    1.3 GB   (was ~2.5 at batch 2)\\nCUDA context + fragmentation  0.6 GB\\n-----------------------------------\\npeak                          4.6 GB   of 8.0 GB\\n```\\n\\nVerify rather than trust the estimate \u2014 `train_student.py` prints `torch.cuda.max_memory_reserved()` at the end of every run.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"qlora\", \"oom\", \"tradeoffs\"]\n    },\n\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"supply_chain\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"A dependency offers a new major version where the wheel shrinks from 33 MB to 2.5 MB because grammars are now downloaded on first use instead of being compiled in. Our project promises that building and querying make no network calls. Should we raise the version cap?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The framing matters: this looks like a routine version bump but it is a change in trust boundary, so the cap is a security control rather than a compatibility one.\n\nWhat actually changes: in the old version, the grammars - native compiled code - arrive inside a wheel that is hashed in the lockfile and enumerated in the SBOM. In the new version, the wheel is a loader, and ~21 MB of unsigned native code is fetched at runtime, on first use, into the process that is holding the user's source code and, in CI, their tokens.\n\nThree concrete breakages:\n1. The documented promise of no network calls during build/query becomes false.\n2. The lockfile and SBOM stop describing what executes. The hashed artifact is a loader, not the code that runs. Supply-chain attestation is defeated - you can pin the loader and still execute arbitrary fetched code.\n3. Documented deployment modes break: a read-only container cannot write the grammar cache, and egress-restricted CI cannot fetch at all. So it is not merely a policy concern, it is a functional break of supported configurations.\n\nIs there a config escape? If the new version exposes only cache_dir/languages/groups, those are all pre-download controls - they shape what gets fetched and where it is cached, not whether fetching happens. So there is no setting that satisfies the no-network claim.\n\nConclusion: do not raise the cap on the promise alone. Options: keep the cap (with a comment explaining it is a security boundary, so a future maintainer does not \"helpfully\" bump it), or vendor the grammars yourself and verify hashes, or drop the published claim. The first is right until someone does the vendoring work.\n\nI should also say what would change my mind: an offline/no-network switch, or signed grammar artifacts with pinned hashes.\",\n      \"output\": \"**No \u2014 not on the strength of a smaller wheel.** This is a change of trust boundary dressed as a version bump, so the cap is a *security* control, not a compatibility one.\\n\\n**What actually changes.** In the current major, the grammars \u2014 native compiled code \u2014 ship *inside* the wheel, so they are covered by the hash in `uv.lock` and enumerated in the SBOM. In the new major, the wheel is a loader, and roughly 21 MB of unsigned native code is fetched at runtime, on first `get_parser()`, into the process that is holding the user's source and, in CI, their tokens.\\n\\n**Three things that breaks, concretely:**\\n\\n1. **The documented promise becomes false.** If the README and `SECURITY.md` state that building, querying and the MCP server make no network calls, that claim no longer holds.\\n2. **The lockfile and SBOM stop describing what runs.** The hashed artefact is a *loader*; the code that actually executes is fetched later. You can pin the dependency perfectly and still execute arbitrary fetched native code \u2014 which defeats the point of pinning.\\n3. **Supported deployments stop working.** A read-only container cannot write the grammar cache, and egress-restricted CI cannot fetch at all. This is a functional break of documented configurations, not only a policy objection.\\n\\n**Is there a configuration escape?** Check what the new version actually exposes. If its config object offers only `cache_dir`, `languages` and `groups`, those are all **pre-download** controls \u2014 they shape *what* is fetched and *where* it is cached, not *whether* fetching happens. In that case no setting satisfies the claim, and raising the cap means silently dropping the promise.\\n\\n**Recommendation \u2014 keep the cap, and record why:**\\n\\n```toml\\n# Capped below 1.0 deliberately: this is a security boundary, not a\\n# compatibility one. 0.x compiles every grammar into the wheel, so uv.lock's\\n# hash and the SBOM cover the code that executes. 1.x ships a loader and\\n# fetches ~21 MB of unsigned native code at first use, which breaks the\\n# no-network claim in README/SECURITY.md, makes the lockfile stop describing\\n# what runs, and breaks the read-only container and egress-restricted CI.\\n# 1.x exposes no offline switch. Raising this cap therefore means either\\n# vendoring grammars with pinned hashes, or dropping the published claim.\\n\\\"tree-sitter-language-pack>=0.7,<1.0\\\",\\n```\\n\\nThe comment is load-bearing: without it, a future maintainer reads `<1.0` as staleness and bumps it as housekeeping.\\n\\n**What would change the answer:** an explicit offline/no-fetch mode, or signed grammar artefacts you can pin by hash. Alternatively, do the vendoring work yourself \u2014 build the grammars at release time and verify hashes \u2014 which buys the smaller wheel without giving up attestation.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"supply-chain\", \"pinning\", \"sbom\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"supply_chain\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"A contributor adds `requests` to run a one-time check during package import. Review this decision.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"Two separate objections, and the import-time part is the serious one.\n\nImport-time network I/O is the core problem:\n1. It makes importing the package a network operation. Any consumer importing it in an offline environment, a read-only container, or egress-restricted CI now fails or hangs.\n2. It is a hang risk with no timeout by default - requests has no default timeout, so a black-holed connection blocks import indefinitely. An import that can hang forever is a very bad failure mode because it is often inside a tool's startup path.\n3. It is invisible side-effecting. `import x` should be cheap and pure. Telemetry or version checks at import are widely considered hostile.\n4. For an MCP server specifically: the server is spawned by a coding agent, so a slow import directly delays every session, and a hang bricks the agent.\n\nSecond objection, the dependency itself: requests pulls urllib3, certifi, charset-normalizer, idna. For a single HTTP call, stdlib urllib.request is sufficient and adds zero dependencies. Every added dependency is added attestation surface and CVE surface.\n\nRecommendation: move the check out of import entirely - make it explicit and opt-in (a CLI subcommand), with a timeout, and off by default. If it must stay, lazy-import inside the function so importing the package stays pure.\n\nI should be constructive rather than just blocking - give the shape that would be acceptable.\",\n      \"output\": \"Two objections. The import-time placement is the serious one; the dependency choice is secondary.\\n\\n**1. Network I/O at import time is the real problem.**\\n\\n`import` should be cheap and side-effect-free. Making it perform a network call means:\\n\\n- **Offline and sandboxed consumers break.** Anyone importing the package in an air-gapped environment, a read-only container, or egress-restricted CI now fails \u2014 or worse, hangs.\\n- **It can hang indefinitely.** `requests` has **no default timeout**. A black-holed connection blocks the import forever, and an import that never returns is a brutal failure mode because it sits in every consumer's startup path.\\n- **For an MCP server it is acutely bad.** The server is spawned by a coding agent on every session, so a slow import delays every session and a hung import bricks the agent with no useful error.\\n- **It is invisible.** Nothing in `import repo2graph` suggests a network call. That surprise is why import-time version checks and telemetry are widely treated as hostile.\\n\\n**2. `requests` is the wrong tool for one call.**\\n\\nIt pulls in `urllib3`, `certifi`, `charset-normalizer` and `idna` \u2014 four transitive dependencies, each one more CVE surface and more SBOM surface, for something `urllib.request` does with zero additions.\\n\\n**What I would accept instead** \u2014 explicit, opt-in, bounded, and out of the import path:\\n\\n```python\\n# In the CLI, behind an explicit subcommand. Never at import time.\\ndef check_for_updates(timeout: float = 3.0) -> str | None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Check PyPI for a newer release. Returns None on any failure.\\n\\n    Deliberately: stdlib only, explicit timeout, never raises, and never\\n    called during import - `repo2graph update-check` has to ask for it.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    import json\\n    import urllib.request\\n\\n    try:\\n        with urllib.request.urlopen(\\n            \\\"https://pypi.org/pypi/repo2graph/json\\\", timeout=timeout\\n        ) as r:\\n            return json.load(r)[\\\"info\\\"][\\\"version\\\"]\\n    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError):\\n        return None   # a version check must never break the tool\\n```\\n\\nFour properties that matter: **stdlib only** (no new dependencies), **explicit timeout** (cannot hang), **never raises** (a cosmetic feature must not break the tool), and **never at import** (the user asks for it).\\n\\nIf the check genuinely must live in library code, at minimum lazy-import inside the function so that importing the package stays pure \u2014 and still give it a timeout and a default of off. Also update `SECURITY.md`'s list of network-calling entry points; an undocumented network call is a documentation bug even when the code is fine.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"supply-chain\", \"import-time\", \"review\"]\n    },\n\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"A user says: \\\"the MCP server returns empty results for some repos but I don't know which\\\". Plan your investigation as an ordered list of tool calls, cheapest first.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The complaint is non-deterministic and unlocalised, so the plan should first turn \\\"some repos\\\" into a concrete reproducing case before touching code. The expensive mistake is to start reading the query path immediately.\n\nOrder by cost:\n1. Cheapest: read logs/existing artifacts. If the server logs per-request result counts, the failing repos are already recorded. Zero compute, might solve it outright.\n2. Next: compare a working repo against a failing one on their index state. Empty results almost always means the index is empty or stale, not that the query is wrong. Check index row counts per repo.\n3. Then: look at what the failing repos have in common - language, size, namespace packages, submodules, symlinks. A hypothesis is cheaper than instrumentation.\n4. Only then: read the query path, and specifically the point where index -> results, with the hypothesis in hand.\n5. Reproduce deterministically with the smallest failing repo.\n6. Write a failing test before fixing. This is the verification step.\n7. Fix, run the test plus the full suite to check nothing else regressed.\n\nFailure handling: if logs are absent, go to step 2 directly. If no repo reproduces, the bug may be time or concurrency dependent, which changes the approach to instrumentation.\n\nI must include a verification step and explicit failure handling since that's what the skill demands. And state the key assumption: empty results usually means empty index.\",\n      \"output\": \"**Assumption driving the order:** \\\"empty results\\\" is far more often an empty or stale *index* than a broken *query*. The plan tests that first, and converts \\\"some repos\\\" into one reproducing case before any code is read.\\n\\n| # | Step | Why here |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | **Read the server logs** \u2014 grep for requests returning 0 results | Free, and may name the failing repos outright. If the server logs per-request result counts, the answer may already exist on disk. |\\n| 2 | **Compare index state**, working repo vs failing repo \u2014 row/node counts per repo in the SQLite index | Distinguishes \\\"index is empty\\\" from \\\"query is wrong\\\". One query, and it splits the problem in half. |\\n| 3 | **Look for what failing repos share** \u2014 language, size, namespace packages, symlinks, submodules, non-UTF-8 files | A hypothesis costs nothing and aims every later step. |\\n| 4 | **Read the indexing path** at the point where files are enumerated and skipped | Now targeted by a hypothesis rather than a general code tour. The skip list from step 3 is the prime suspect. |\\n| 5 | **Reproduce on the smallest failing repo** | Turns an intermittent report into a deterministic local failure. |\\n| 6 | **Write a failing test** that asserts a non-empty index for that repo | \u2190 **verification gate.** Proves the diagnosis before any fix, and prevents regression afterwards. |\\n| 7 | **Fix, then run the new test plus the full suite** | Confirms the fix works *and* that it broke nothing else. |\\n\\n**If a step fails:**\\n\\n- **No logs, or no result counts logged** \u2192 skip to step 2. Separately, add the per-request count log: a silent empty result is itself a reportable defect.\\n- **No repo reproduces locally** \u2192 the cause is likely environmental or concurrent (file mtime races, a cache keyed without staleness, a partially-written index). Switch from static reading to instrumentation and ask the user for one failing repo's index file.\n- **Step 2 shows the index is fully populated** \u2192 the hypothesis is wrong, the bug is in the query/retrieval path, and steps 3-4 re-aim there instead.\\n\\n**What I will not do:** report it fixed because step 7's test passes on one repo. The original report said *some* repos, so after fixing I would re-run across the full set from step 1 to confirm the class of failure is gone, not just the instance.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"planning\", \"debugging\", \"verification\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n      \"instruction\": \"A user asks you to \\\"add caching to the parser so it's faster\\\". Before writing code, what do you establish, and what would make you push back?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The request presupposes that parsing is the bottleneck and that caching is the fix. Both might be false. A good agent validates the premise cheaply before building.\n\nWhat to establish:\n1. Is parsing actually the bottleneck? Profile. If 80% of time is in the filesystem walk or in the graph write, a parser cache changes nothing. This is the measurement that makes or breaks the whole task.\n2. What is the access pattern? A cache only helps if the same file is parsed more than once in a run or across runs. If each file is parsed exactly once per index build, an in-process cache is useless - what they want is persistence across runs.\n3. Lifetime/scope: per-process, or persisted to disk? Persisted means invalidation becomes a correctness problem (mtime/size keying), not just a memory question.\n4. Correctness constraint: for a long-running MCP server serving a live working tree, a cache keyed on path alone will serve stale trees. This is the risk that makes caching actively dangerous rather than merely useless.\n5. Memory budget: tree-sitter Trees retain source bytes, so unbounded caching leaks.\n\nWhat would make me push back: if profiling shows parsing is not dominant, or if each file is parsed once per run. Then the real fix is elsewhere (prune the walk, batch the writes, parallelise).\n\nI should be concrete and deliver the measurement commands, and make clear I'd still do the work if they reaffirm - but with the right design.\",\n      \"output\": \"The request bundles a diagnosis (\\\"parsing is slow\\\") with a prescription (\\\"add caching\\\"). Both are worth a few minutes of checking, because a cache that misses is pure overhead and a cache that goes stale is a correctness bug.\\n\\n**What I establish first:**\\n\\n1. **Is parsing actually the bottleneck?** Profile one real index build:\\n   ```bash\\n   python -m cProfile -s cumtime -m repo2graph index /path/to/repo 2>&1 | head -30\\n   ```\\n   If the time is dominated by the filesystem walk or the index writes, a parser cache buys nothing. This single measurement decides whether the task is worth doing.\\n\\n2. **Is any file parsed more than once?** A cache only pays if there are repeat reads. Count them:\\n   - If each file is parsed exactly once per run, an in-process cache has a **0% hit rate** \u2014 it adds memory and complexity for nothing. What would actually help is *persistence across runs*, which is a different feature.\\n   - If the MCP server re-parses on every query, the hit rate is high and caching is clearly right.\\n\\n3. **What is the cache's lifetime?** Per-process is a memory question. Persisted to disk makes **invalidation a correctness question** \u2014 the key must include `st_mtime_ns` and `st_size`, not just the path.\\n\\n4. **Does a live working tree need to be visible?** For an MCP server this is the sharp edge: a cache keyed on path alone will happily serve a tree for code the developer edited thirty seconds ago, with citations pointing at lines that have since moved. Here caching is not merely useless if done naively \u2014 it is actively harmful.\\n\\n5. **What is the memory budget?** Tree-sitter `Tree` objects retain their source bytes, so an unbounded cache is a slow leak on a long-running server. `maxsize` is mandatory, not tidy.\\n\\n**What would make me push back:**\\n\\n- **Profiling shows parsing is not dominant.** Then I would say so and propose the real fix \u2014 pruning `.git`/`node_modules`/`__pycache__` from the walk, batching index writes, or parallelising across files \u2014 rather than build a cache that cannot help.\\n- **Each file is parsed once per run.** Then the honest answer is that an in-process cache has no hits, and I would ask whether they actually want a persistent on-disk index.\\n\\n**If you reaffirm after seeing the numbers, I will build it** \u2014 bounded `maxsize`, keyed on `(path, st_mtime_ns, st_size)`, with a hit-rate counter exposed so we can confirm it is earning its keep rather than assume it.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"planning\", \"premise-checking\", \"pushback\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"You ran the test suite after a change. 3 tests fail, but they also failed before your change. What do you report, and what do you do?\",\n      \"thinking\": \"The honest-reporting case. The temptation is either to claim success (\"my change works, those failures are unrelated\") or to get derailed fixing pre-existing failures that weren't asked about.\n\nWhat I must establish: are these failures genuinely pre-existing and genuinely unrelated? \"Failed before\" is necessary but not sufficient - my change could have changed the failure mode while the test still fails. So I should compare the actual failure output, not just pass/fail status.\n\nMethod: stash my change, run the 3 tests, capture output. Restore, run again, compare. If identical, they are untouched by my change. If the error differs, my change interacted with them and that's my problem.\n\nWhat to report: both facts plainly. My change's status, and the pre-existing failures with evidence that they predate me. Do not bury either. Do not claim the suite is green.\n\nWhat to do: not fix them silently - that mixes unrelated work into the change and makes review harder. Ask, or note them. Scaling scope is the user's call.\n\nSo the answer structure: verify sameness, report both plainly, don't expand scope unasked, offer.\n\nThis is exactly the behavior worth distilling - small models tend to either over-claim success or spiral into unrelated fixes.\",\n      \"output\": \"**Report both facts plainly, verify the \\\"pre-existing\\\" claim before making it, and do not silently expand scope.**\\n\\n**First, verify that they are genuinely untouched by my change** \u2014 \\\"it failed before too\\\" is necessary but not sufficient. My change could alter *how* a test fails while it still fails, and that would be my problem. So compare the failure output, not just the pass/fail status:\\n\\n```bash\\ngit stash                                   # set my change aside\\npytest tests/test_a.py tests/test_b.py tests/test_c.py -v > /tmp/before.txt 2>&1\\ngit stash pop                               # restore it\\npytest tests/test_a.py tests/test_b.py tests/test_c.py -v > /tmp/after.txt 2>&1\\ndiff /tmp/before.txt /tmp/after.txt\\n```\\n\\n- **Identical output** \u2192 genuinely pre-existing and unrelated. I can say so with evidence.\\n- **Different error, still failing** \u2192 my change *did* interact with them. That is now in scope and I own it.\\n\\n**What I report:**\\n\\n> My change is complete and the 47 tests covering it pass.\\n>\\n> 3 tests were already failing before my change and still fail identically \u2014 `test_a`, `test_b`, `test_c`, all on `KeyError: 'lang'` in the fixture. I verified this by stashing my change and re-running: the output is byte-identical, so they are unrelated to this work.\\n>\\n> I have not touched them. Want me to fix them as a separate change?\\n\\n**What I do not do:**\\n\\n1. **Claim the suite is green.** It is not. \\\"All tests pass\\\" when three fail is the kind of report that destroys trust in every later report I make.\\n2. **Quietly fix them.** That mixes unrelated work into this change, makes the diff harder to review, and takes a scoping decision that is the user's to make.\\n3. **Bury the pre-existing failures** in a footnote. They are real information about the repo's health, even though they are not mine.\\n4. **Get derailed.** The requested work is done; the pre-existing failures are a separate, offered piece of work.\\n\\nThe principle: **report what is true, separate what I caused from what I inherited, and let the user decide whether to widen the scope.**\",\n      \"tags\": [\"planning\", \"honest-reporting\", \"scope\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"agentic_planning\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n      \"instruction\": \"You're asked to index a 500k-file monorepo, but the tool OOMs partway through. Plan the fix. You may not simply tell the user to buy more RAM.\",\n      \"thinking\": \"At 500k files, anything that accumulates per-file state in memory will die. The fix is architectural: make memory usage independent of repo size.\n\nWhere the memory goes, likely:\n1. Collecting all file paths into a list before processing (hundreds of thousands of strings + Path objects).\n2. Accumulating all parsed nodes/edges in memory before writing to the index.\n3. Holding tree-sitter Trees (which retain source bytes) longer than needed.\n4. An unbounded cache.\n\nThe fix for each:\n1. Stream paths with a generator instead of materialising.\n2. Batch-write to SQLite in chunks with periodic commits, so the node/edge buffer is bounded. This is the main one.\n3. Drop the Tree as soon as symbols are extracted - don't keep it past the loop iteration.\n4. Bound caches.\n\nPlus: make it resumable. At 500k files the job takes a long time and OOM/interruption midway is catastrophic if you have to restart. Track completed files in the index so a rerun skips them. That also makes the OOM survivable while we fix it - the user can make progress today.\n\nPlus: measure before guessing. tracemalloc or memory_profiler to find the actual accumulator.\n\nPriority: measurement -> streaming+batched writes -> resumability -> drop trees eagerly -> bound caches. And the immediate unblock: resumability lets them finish today in chunks.\n\nI should give the concrete batched-write shape since that's the crux. And a verification step: peak RSS must be flat as file count grows.\",\n      \"output\": \"The goal is to make peak memory **independent of repo size**. At 500k files, any per-file accumulation is fatal, so this is an architecture fix rather than a tuning one.\\n\\n**Step 1 \u2014 find the actual accumulator** (do not guess):\\n```python\\nimport tracemalloc\\ntracemalloc.start()\\n# ... index ~20k files ...\\nfor stat in tracemalloc.take_snapshot().statistics(\\\"lineno\\\")[:10]:\\n    print(stat)\\n```\\nThe four usual culprits, in order of likelihood:\\n\\n1. Materialising every path into a list before processing\\n2. Buffering all nodes/edges in memory before one big index write\\n3. Holding tree-sitter `Tree` objects (which retain source bytes) past their use\\n4. An unbounded cache\\n\\n**Step 2 \u2014 stream paths instead of collecting them.** Return an `Iterator[Path]`, and prune `.git`/`node_modules`/`__pycache__` at the directory level so you never descend into them. On a monorepo the pruning is also most of the speed win.\\n\\n**Step 3 \u2014 batch the writes. This is the crux.** Flush on a bounded buffer so memory is O(batch), not O(repo):\\n\\n```python\\nBATCH = 2_000\\n\\ndef index_repo(root: Path, conn: sqlite3.Connection) -> None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Index `root` with memory bounded by BATCH, not by repo size.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    buf: list[tuple] = []\\n    for path in find_source_files(root):          # generator, pruned\\n        if already_indexed(conn, path):           # resumability, see step 4\\n            continue\\n        tree = parse(path)\\n        buf.extend(extract_symbols(tree, path))\\n        del tree                                  # drop source bytes now\\n\\n        if len(buf) >= BATCH:\\n            conn.executemany(\\\"INSERT INTO symbols VALUES (?,?,?,?)\\\", buf)\\n            conn.commit()                         # checkpoint: survives a crash\\n            buf.clear()\\n\\n    if buf:\\n        conn.executemany(\\\"INSERT INTO symbols VALUES (?,?,?,?)\\\", buf)\\n        conn.commit()\\n```\\n\\n**Step 4 \u2014 make it resumable.** At this scale the run takes a long time, and an interruption that forces a full restart is its own outage. Record each completed file in the index and skip it on re-entry. This also **unblocks the user today**: they can run it repeatedly and make progress in chunks even before the memory fix lands.\\n\\n**Step 5 \u2014 bound every cache.** `lru_cache(maxsize=None)` cannot survive 500k files. Give it a real ceiling, and key it on `(path, st_mtime_ns, st_size)` so entries are correct as well as bounded.\\n\\n**Verification \u2014 the test that proves it:**\\n\\n```bash\\n/usr/bin/time -v python -m repo2graph index /monorepo 2>&1 | grep \\\"Maximum resident\\\"\\n```\\nRun at 10k, 50k and 200k files. **Peak RSS must be flat, not rising.** If it still climbs linearly, step 1 missed an accumulator and I go back rather than declaring it fixed.\\n\\nSecondary benefit worth noting: periodic `commit()` turns one unbounded transaction into checkpoints, so a crash loses one batch rather than the entire run.\",\n      \"tags\": [\"planning\", \"memory\", \"scalability\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n"
