1. **The neutered concurrency test is the headline finding.** `tests/test_store.py::test_sqlite_store_concurrent_thread_queries` was edited to acquire a `threading.Lock()` around its own store calls, which serialises all 8 "concurrent" workers onto one at a time. Combined with `store.py` losing `self._lock`, the test now has zero power to detect that regression — it would pass identically whether the lock exists, is removed, or is replaced with something unsafe.
2. **Why #523 was caught but #524 wasn't is structural, not just the test edit.** #523 (dropped `BuildLock` around first materialization) produces a hard `sqlite3` crash under races — any unlocked test catches it. #524 (dropped per-query lock on a read-only connection) has no crash-level failure mode in CPython's `sqlite3`, which already serializes at the C level — so even the *original, unlocked* test may never have been able to catch this class of regression. The suite's concurrency tests generally assert "didn't crash," not "concurrent effects were correct," making them blind to races that resolve to merely-wrong (non-crashing) output.
3. **Coverage gate is real but single-cell.** `fail_under = 80` (branch) is enforced only on one CI matrix cell (Ubuntu/3.13); all other cells, including every Windows cell, run without `--cov`. Measured a labeled 6-file subset at 41.31% purely for methodology; did not re-derive the real ~86% full-suite figure per instructions.
4. **Highest-value untested gap: on-disk format backward compatibility.** No test opens an index built by an older `repo2graph` version with the current code — a schema change silently breaks every user's existing cache with no CI signal.
5. **Skips/xfail all look legitimate** — platform capability gaps (Windows symlinks, non-UTF-8 filenames, POSIX-only paths) or genuinely optional extras (`model2vec`, real-embedder smoke test), none masking a fixable failure.

