Published: **https://claude.ai/code/artifact/aece4ae7-3fae-4515-93c2-b9cbf37e9fc3**

All three parts are in there in full. The four judgments it argues:

**Part A — one program, three overlays.** The useful split isn't by framework, it's between *controls* (things Ajaia does — access, logging, training, vendor review) and *obligations* (things a law requires of a named party on a named clock). Controls generalize; obligations don't. So: SOC 2 TSC as the control spine, HIPAA cross-walked via NIST SP 800-66r2, FERPA/state law as a third reference column — then three overlay procedures for what won't map. Two operating rules keep it safe: shortest clock governs, strictest control governs. One hard limit on "unified": PHI and education records must not share a logical store.

**Part B1 — legal is wrong, and it's cheap to fix.** Symptoms and medications are the clinical core of a record, not metadata. HIPAA has no "PII" concept; the test is individual identifiability, and Safe Harbor demands all 18 identifiers gone plus no actual knowledge of residual risk. These are patient-facing *free-text* translation prompts — narrative routinely carries names, places, dates — plus transport identifiers (IP, session/request IDs) in categories 15 and 18. And even successful de-identification wouldn't help: de-identifying PHI is itself a use of PHI, and §164.308(b)(2) requires a BAA with Anthropic as subcontractor. No BAA → impermissible disclosure → *presumed* breach under §164.402. Anthropic offers a BAA; this is procurement, not engineering.

**Part B2 — FERPA permits the OpenAI flow, conditionally.** Ajaia is the school official; OpenAI is a *further* disclosure the district didn't authorize. Eleven controls listed; the sanction to design against is §99.33(a)(2) — improper redisclosure lets ED bar a district from disclosing to Ajaia for five years. Note that OpenAI's no-training default is a product setting, not a contractual commitment, and FERPA compliance is demonstrated by contract.

**Part C — 13 IR gaps, 11 classification gaps, each with the specific citation.** The two IR findings that matter most aren't the 96-hour clock (that's inside HIPAA's 60-day ceiling, though it almost certainly breaches the BAAs and blows NY §2-d's 7 days): they're the **absence of the four-factor breach determination** — so "confirmed breach" is undefined and the clock has no start — and the **blanket 4-hour containment**, which under HIPAA's definition of security incident (§164.304 includes *attempted* access) means every failed login triggers CEO notification. Unachievable, so it'll be ignored, and in Type I an unperformable commitment is a design deficiency on its face.

The classification decision creating immediate risk: **education records are Internal, and only Confidential must be encrypted** — so the policy states in writing that student PII needn't be encrypted. That breaches NY Ed. Law §2-d/Part 121 and every district DPA, and because "Internal" means company-wide access it destroys the legitimate-educational-interest limitation the school-official exception is conditioned on. It also forfeits the encryption breach safe harbor, making every exposure notifiable at full scope. Fix is a fourth *Regulated* tier — and verify production encryption state rather than assuming the policy is the only thing broken.

