<div class="wrap">

  <header class="mast">
    <div class="eyebrow">Module 3 · Compliance governance &amp; policy</div>
    <h1>One program, three frameworks, <em>and one misclassification that has to be fixed this week</em></h1>
    <div class="mast-meta">
      <span><b>Prepared for</b> CEO, Ajaia</span>
      <span><b>Scope</b> HIPAA · FERPA · SOC 2 Type I</span>
      <span><b>Date</b> 1 November 2026</span>
      <span><b>Status</b> No formal security policies in force</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- ============ SUMMARY ============ -->
  <section id="summary">
    <div class="part-head">
      <div class="part-kicker">Executive summary</div>
      <h2>Four judgments</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="legend">
      <span><i class="swatch" style="background: var(--hipaa)"></i> HIPAA</span>
      <span><i class="swatch" style="background: var(--ferpa)"></i> FERPA</span>
      <span><i class="swatch" style="background: var(--soc2)"></i> SOC 2</span>
      <span><i class="swatch" style="background: var(--crit)"></i> Critical</span>
      <span><i class="swatch" style="background: var(--warn)"></i> High</span>
      <span><i class="swatch" style="background: var(--ink-3)"></i> Medium</span>
    </div>

    <div class="judgments">
      <div class="judgment">
        <div class="j-num">01</div>
        <div class="j-body">
          <span class="j-title">Build one program with three overlays — not three programs, and not one undifferentiated program.</span>
          <p>Roughly 70–80% of the required controls are shared and should be written, operated, and evidenced once. The remaining 20–30% are framework-specific obligations that do not map across — breach notification clocks, parental access rights, the school-official exception — and those get overlay procedures, not merged ones. Expect total effort near 1.3× a single program, not 3×.</p>
          <div class="chips j-tags"><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip f">FERPA</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="judgment">
        <div class="j-num">02</div>
        <div class="j-body">
          <span class="j-title">The legal team's HIPAA conclusion is wrong, and the gap is cheap to close.</span>
          <p>Symptoms and medication names <em>are</em> protected health information. "We stripped PII" is not a HIPAA standard; de-identification requires either Safe Harbor's 18-identifier removal or an Expert Determination, and free-text clinical narrative cannot satisfy Safe Harbor without a validated and verified scrubbing pipeline. Anthropic offers a BAA for the API. Execute it before the next release.</p>
          <div class="chips j-tags"><span class="chip sev sev-crit">Critical</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="judgment">
        <div class="j-num">03</div>
        <div class="j-body">
          <span class="j-title">Sending student performance data to OpenAI does not inherently violate FERPA — but Ajaia almost certainly lacks the paper to justify it today.</span>
          <p>The school-official exception permits it if the district retains direct control, the purpose is fixed, and onward disclosure is authorized. Ajaia is the school official; OpenAI is a sub-disclosure. Without district-approved subprocessor terms and a written no-training / no-secondary-use commitment, the exposure is improper redisclosure — which can bar a district from disclosing to Ajaia for five years.</p>
          <div class="chips j-tags"><span class="chip sev sev-high">High</span><span class="chip f">FERPA</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="judgment">
        <div class="j-num">04</div>
        <div class="j-body">
          <span class="j-title">The Data Classification Policy contains one decision that creates immediate regulatory risk.</span>
          <p>Student education records are classified <strong>Internal</strong>. Encryption is required only for <strong>Confidential</strong>. The policy therefore states, in writing, that Ajaia does not require student records to be encrypted — and that "Internal" grants access company-wide, which destroys the legitimate-educational-interest limitation FERPA's school-official exception depends on. <a class="cite-link" href="#critical">Detail below.</a></p>
          <div class="chips j-tags"><span class="chip sev sev-crit">Critical</span><span class="chip f">FERPA</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ PART A ============ -->
  <section id="part-a">
    <div class="part-head">
      <div class="part-kicker">Part A · Compliance program design</div>
      <h2>Can one system cover HIPAA, FERPA, and SOC 2?</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
      <p class="lede">Yes — for the control layer. No — for the obligation layer. The distinction matters, because conflating them is how startups end up with a policy binder that satisfies an auditor and still breaches a statute.</p>
      <p>A control is something Ajaia <em>does</em>: provision access, review logs, train staff, assess a vendor. An obligation is something a law <em>requires of a particular party on a particular clock</em>: notify a covered entity within a contractual window, let a parent inspect a record, retain documentation for six years. Controls generalize well across frameworks. Obligations do not generalize at all — they attach to specific data, specific counterparties, and specific deadlines.</p>
      <p>The recommended architecture is therefore <strong>one control framework, one policy set, one evidence pipeline — and three obligation overlays</strong>.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Where the frameworks overlap</h3>
    <div class="col">
      <p>These ten domains carry substantially the same requirement under all three regimes. Write each once, operate each once, and map the single control to all three framework references so one piece of evidence satisfies three reviewers.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tscroll">
      <table>
        <caption>Shared control domains and their framework anchors</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Control domain</th>
            <th class="h-col">HIPAA</th>
            <th class="f-col">FERPA</th>
            <th class="s-col">SOC 2 (TSC)</th>
            <th>Build</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Risk assessment</td>
            <td>Required risk analysis and risk management <span class="ref">§164.308(a)(1)(ii)(A)–(B)</span></td>
            <td>No explicit requirement; implied by "reasonable methods" and district contracts <span class="ref">34 CFR 99.31(a)(1)(ii)</span></td>
            <td>Objectives set, risks identified, change assessed <span class="ref">CC3.1–CC3.4</span></td>
            <td class="verdict v-one">One</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Access control</td>
            <td>Unique user ID, authorization, emergency access, auto-logoff <span class="ref">§164.308(a)(4), §164.312(a)</span></td>
            <td>Access limited to legitimate educational interest <span class="ref">99.31(a)(1)(i)(A)</span></td>
            <td>Logical access provisioned, modified, removed <span class="ref">CC6.1–CC6.3</span></td>
            <td class="verdict v-one">One</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Audit logging</td>
            <td>Audit controls; information system activity review <span class="ref">§164.312(b), §164.308(a)(1)(ii)(D)</span></td>
            <td>Record of disclosures where applicable <span class="ref">99.32</span></td>
            <td>Monitoring, anomaly detection <span class="ref">CC7.1–CC7.2</span></td>
            <td class="verdict v-one">One</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Workforce training</td>
            <td>Security awareness and training program <span class="ref">§164.308(a)(5)</span></td>
            <td>Required by district contracts and state student-privacy law</td>
            <td>Competence, accountability <span class="ref">CC1.4–CC1.5</span></td>
            <td class="verdict v-one">One</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Vendor &amp; subprocessor management</td>
            <td>BAA with every subcontractor touching PHI <span class="ref">§164.308(b), §164.502(e)</span></td>
            <td>Direct control retained; no unauthorized redisclosure <span class="ref">99.31(a)(1)(i)(B), 99.33(a)</span></td>
            <td>Vendor risk assessed and monitored <span class="ref">CC9.2</span></td>
            <td class="verdict v-overlay">One + overlay</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Encryption</td>
            <td>Addressable at rest and in transit; documented decision if not implemented <span class="ref">§164.312(a)(2)(iv), (e)(2)(ii)</span></td>
            <td>Required at rest and in transit by state law and contract <span class="ref">e.g. NY Ed. Law §2-d / Part 121</span></td>
            <td>Protection of data in transit and at rest <span class="ref">CC6.7</span></td>
            <td class="verdict v-one">One</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Incident response</td>
            <td>Response and reporting; document incidents and outcomes <span class="ref">§164.308(a)(6)</span></td>
            <td>No federal process requirement; state laws impose clocks</td>
            <td>Incidents identified, evaluated, responded to, resolved <span class="ref">CC7.3–CC7.5</span></td>
            <td class="verdict v-overlay">One + overlay</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Contingency &amp; backup</td>
            <td>Backup, disaster recovery, emergency mode, testing <span class="ref">§164.308(a)(7)</span></td>
            <td>Records availability under district contract</td>
            <td>Availability criteria if in scope <span class="ref">A1.1–A1.3</span></td>
            <td class="verdict v-one">One</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Retention &amp; disposal</td>
            <td>Media disposal and re-use; six-year documentation retention <span class="ref">§164.310(d)(2), §164.316(b)(2)</span></td>
            <td>Destruction on district direction; no destruction while access request pending <span class="ref">99.10(e)</span></td>
            <td>Confidential data retained and disposed per commitments <span class="ref">C1.1–C1.2</span></td>
            <td class="verdict v-overlay">One + overlay</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Policy governance</td>
            <td>Policies maintained, reviewed, updated <span class="ref">§164.316(a)</span></td>
            <td>Annual notification of rights by the school <span class="ref">99.7</span></td>
            <td>Policies deployed and periodically reviewed <span class="ref">CC5.3</span></td>
            <td class="verdict v-one">One</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Where the frameworks genuinely diverge</h3>
    <div class="col">
      <p>These are the places a unified program breaks if you force it. Each needs its own procedure, its own owner, and its own clock.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tscroll">
      <table>
        <caption>Divergences requiring framework-specific overlays</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Dimension</th>
            <th class="h-col">HIPAA</th>
            <th class="f-col">FERPA</th>
            <th class="s-col">SOC 2</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Legal character</td>
            <td>Federal regulation; OCR enforcement; civil and criminal penalties</td>
            <td>Federal funding condition; no private right of action; enforcement runs against the <em>school</em></td>
            <td>Voluntary attestation against management's own assertions; no regulator</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Who is liable</td>
            <td>Ajaia is directly liable as a business associate <span class="ref">HITECH / 2013 Omnibus Rule</span></td>
            <td>The district is liable; Ajaia's exposure is contractual, reputational, and under state law</td>
            <td>Ajaia is accountable only for what it asserts in its own description of the system</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Breach notification</td>
            <td>BA notifies the covered entity without unreasonable delay, no later than 60 days <span class="ref">§164.410</span>; 500+ records triggers media and contemporaneous HHS notice <span class="ref">§164.408</span></td>
            <td><strong>No federal breach notification requirement.</strong> State laws govern — NY Ed. Law §2-d: 7 calendar days to the district; IL SOPPA: 30 days</td>
            <td>No statutory clock; the process must match the commitments you published</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">De-identification standard</td>
            <td>Safe Harbor's 18 identifiers, or Expert Determination <span class="ref">§164.514(b)</span></td>
            <td>A different test: removal of all PII including indirect identifiers, plus reasonable determination that re-identification risk is low <span class="ref">99.31(b), 99.3</span></td>
            <td>No standard</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Purpose limitation</td>
            <td>Minimum necessary; use only as permitted by the BAA <span class="ref">§164.502(b)</span></td>
            <td>Use only for the purpose of disclosure; no redisclosure; state laws bar targeted advertising and commercial profiling <span class="ref">99.33(a)</span></td>
            <td>Silent unless asserted under the Privacy criteria</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Individual rights</td>
            <td>Access, amendment, accounting of disclosures — routed through the covered entity</td>
            <td>Parent/eligible-student inspection within 45 days, amendment, hearing — Ajaia must be able to produce records on district request <span class="ref">99.10, 99.20–99.22</span></td>
            <td>None</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Assurance model</td>
            <td>Continuous; no certification exists; no safe harbor from having policies</td>
            <td>Continuous; evidenced through the district contract</td>
            <td>Type I is point-in-time design only; Type II requires 3–12 months of operating evidence</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="domain">Data segregation</td>
            <td colspan="3">PHI and education records must not be commingled in one logical store. Different lawful bases, different deletion triggers, different notification duties. <strong>This is the one place "unified" must not mean "merged."</strong></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Recommended approach</h3>
    <div class="col">
      <p>Adopt the <strong>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria as the control spine</strong>, because it is the broadest control-based framework of the three and the one an external auditor will test. Cross-walk HIPAA Security Rule requirements onto it using NIST SP 800-66r2 as the mapping reference, and attach FERPA and state student-privacy requirements as a third column on the same control register. Every control then carries three reference tags, and one test produces three pieces of evidence.</p>
      <p>Then layer the obligations that do not map:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>HIPAA overlay</strong> — named Privacy Officer and Security Officer, BAA register with subcontractor flow-down, the four-factor breach risk assessment, minimum-necessary review of prompt payloads, six-year documentation retention.</li>
        <li><strong>FERPA overlay</strong> — school-official designation tracking per district, approved subprocessor list, direct-control and audit rights, records-production procedure for parent access requests, deletion on district direction, state-law clock matrix.</li>
        <li><strong>SOC 2 overlay</strong> — system description, control-test calendar, management assertion, and an honest internal note that Type I is a point-in-time design opinion. Many enterprise buyers discount it; commit to Type II within twelve months so the sales unblock does not become a credibility problem at renewal.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Two operating rules keep the unified program safe. First, <strong>the shortest clock governs</strong>: where HIPAA allows 60 days, a district contract demands 24 hours, and a state statute demands 7, the policy states the shortest and the overlays record the rest. Second, <strong>the strictest control governs</strong>: where HIPAA treats encryption as addressable and New York treats it as mandatory, Ajaia encrypts.</p>
    </div>

    <h4>Sequencing</h4>
    <div class="phases">
      <div class="phase">
        <div class="ph-when">Weeks 1–2</div>
        <div class="ph-body">
          <b>Stop the bleeding</b>
          <p>Execute a BAA with Anthropic. Reclassify student education records as Confidential. Get written no-training / no-secondary-use terms from OpenAI. Name a single accountable compliance owner, plus the HIPAA Privacy and Security Officer roles — one person may hold all three at this size, but the roles must be named in writing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="phase">
        <div class="ph-when">Weeks 3–6</div>
        <div class="ph-body">
          <b>Know what you hold</b>
          <p>Data inventory and flow map covering every system, vendor, and API payload. One risk register. One vendor register with agreement status per vendor. This is a HIPAA requirement in its own right — you cannot perform §164.308(a)(1)(ii)(A) risk analysis without knowing where ePHI lives.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="phase">
        <div class="ph-when">Weeks 6–12</div>
        <div class="ph-body">
          <b>One policy set</b>
          <p>Twelve to fifteen policies, each tagged with the frameworks it serves and each naming an owner, an effective date, and a review cadence. Rewrite the two drafts reviewed in Part C. Add per-tier handling procedures and an AI/LLM acceptable-use standard that states which classification tiers may be sent to which vendors under which agreement.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="phase">
        <div class="ph-when">Months 3–6</div>
        <div class="ph-body">
          <b>Operate and evidence</b>
          <p>Access reviews, log reviews, training completion, vendor reassessment, one documented tabletop exercise. Tenant and key separation between Harmoni PHI and Ethos education records. Readiness assessment, then the Type I engagement.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="phase">
        <div class="ph-when">Months 6–18</div>
        <div class="ph-body">
          <b>Prove it runs</b>
          <p>Type II observation window. Annual HIPAA evaluation under §164.308(a)(8). Policy review cycle closes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ PART B1 — MEMO ============ -->
  <section id="memo">
    <div class="part-head">
      <div class="part-kicker">Part B.1 · PHI determination</div>
      <h2>Memo to the CEO</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="memo">
      <dl class="memo-head">
        <dt>To</dt><dd>Chief Executive Officer, Ajaia</dd>
        <dt>From</dt><dd>Security &amp; Compliance</dd>
        <dt>Date</dt><dd>1 November 2026</dd>
        <dt>Re</dt><dd>Whether HIPAA applies to Harmoni's Claude API calls — and why the current legal position does not hold</dd>
        <dt>Class.</dt><dd>Confidential · Attorney work product requested</dd>
      </dl>

      <div class="col">
        <p class="verdict-line">The legal team's interpretation is incorrect. Stripping names and dates of birth does not remove data from HIPAA's scope, and the API calls to Anthropic are disclosures of PHI to a subcontractor that today has no Business Associate Agreement in place.</p>

        <h4>1. What PHI actually is</h4>
        <p>PHI is individually identifiable health information held or transmitted by a covered entity or business associate (<code>45 CFR 160.103</code>). Three elements: it relates to an individual's physical or mental health, the provision of care, or payment for care; it identifies the individual <em>or</em> there is a reasonable basis to believe it could be used to identify them; and it is created or received in connection with a covered function.</p>
        <p>Patient symptoms and medication names satisfy the first element directly — they are the clinical core of a record, not metadata around it. The legal team's position rests entirely on defeating the second element, and that is where it fails. HIPAA has no concept of "PII." The relevant standard is not whether direct identifiers were removed but whether the information remains <em>individually identifiable</em>, and that is a far lower bar than a name.</p>

        <h4>2. The safe harbor standard, and why free text cannot meet it</h4>
        <p>There are exactly two ways to de-identify PHI under <code>§164.514(b)</code>: Expert Determination, or Safe Harbor. No Expert Determination has been performed. Safe Harbor requires removal of <strong>all eighteen</strong> identifier categories <em>and</em> no actual knowledge that the residual information could identify an individual. Removing two of eighteen is not partial compliance; it is non-compliance.</p>

        <div class="ident">
          <div><span class="n">1</span>Names</div>
          <div class="hit"><span class="n">2</span>Geography below state, incl. ZIP</div>
          <div class="hit"><span class="n">3</span>All date elements except year</div>
          <div><span class="n">4</span>Telephone numbers</div>
          <div><span class="n">5</span>Fax numbers</div>
          <div class="hit"><span class="n">6</span>Email addresses</div>
          <div><span class="n">7</span>Social Security numbers</div>
          <div class="hit"><span class="n">8</span>Medical record numbers</div>
          <div><span class="n">9</span>Health plan beneficiary nos.</div>
          <div class="hit"><span class="n">10</span>Account numbers</div>
          <div><span class="n">11</span>Certificate / license nos.</div>
          <div><span class="n">12</span>Vehicle identifiers</div>
          <div><span class="n">13</span>Device identifiers &amp; serials</div>
          <div class="hit"><span class="n">14</span>URLs</div>
          <div class="hit"><span class="n">15</span>IP addresses</div>
          <div><span class="n">16</span>Biometric identifiers</div>
          <div><span class="n">17</span>Full-face photographs</div>
          <div class="hit"><span class="n">18</span>Any other unique identifying number, characteristic, or code</div>
        </div>
        <p class="ident-note"><i class="key"></i> Shaded categories are ones Harmoni's API traffic plausibly carries today.</p>

        <p>Two problems specifically. First, the transport layer: an API call carries an IP address, and our prompts carry session, request, and account identifiers for correlation. Those land squarely in categories 15 and 18 — and category 18, the catch-all for "any other unique identifying number, characteristic, or code," is what defeats most engineering-led de-identification claims.</p>
        <p>Second, and more serious, the <em>content</em> layer. These are patient-facing translation prompts, which means the payload is free-text clinical narrative. Patients write "my daughter Maya," "my doctor at Mercy in Tulsa," "since my surgery on the 14th." A rare diagnosis plus a geography is itself identifying. No one can assert Safe Harbor over unstructured free text without a validated scrubbing pipeline and measured residual-risk verification. We have neither, and the "actual knowledge" prong means that once we are aware narrative text routinely contains identifiers — which this memo establishes — an unverified claim is no longer defensible.</p>

        <h4>3. BAA implications</h4>
        <p>Even if de-identification were achieved, the conclusion would not change, because <strong>de-identifying PHI is itself a use of PHI</strong>. Ajaia must be contractually permitted to perform it. More directly: <code>§164.308(b)(2)</code> and <code>§164.502(e)(1)(ii)</code> require a business associate to obtain satisfactory assurances — a BAA — from any subcontractor that creates, receives, maintains, or transmits PHI on its behalf. Anthropic is such a subcontractor. The chain must be unbroken: covered entity → Harmoni → Ajaia → Anthropic.</p>
        <p>Disclosing PHI to a subcontractor without a BAA is an impermissible disclosure. Under <code>§164.402</code> an impermissible disclosure is <em>presumed</em> to be a reportable breach unless we document a four-factor risk assessment concluding low probability of compromise. We have no such documentation, and we could not currently produce the forensic facts to support one.</p>
        <p>Exposure: direct business-associate liability since the 2013 Omnibus Rule; willful-neglect penalty tiers reaching roughly $2.1M per violation category per year as adjusted; OCR has settled missing-BAA cases on their own facts, independent of any data loss. Add Harmoni's indemnity claim and the deal risk of a client discovering this before we disclose it.</p>

        <h4>4. What I recommend</h4>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Treat every Harmoni API payload as PHI by default.</strong> This is the correct posture regardless of what a de-identification review later concludes, and it costs us nothing operationally.</li>
          <li><strong>Execute a BAA with Anthropic before the next release</strong>, with zero-data-retention and no-training terms confirmed in writing. Anthropic offers this for the API; it is a procurement task, not an engineering one.</li>
          <li><strong>Confirm our BAA with Harmoni</strong> is in force and permits subcontracting, with flow-down obligations.</li>
          <li><strong>Do not pursue Safe Harbor over free text.</strong> If we want a de-identified processing path, commission an Expert Determination with measured re-identification risk — or use a Limited Data Set under <code>§164.514(e)</code> with a data use agreement as a middle path.</li>
          <li><strong>Apply minimum necessary</strong> to prompt construction: strip transport identifiers, send no more clinical context than the translation requires.</li>
          <li><strong>Put the determination in writing.</strong> A verbal legal conclusion with no documented analysis is precisely the fact pattern OCR characterizes as willful neglect. The written record is itself a control.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>None of this requires pausing the Harmoni integration. It requires one agreement, one documented determination, and one change to how we build prompts.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ PART B2 — FERPA ============ -->
  <section id="ferpa">
    <div class="part-head">
      <div class="part-kicker">Part B.2 · Ethos and FERPA</div>
      <h2>Does sending student performance data to OpenAI violate FERPA?</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
      <p class="lede">Not inherently. FERPA permits it — conditionally. The violation risk is not the act of sending data to a vendor; it is failing to satisfy the four conditions that make the disclosure lawful, and Ajaia likely fails at least two today.</p>

      <h4>The data is in scope</h4>
      <p>Quiz responses and performance data, maintained by or on behalf of a school and linked to an identifiable student, are education records containing personally identifiable information. FERPA's definition of PII at <code>34 CFR 99.3</code> is deliberately broad: it includes indirect identifiers and any information that, alone or in combination, would allow identification "with reasonable certainty." A pseudonymous student ID does not take data out of scope when Ajaia holds the mapping — a point worth noting because it is the same reasoning error the legal team made on HIPAA.</p>

      <h4>Ajaia's position: school official</h4>
      <p>The lawful basis is the school-official exception, <code>99.31(a)(1)(i)(B)</code>, which permits disclosure without parental consent to a contractor, consultant, or outsourced service provider if four conditions hold:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>The service is one the school would otherwise use its own employees to perform.</li>
        <li>The school <strong>retains direct control</strong> over the vendor's use and maintenance of the education records.</li>
        <li>The vendor meets <code>99.33(a)</code>: it uses the data only for the purpose of the disclosure and does not redisclose without authorization.</li>
        <li>The school has specified criteria for "legitimate educational interest" in its annual FERPA notification, <code>99.7</code>, and limits access accordingly.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Condition 4 is the district's duty, but it is Ajaia's risk — if a district's annual notification does not include outsourced service providers in its definition of school official, the exception does not cover us at that district. This belongs on the onboarding checklist.</p>

      <h4>Where OpenAI creates the problem</h4>
      <p>Ajaia is the school official. OpenAI is a <em>further</em> disclosure beyond the one the district authorized. FERPA has no subprocessor mechanism equivalent to HIPAA's subcontractor BAA; the only workable theory is that OpenAI acts as an extension of Ajaia's service, under the district's retained direct control, for the single fixed purpose of generating learning recommendations. That theory requires evidence, and the evidence is contractual.</p>
      <p>The sanction for getting this wrong is specific and severe: under <code>99.33(a)(2)</code>, where a party improperly rediscloses PII from education records, the Department of Education may bar the educational agency from disclosing information to that party for <strong>five years</strong>. That is not a fine. For Ajaia it is loss of the district relationship, and it is the single most consequential FERPA outcome to design against.</p>

      <h4>Controls required</h4>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Subprocessor authorization in the district contract.</strong> Confirm each agreement permits subprocessors, maintain a published subprocessor list, and give districts notice and an objection right before adding one.</li>
        <li><strong>A written data processing addendum with OpenAI</strong> covering: no use of data for model training or improvement; no use for any purpose other than providing the service; no onward disclosure; deletion on instruction; confidentiality; security measures; and zero data retention on the API endpoint. OpenAI's default API posture does not train on submitted data — but a default is a product setting, not a contractual commitment, and FERPA compliance is demonstrated through contract.</li>
        <li><strong>Demonstrable direct control.</strong> District audit rights, the ability to direct deletion and correction, a fixed and documented processing purpose, and no unilateral change of purpose by Ajaia.</li>
        <li><strong>Data minimization.</strong> Send pseudonymous student identifiers and only the performance fields the recommendation actually needs; hold the mapping table solely within Ajaia under separate access control. This does not change the legal classification, but it materially reduces breach impact and supports the reasonableness of the arrangement.</li>
        <li><strong>Prohibition on secondary use</strong> — no targeted advertising, no commercial profiling, no sale of data. Required by CA AB 1584 and SOPIPA, IL SOPPA, and most district riders.</li>
        <li><strong>Retention limits and deletion on district request</strong>, with the constraint at <code>99.10(e)</code> that records may not be destroyed while an access request is pending.</li>
        <li><strong>Encryption at rest and in transit</strong> — mandatory under NY Ed. Law §2-d and its Part 121 regulations, not discretionary. See the Part C critical finding.</li>
        <li><strong>Access limited to staff with a legitimate educational interest</strong>, plus an audit log of who accessed student data and what was transmitted to OpenAI.</li>
        <li><strong>State-law clock matrix.</strong> NY §2-d requires notice to the district within 7 calendar days of discovery; IL SOPPA requires 30 days to the school, which then has 30 days to notify parents. Ajaia's incident policy must encode the shortest applicable clock per district.</li>
        <li><strong>Records-production capability</strong> so a district can satisfy a parent's inspection request within 45 days under <code>99.10</code>, including any Ajaia-generated recommendation that has become part of the education record.</li>
        <li><strong>COPPA and automated-decision review.</strong> If Ethos serves students under 13, confirm the school-consent pathway is properly documented. If recommendations materially influence placement or grading, add human review and district-facing transparency about the automated component — increasingly a contract requirement and in some states a statutory one.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>In short: the architecture is defensible, the paperwork probably is not. Fix the contracts and the posture becomes sound.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ PART C ============ -->
  <section id="part-c">
    <div class="part-head">
      <div class="part-kicker">Part C · Policy gap analysis</div>
      <h2>Draft Incident Response Policy</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
      <p class="lede">Thirteen gaps. The two most consequential are not the obvious ones — it is not the 96-hour clock that would fail an audit, it is the absence of a breach determination step and a containment commitment the company cannot actually meet.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="findings">
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">IR-01</span>
          <span class="f-title">No breach risk assessment — the policy cannot determine whether a breach occurred</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-crit">Critical</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>The policy jumps from "confirmed breach" to notification with no procedure for reaching that confirmation. HIPAA presumes any impermissible use or disclosure of PHI <em>is</em> a breach unless the entity documents a four-factor risk assessment: the nature and extent of the PHI, who received or used it, whether it was actually acquired or viewed, and the extent of mitigation. Without this step Ajaia can neither justify notifying nor justify not notifying — and "confirmed breach" is left undefined, so the 96-hour clock has no start.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.402 (definition of breach and the four-factor exception); §164.414(b) (burden of proof on the entity)</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Add a documented breach determination procedure with the four factors, a defined discovery trigger, and a written record retained six years.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">IR-02</span>
          <span class="f-title">Blanket 4-hour containment and 7-day review for all incidents — unachievable, therefore a design deficiency</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-crit">Critical</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>There is no severity tiering and no definition distinguishing a security <em>incident</em> from a <em>breach</em>. HIPAA defines a security incident at <code>§164.304</code> to include <em>attempted</em> unauthorized access — so as drafted, every failed login requires CEO notification within an hour, containment within four, and a post-incident review within seven days. The policy will be ignored, and an ignored control is a control failure.</p>
          <p>This matters specifically for SOC 2 Type I, where the auditor tests control <em>design</em> against the commitments management asserts. A commitment the organization cannot perform is a design deficiency on its face. It is also discoverable: a self-imposed four-hour SLA that was missed becomes the plaintiff's exhibit in any subsequent litigation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>SOC 2 CC7.3–CC7.4 (incidents evaluated and responded to based on severity); 45 CFR §164.304 and §164.308(a)(6)(ii)</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Define Sev 1–4 with distinct response, containment, and review targets. Reserve the aggressive clocks for Sev 1. Separate "security event," "security incident," and "breach."</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">IR-03</span>
          <span class="f-title">No evidence preservation, forensics, or chain of custody</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-crit">Critical</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>Identified in the draft itself as absent. The consequence compounds IR-01: the four-factor assessment turns on whether PHI was <em>actually acquired or viewed</em>, which is a forensic question answerable only from preserved logs and images. A four-hour containment mandate with no preservation rule actively incentivizes engineers to wipe and rebuild, destroying the evidence needed to avoid notifying.</p>
          <p>Missing: a do-not-remediate-before-imaging rule, chain of custody, log retention minimums, legal hold, and a pre-negotiated external forensics retainer. HIPAA requires documenting security incidents <em>and their outcomes</em> — an outcome cannot be documented without facts.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.308(a)(6)(ii) (identify, respond, mitigate, document incidents and outcomes); §164.312(b) (audit controls); §164.316(b)(2)(i) (six-year retention); SOC 2 CC7.3–CC7.4 (root cause determination), CC4.2</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Preservation-before-remediation standard, 12-month minimum log retention, forensic retainer in place before it is needed.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">IR-04</span>
          <span class="f-title">Notification clocks ignore contractual and state-law obligations</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-high">High</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip f">FERPA</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>96 hours sits inside HIPAA's 60-day outer limit at <code>§164.410</code>, so it is not a direct Security Rule violation — but it almost certainly breaches the BAAs. Most covered entities contract this down to 24–72 hours because they need the remaining time to meet their own 60-day duty to individuals. More acutely, NY Ed. Law §2-d requires notice to the district within <strong>7 calendar days of discovery</strong>, and district riders commonly impose 24–48 hours. The policy sets one number and references no contract.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.410 (BA notification to covered entity); NY Education Law §2-d and 8 NYCRR Part 121; individual BAA and district contract terms</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> State "within 24 hours of discovery, or the shortest period required by applicable law or contract," and maintain a per-client clock matrix.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">IR-05</span>
          <span class="f-title">No regulatory or third-party notification obligations at all</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-high">High</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip f">FERPA</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>The policy addresses only the CEO and clients. Absent: HHS OCR notification support, the 500-plus-records trigger for media notice and contemporaneous HHS reporting, state attorney general breach statutes in all fifty states, state student-privacy notifications, cyber insurance carrier notice (late notice can void coverage), law enforcement coordination, and a single designated spokesperson with a communications hold.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.404–§164.408 (individual, media, and HHS notification); state breach notification statutes; IL SOPPA 105 ILCS 85/; policy terms of the cyber policy</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Add a notification matrix: audience, trigger, clock, owner, template, approval authority.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">IR-06</span>
          <span class="f-title">No roles, no incident commander, no escalation path beyond the CEO</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-high">High</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>"CEO notified within 1 hour" is the entire governance model. There is no incident commander, no technical lead, no communications or legal role, no on-call rotation, and no alternate when the CEO is unreachable — which creates a single point of failure on the one control with the tightest clock. HIPAA requires security responsibility to be formally assigned to a named individual.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.308(a)(2) (assigned security responsibility); SOC 2 CC1.3 (structures and authorities), CC2.2 (internal communication of responsibilities)</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Named roles with named alternates, a documented escalation tree, and 24/7 contact details maintained out of band.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">IR-07</span>
          <span class="f-title">No handling of incidents at Anthropic, OpenAI, or any other vendor</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-high">High</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip f">FERPA</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>Ajaia's highest-volume data flows run through third-party model APIs, and the policy is silent on what happens when the incident is theirs. No vendor incident intake, no obligation to assess and pass through downstream, no requirement that subcontractor agreements contain notification duties, no procedure for a vendor-side retention or disclosure failure. A breach at a subcontractor is still Ajaia's reportable breach.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.308(b) and §164.502(e) (subcontractor assurances and flow-down); 34 CFR §99.33(a) (redisclosure); SOC 2 CC9.2 (vendor and business partner risk)</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Vendor incident section with intake, assessment, downstream notification duty, and a requirement that every BAA and DPA carry a notification clock no longer than Ajaia's own.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">IR-08</span>
          <span class="f-title">No FERPA incident category for unauthorized disclosure without a security failure</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-high">High</span><span class="chip f">FERPA</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>The policy is framed entirely around security events. Sending student data to an unapproved subprocessor, retaining it past a district's deletion instruction, or using it for a purpose outside the disclosure is a FERPA violation with no breach, no attacker, and no security incident to detect — yet it carries the five-year disclosure bar under <code>99.33(a)(2)</code>. Nothing in the current policy would cause such an event to be reported, assessed, or disclosed to the district.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>34 CFR §99.33(a) (limits on redisclosure) and §99.33(a)(2) (five-year bar); 34 CFR §99.31(a)(1)(i)(B) (direct control)</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Add "privacy incident" as a first-class category covering unauthorized use, disclosure, and retention — with its own assessment and client notification path.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">IR-09</span>
          <span class="f-title">No detection or internal reporting channel</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-med">Medium</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>The policy describes what happens after an incident is known but never how it becomes known. No employee duty to report, no reporting channel, no intake SLA, no anonymous route, no monitoring or alerting requirement, no customer-reported path. The one-hour CEO clock starts from an undefined event.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.308(a)(5)(ii)(B)–(C) (protection from malicious software, log-in monitoring); §164.308(a)(6)(ii); SOC 2 CC2.3, CC7.1–CC7.2</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Define the reporting channel, a mandatory employee reporting duty, non-retaliation, and the detection controls that feed it.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">IR-10</span>
          <span class="f-title">No mitigation duty and no remediation tracking to closure</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-med">Medium</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>A post-incident review within seven days is required, but nothing requires mitigating the known harmful effects of a disclosure, assigning corrective actions, tracking them to completion, or verifying effectiveness. The review produces findings that nothing obliges anyone to act on.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.308(a)(6)(ii) (mitigate harmful effects) and §164.530(f); SOC 2 CC4.2 (deficiencies evaluated and communicated), CC7.5 (recovery and resolution)</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Mandatory mitigation step, a corrective action plan with named owners and due dates, and verification before closure.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">IR-11</span>
          <span class="f-title">No testing or tabletop requirement</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-med">Medium</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>An untested plan with a four-hour containment target is an assertion, not a capability. SOC 2 Type I can be achieved on design alone, but an auditor will ask how design was validated, and HIPAA requires periodic technical and non-technical evaluation of the whole program.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.308(a)(8) (periodic evaluation); §164.308(a)(7)(ii)(D) (testing and revision, contingency); SOC 2 CC7.5, CC4.1</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Annual tabletop minimum, documented, with findings feeding the policy review.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">IR-12</span>
          <span class="f-title">No documentation retention requirement</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-med">Medium</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>The policy never states that incident records, determinations, notifications, and reviews must be retained. HIPAA sets a hard six-year floor from creation or last effective date, and the burden of proving that notification was unnecessary rests on Ajaia — which is impossible without the record.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.316(b)(1)–(2) (written documentation, six-year retention); §164.414(b) (burden of proof)</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Six-year retention for all incident documentation, in a defined system of record.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">IR-13</span>
          <span class="f-title">No owner, approval, review cadence, or exception process</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-med">Medium</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>No named policy owner, no approving authority, no effective date, no version history, no annual review commitment, and no documented exception process. Scope says "all Ajaia systems and data" but does not reach personal devices, contractors, SaaS tools, or the model-API layer — so the broad statement is simultaneously unbounded and unenforceable.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.316(a) (maintain, review, and update policies); SOC 2 CC5.3 (policies deployed and reviewed), CC1.1</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Add a standard policy header and an annual review and re-approval cycle across the whole policy set.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:72px">Draft Data Classification Policy</h2>

    <div class="critical" id="critical">
      <div class="c-kicker">The classification decision that creates immediate regulatory risk</div>
      <h3>Student education records are classified Internal. Only Confidential data must be encrypted.</h3>
      <p>Read the two clauses together and the policy states, in Ajaia's own writing, that <strong>student education records are not required to be encrypted</strong> — at rest or in transit. That single inconsistency produces four distinct consequences, and the written policy is the evidence for all of them.</p>
      <div class="chain">
        <span>Education records → Internal</span><i>+</i>
        <span>Only Confidential encrypted</span><i>=</i>
        <span>Student PII exempted from encryption by policy</span>
      </div>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Direct breach of state student-privacy law.</strong> NY Education Law §2-d and 8 NYCRR Part 121 require encryption of student PII at rest and in transit. CA AB 1584 and SOPIPA and IL SOPPA require reasonable security measures. A policy that affirmatively excludes student data from the encryption requirement is not a gap in the record — it is a document that proves the inadequacy.</li>
        <li><strong>Breach of every district contract.</strong> Standard district data privacy agreements and the national Student Data Privacy Agreement template require encryption and a data security plan. This policy contradicts commitments Ajaia has already signed, in a document districts are entitled to request.</li>
        <li><strong>Collapse of the FERPA school-official basis.</strong> "Internal" conventionally means available to all employees. The school-official exception at <code>99.31(a)(1)(i)(A)</code> requires access to be limited to those with a legitimate educational interest, and <code>(B)</code> requires the district to retain direct control. A classification that grants company-wide access removes the limitation the exception is conditioned on — which is improper use, carrying the five-year disclosure bar under <code>99.33(a)(2)</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Loss of the breach notification safe harbor, and a SOC 2 design deficiency.</strong> Properly implemented NIST-conforming encryption renders data unusable and unreadable, which is what removes an incident from breach notification entirely. Unencrypted student records mean every lost laptop or exposed bucket becomes a notifiable event at full scope. For SOC 2, classification drives protection; a tier assignment that defeats the stated control is a design deficiency under CC6.1, CC6.7, and C1.1.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Remediation, this week:</strong> reclassify student education records as Confidential — better, create a fourth <em>Regulated</em> tier holding both PHI and education records, with mandatory encryption, agreement-gated vendor access, logical separation, and separate key custody. Verify current encryption state in production before assuming the policy is the only thing broken.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="findings">
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">DC-01</span>
          <span class="f-title">No handling procedures per tier — the policy classifies but does not protect</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-crit">Critical</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span><span class="chip f">FERPA</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>Identified in the draft itself as absent, and it is the gap that makes the rest inert. A classification scheme with no handling rules tells an employee what label applies and nothing about what to do differently. Required per tier: labeling, permitted storage locations, transmission rules, access approval, third-party sharing, printing, removable media, retention, destruction, and — specific to Ajaia — which tiers may be sent to which model APIs under which agreement.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.308(a)(3)–(4) (workforce access management and authorization); §164.310(d)(1)–(2) (device and media controls, disposal, re-use); §164.312(a)(2)(iv) and (e) (encryption and transmission security); SOC 2 CC6.1, CC6.5, CC6.7, C1.1–C1.2</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> A one-page handling matrix: tiers across the top, handling dimensions down the side, no blank cells.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">DC-02</span>
          <span class="f-title">No rules on which tiers may be sent to third-party AI APIs</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-crit">Critical</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip f">FERPA</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>This is the gap that produced Part B. Ajaia's core product architecture transmits regulated data to external model providers, and the classification policy — the document that should govern exactly that decision — does not mention it. With no rule, engineering made the call, and the determination was wrong.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.308(b)(1)–(2) and §164.502(e) (satisfactory assurances before disclosure to a subcontractor); 34 CFR §99.33(a) (redisclosure); SOC 2 CC9.2, CC6.1</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> An explicit rule: Regulated data may be transmitted to an external provider only where a BAA or DPA is executed, zero retention is confirmed, and the flow is recorded in the data inventory. Pair it with an AI acceptable-use standard.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">DC-03</span>
          <span class="f-title">"All Confidential data must be encrypted" is a sentence, not a control</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-high">High</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>No distinction between at rest and in transit, no algorithm or strength, no key management, no statement of who holds keys, no backup and log coverage. HIPAA treats encryption as <em>addressable</em>, which means Ajaia must either implement it or document why an equivalent alternative is reasonable — the policy does neither. It also omits the most commercially valuable fact about encryption: conforming encryption is what takes an incident out of breach notification altogether.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.312(a)(2)(iv), §164.312(e)(2)(ii) (addressable implementation specifications requiring documented decisions); §164.402(2) and HHS guidance on unsecured PHI; SOC 2 CC6.7</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Specify AES-256 at rest, TLS 1.2+ in transit, managed KMS with documented custody and rotation, and record the addressable-specification decision in writing.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">DC-04</span>
          <span class="f-title">Three tiers cannot separate regulated data from ordinary business confidences</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-high">High</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip f">FERPA</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>With PHI, board material, and the pricing sheet all sitting in one Confidential tier, the controls that only regulated data needs — BAA gating, residency limits, separate keys, segregation, statutory retention — either get applied to everything or to nothing. In practice, to nothing. The tier also fails to require that PHI and education records stay in separate logical stores, which they must: different lawful bases, different deletion triggers, different notification duties.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.308(a)(1)(ii)(A) (risk analysis by data type and location); §164.502(b) (minimum necessary); 34 CFR §99.31(a)(1)(i)(B) (direct control over education records); SOC 2 CC6.1, C1.1</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Add a fourth Regulated tier with mandatory segregation between PHI and education records.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">DC-05</span>
          <span class="f-title">No de-identified, anonymized, or limited data set category</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-high">High</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip f">FERPA</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>The policy provides no category for de-identified data and no rule for when data leaves scope. The two regimes use different tests — HIPAA's Safe Harbor or Expert Determination at <code>§164.514(b)</code>, FERPA's reasonable-determination standard at <code>99.31(b)</code> — and neither is satisfied by removing the identifiers an engineer happens to think of. Without a governed category, teams will keep self-certifying "we stripped PII," which is precisely how the Harmoni exposure arose.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.514(a)–(c) (de-identification standard and re-identification code); §164.514(e) (limited data set and data use agreement); 34 CFR §99.31(b) and §99.3</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Define the category, require documented determination by the compliance owner before any data is treated as out of scope, and prohibit self-certification by engineering.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">DC-06</span>
          <span class="f-title">No retention or disposal schedule per tier</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-high">High</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip f">FERPA</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>No retention periods, no disposal methods, no media sanitization standard, no deletion verification. The three regimes pull in opposite directions here — HIPAA mandates six-year documentation retention while FERPA requires deletion on district direction but <em>prohibits</em> destruction while a parental access request is pending. Reconciling them requires a schedule, not a principle.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.316(b)(2)(i) (six-year retention); §164.310(d)(2)(i)–(ii) (disposal and media re-use); 34 CFR §99.10(e) (no destruction while a request is pending); SOC 2 C1.2</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> A retention schedule by tier and data type, naming the legal basis for each period and the sanitization standard for disposal.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">DC-07</span>
          <span class="f-title">No data inventory tying classifications to real systems and flows</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-high">High</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>Classifications exist in the abstract with no map of where each type actually lives, which vendors touch it, and how it moves. HIPAA's required risk analysis cannot be performed without it — you cannot assess risk to ePHI whose locations you have not enumerated. It is also the artifact a SOC 2 auditor asks for first.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.308(a)(1)(ii)(A) (risk analysis across all ePHI the entity creates, receives, maintains, or transmits); SOC 2 CC3.2, CC6.1</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Maintained data inventory and flow map, reviewed at least annually and on any new vendor or integration.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">DC-08</span>
          <span class="f-title">No owner, no default classification, no reclassification process</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-med">Medium</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>No data owner or steward roles, no rule for who classifies new data, no labeling requirement, no declassification or reclassification procedure, and no stated default for unclassified data — which must default to Confidential, since a permissive default means every new dataset starts unprotected.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.308(a)(2) (assigned responsibility); §164.308(a)(3)(ii)(A) (authorization and supervision); SOC 2 CC2.1, CC6.1</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Named owners per data domain, mandatory labeling, a default of Confidential, and a documented reclassification path.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">DC-09</span>
          <span class="f-title">No prohibition on real regulated data in development and test</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-med">Medium</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip f">FERPA</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>Unaddressed, and near-universal at this stage of company: production PHI and student records copied into staging, local machines, notebooks, and prompt-evaluation fixtures, where none of the production controls apply. Prompt and response logs from model APIs deserve explicit mention — they are a regulated data store that no one classifies as one.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.502(b) (minimum necessary); §164.308(a)(4) (information access management); SOC 2 CC6.1, CC8.1 (change management)</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Prohibit regulated data outside production; require synthetic or de-identified fixtures; classify prompt and response logs explicitly.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">DC-10</span>
          <span class="f-title">Employee and general personal data is unclassified</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-med">Medium</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>The policy names only PHI and student records. Employee SSNs, payroll, benefits, background checks, and candidate data are regulated by all fifty state breach notification statutes and fall within the SOC 2 confidentiality and privacy criteria. A breach here is just as notifiable and lands on the same leadership team.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>State breach notification statutes (all 50 states); SOC 2 C1.1, P-series criteria where privacy is in scope</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Classify HR and personal data explicitly, at Confidential or above.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="f-top">
          <span class="f-id">DC-11</span>
          <span class="f-title">No sanctions for mishandling, and no data residency statement</span>
          <span class="chips"><span class="chip sev sev-med">Medium</span><span class="chip h">HIPAA</span><span class="chip s">SOC 2</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="f-body">
          <p>HIPAA explicitly requires a sanction policy for workforce members who fail to comply; the draft has none, and without it the classification rules carry no consequence. Residency is also unstated — health systems and districts frequently require US-only processing and storage, which constrains vendor and region selection and should be a policy constraint rather than a deal-desk surprise.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="f-cite"><b>Requirement not met</b><br>45 CFR §164.308(a)(1)(ii)(C) (sanction policy — a required, not addressable, specification); SOC 2 CC1.1, CC1.5 (accountability)</div>
        <div class="f-fix"><b>Fix</b> Add a sanction policy cross-referenced from the employee handbook, and state the data residency commitment.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <span>Ajaia · Compliance governance &amp; policy</span>
    <span>Citations: 45 CFR Parts 160 &amp; 164 · 34 CFR Part 99 · AICPA TSC 2017 (rev. 2022)</span>
    <span>Illustrative analysis — not legal advice</span>
  </footer>

</div>
"
  }
}
