```json
{
  "name": "Write",
  "input": {
    "file_path": "C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-staging\\SECURITY_REVIEW.md",
    "content": "# OBS Streaming Environment \u2014 Security & Reliability Review\n\nReview date: 2026-10-31\nScope: C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-tools, C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-overlays, C:\\Users\\Srini\\AppData\\Roaming\\obs-studio, C:\\Program Files\\obs-studio\\obs-plugins\\64bit, C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-backups\nMode: read-only. No files were modified, moved, or deleted during this review.\n\nAll secret values below are REDACTED. Locations are given as file path + line number so the value can be found and rotated.\n\n---\n\n## Ranked Findings\n\n### CRITICAL\n\n**C1. obs-websocket password stored in plaintext in at least 5 locations, and the service it unlocks is bound to ALL network interfaces, not loopback-only.**\n\n- `C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-tools\\autopilot.config.json:4` \u2014 `\"password\": \"<REDACTED, 24 chars>\"`\n- `C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-tools\\backup-pre-agentselect-012817\\autopilot.config.json:4` \u2014 same value, stray self-made backup *inside* obs-tools (not the official backup location, easy to forget about)\n- `C:\\Users\\Srini\\AppData\\Roaming\\obs-studio\\plugin_config\\obs-websocket\\config.json:5` \u2014 `\"server_password\": \"<REDACTED, same value>\"` (this is obs-websocket's own on-disk store; expected plugin behavior, but it's one more plaintext copy that any backup/sync tool will pick up)\n- `C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-backups\\20261031-160039-baseline-pre-overhaul\\obs-tools\\autopilot.config.json:4` and `...\\plugin_config\\obs-websocket\\config.json:5` \u2014 duplicated again by the baseline backup; every future `backup-obs.ps1` run will keep duplicating it\n\nNetwork exposure, confirmed directly from the OBS log (`AppData\\Roaming\\obs-studio\\logs\\2026-10-31 01-34-10.txt`):\n```\n01:34:13.832: [obs-websocket] [WebSocketServer::Start] Not locked to IPv4 bindings\n01:34:13.835: [obs-websocket] [WebSocketServer::Start] Server started successfully on port 4456. Possible connect address: 192.168.0.4\n```\nThis is the server's own startup banner: it is listening on **all interfaces** (0.0.0.0 / ::), not `127.0.0.1`, despite `autopilot.config.json`'s `\"host\": \"127.0.0.1\"` \u2014 that field only controls which address *autopilot's client* dials, it has no effect on where the obs-websocket *server* binds. obs-websocket's config.json / UI has no host-bind option at all; \"all interfaces\" is the plugin's only mode.\n\n`auth_required: true` is set, so a blind LAN attacker is stopped at the handshake \u2014 but the password that gates that handshake is sitting in plaintext in five files, three of which (`obs-tools`, the stray backup, and the baseline backup) are plain files with no OS-level encryption and get copied by `backup-obs.ps1` without redaction. Anyone who can read any one of those files, plus anyone on the same LAN/Wi-Fi segment at 192.168.0.4:4456, can authenticate and have full remote control of OBS (switch scenes, stop the stream, read the configured stream key once one is set, start a virtual camera, etc.).\n\n**Impact:** Full remote control of the streaming rig from anywhere on the local network, given either (a) local file read access, or (b) network+password access. Currently no stream key is configured so the most damaging leak (the Twitch stream key) isn't live yet, but the control-plane compromise is real today.\n\n**Remediation:**\n1. Rotate the obs-websocket password (Tools \u2192 WebSocket Server Settings \u2192 Generate Password) once the plaintext copies are being cleaned up.\n2. Move the password out of `autopilot.config.json` into a value that isn't committed/backed-up in the clear \u2014 e.g. Windows Credential Manager (`keyring` Python package) or an environment variable set outside the repo tree, read at process start.\n3. Delete `C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-tools\\backup-pre-agentselect-012817\\` once it's no longer needed \u2014 it's a second, forgotten plaintext copy outside of the sanctioned backup directory.\n4. If LAN exposure is not needed (it almost certainly isn't \u2014 autopilot, obs-meter.js, and all browser sources only ever dial 127.0.0.1), add a Windows Firewall **inbound block rule** for TCP 4456 from non-loopback sources, since obs-websocket itself cannot be told to bind to loopback only.\n5. Going forward, `backup-obs.ps1` should either exclude `plugin_config/obs-websocket/config.json` and `obs-tools/autopilot.config.json`, or the backup directory should be treated as equally sensitive as the live config (ACL'd, not synced to cloud storage, etc.).\n\n---\n\n**C2. A private StreamElements widget access token is embedded directly in a browser-source URL inside the scene collection, and is duplicated into two backups.**\n\n- `C:\\Users\\Srini\\AppData\\Roaming\\obs-studio\\basic\\scenes\\Main.json:374` \u2014 a `browser_source`'s `\"url\"` is `https://widgets.streamelements.com/host/<channel-id>/<REDACTED-widget-token, ~50 chars>` \u2014 the trailing path segment is StreamElements' per-widget access token; anyone who has it can view/control that overlay widget's config.\n- Same URL, same token, duplicated verbatim in `C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-tools\\backup-pre-agentselect-012817\\Main.json` and `C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-backups\\20261031-160039-baseline-pre-overhaul\\basic\\scenes\\Main.json`.\n\n**Impact:** This is exactly the risk the review brief called out: a secret embedded in a browser-source URL is saved into the scene collection JSON. If that file is ever shared (scene-collection export/import to get help from someone, a GitHub gist of the \"Main.json\" for troubleshooting, a screen-share of OBS's Sources panel properties dialog which shows the full URL in a text field), the token leaks. It also means every backup of OBS silently carries a live credential forward forever.\n\n**Remediation:**\n1. Regenerate the StreamElements widget URL/token from the StreamElements dashboard.\n2. Where possible, prefer local overlay files (as the rest of this setup already does via the HUD server) over remote widget URLs that embed auth in the path.\n3. Before sharing/exporting a scene collection or screenshotting Source Properties, scrub this field.\n\n---\n\n### HIGH\n\n**H1. The custom HUD server hands out the plaintext obs-websocket password over unauthenticated HTTP, to any process on the machine.**\n\n`C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-tools\\obs-autopilot.py:1297-1300`:\n```python\nif route == '/ws.json':\n    return self._json({\"host\": \"127.0.0.1\",\n                       \"port\": cfg['port'],\n                       \"password\": cfg['password']})\n```\nThere is no authentication check on `do_GET`, and no token/origin check \u2014 any local process (or, in principle, any page that can reach `127.0.0.1:4466`, including a non-OBS browser tab doing a background request) can `GET /ws.json` and read the password back in cleartext JSON. The code comment at lines 1291-1296 correctly reasons that this is *no more exposed than the config file it reads from* because the server is loopback-bound \u2014 that reasoning is sound for the network dimension, but it does not account for **other local processes/malware reading from a well-known, unauthenticated local HTTP endpoint**, which is a materially easier target than finding and parsing a JSON config file on disk, and is also reachable from any Chromium-origin web page the user has open elsewhere (any site, not just OBS's own browser sources, since nothing here checks `Origin`/`Referer`).\n\n**Impact:** Any other software running as the same Windows user (or a browser tab that manages to probe this port) can trivially obtain the obs-websocket password without filesystem access, widening who/what can get it beyond \"reads files in obs-tools.\"\n\n**Remediation:** Add a shared-secret header or `Origin` allow-list check on `/ws.json` (e.g. only serve it if `Origin` is `null`/`file://` or a known OBS-browser user agent string is present), or require a one-time token minted at autopilot startup and injected into the overlay HTML server-side instead of being fetchable by an arbitrary GET.\n\n**H2. obs-websocket's LAN exposure (see C1) combined with no Origin/CSWSH protection assessment.**\n\nobs-websocket is a closed-source compiled plugin (`obs-websocket.dll`); I could not inspect its source for Origin-header validation on the WebSocket handshake. What's confirmed is: it binds all interfaces (C1), and the WebSocket protocol does not get CORS preflight protection the way `fetch()` does. If obs-websocket does not validate `Origin`, then **any web page the user visits in their normal browser** \u2014 not just OBS browser sources \u2014 could attempt `new WebSocket('ws://192.168.0.4:4456')` or `ws://127.0.0.1:4456` from JavaScript; the only thing stopping it from succeeding is the password-based `authentication` challenge, which requires knowing the plaintext password already covered in C1. This is a DNS-rebinding-class risk if the password ever leaks: the attack surface is \"connect from any page\" rather than \"connect from the local machine only.\"\n\n**Remediation:** Treat password secrecy (C1) as the only real control here, since the plugin offers no bind-address or Origin controls. Firewalling TCP 4456 from non-loopback sources (C1 remediation #4) removes the remote half of this risk; it does not remove the \"malicious page open in the user's own browser\" half, which again comes back to not leaking the password (C1, H1).\n\n**H3. Risk from StreamElements/other-widget session cookies swept into every backup.**\n\n`C:\\Users\\Srini\\AppData\\Roaming\\obs-studio\\plugin_config\\obs-browser\\Network\\Cookies` is a live Chromium cookie database for the embedded browser-source engine, encrypted at rest via Windows DPAPI (`LocalPrefs.json` shows an `os_crypt.encrypted_key` entry). `backup-obs.ps1:14` copies the entire `plugin_config` tree, including this database, into every backup. DPAPI ties the encryption to this Windows user profile, so the practical risk is low as long as backups stay on this machine under this account \u2014 but if a backup is ever moved to another machine/profile (cloud sync, USB transfer \"for safekeeping\"), the cookie values become unrecoverable garbage (not a security hole) while still being dead weight; more importantly, if an attacker already has code execution as this Windows user, the DPAPI protection is moot and these cookies (for streamelements.com and anything else a browser source ever visited) are exposed same as any other secret here.\n\n**Remediation:** Informational \u2014 no action required unless backups leave this machine/account. If they do, exclude `plugin_config\\obs-browser\\Network\\Cookies*` and `Local Storage` from the backup, or explicitly accept that risk.\n\n---\n\n### MEDIUM\n\n**M1. Twitch IRC `color` tag is concatenated into an HTML `style` attribute without escaping.**\n\n`C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-overlays\\chatbox.html:169`:\n```js\n'<span class=\"who\" style=\"color:' + (color || pickColor(name)) + '\">' + esc(name) + '</span>'\n```\n`color` is `tags.color` from the raw Twitch IRC tags (`chatbox.html:216`), and unlike the message body (`esc()` used, see M-info below) and the display name (`esc(name)`), it is **not** passed through `esc()` before being spliced into an HTML attribute. Twitch's server is expected to only ever send a `#rrggbb` hex value or an empty string for this tag, so exploitation requires either a bug/compromise on Twitch's IRC-tag validation or a modified/relay client sending a crafted tag value \u2014 but the page has no defense of its own if that assumption is ever violated. A crafted value containing a `\"` could close the `style` attribute early and inject arbitrary attributes/markup into a source that is live on stream.\n\n**Impact (if the upstream assumption fails):** XSS/markup injection into a visible, on-stream overlay.\n\n**Remediation:** Escape `color` the same way `name` and the message body are escaped (`esc(color)`), or validate it against `/^#[0-9a-f]{6}$/i` and fall back to `pickColor(name)` otherwise. Trivial, low-risk fix; do it regardless of how likely the upstream bug is.\n\n**M1-info (verified safe).** The chat message body itself IS correctly escaped before being placed into `innerHTML`: `twitchBody()` (`chatbox.html:234-257`) runs every text segment through `esc()` (`chatbox.html:156-157`, escapes `& < > \"`) both around and between emote `<img>` substitutions, and the display name is escaped at `chatbox.html:169` via `esc(name)`. This is the one part of the review brief (\"a chatbox rendering untrusted viewer chat via innerHTML is a real risk\") that was checked carefully and found to be implemented correctly, aside from the `color` attribute gap above.\n\n**M2. YouTube Data API key is designed to travel through the browser-source URL (architectural gap, not currently exploited).**\n\n`C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-overlays\\chatbox.html:338,342`:\n```js\nconst tw = Q.get('twitch'), ytKey = Q.get('yt_key'), ytVid = Q.get('yt_video');\n...\nif (ytKey && ytVid) startYouTube(ytKey, ytVid);\n```\nThe YouTube Data API key is read straight from the OBS browser-source URL query string (`?yt_key=...`), which \u2014 exactly like C2 \u2014 gets written verbatim into the scene collection JSON and into every properties-dialog view/screenshot. **This is not currently exploited**: `yt_key`/`yt_video` do not appear anywhere in `Main.json`, so the YouTube chat path is not configured today. But the design means the moment someone configures it, the key is in the scene collection in plaintext, with no equivalent to the HUD-server indirection that `obs-meter.js` uses for the obs-websocket password.\n\n**Remediation:** Route the YouTube key the same way `obs-meter.js` routes the obs-websocket password \u2014 serve it from `/ws.json`-style endpoint on the already-loopback-bound HUD server, keyed off `autopilot.config.json`, instead of a URL query parameter.\n\n**M3. Valorant PUUID + Riot ID stored in `identity.json`, and used with TLS verification disabled.**\n\n- `C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-tools\\identity.json` \u2014 contains a durable Riot `puuid` and Riot ID (`name#tag`). This is not a password, but a PUUID is a stable, reusable identifier that (with the right Riot API access) can be used to pull match history/rank; the Riot ID is directly searchable in-game.\n- Verified the PUUID/name is **not** rendered into any overlay HTML and is not broadcast on stream (checked all of `obs-overlays/*.html` for `identity`/`puuid`/`riot` \u2014 no matches); it's only consumed internally by `obs-autopilot.py` to match \"me\" in match data (`obs-autopilot.py:879-908`). The HUD server that exposes derived state is loopback-only (see Network section).\n- `obs-autopilot.py:688-692` disables TLS verification (`check_hostname = False`, `verify_mode = ssl.CERT_NONE`) when talking to Riot's local client API. This is the standard, necessary workaround for Riot's self-signed local certificate and the target is always `127.0.0.1` (`obs-autopilot.py:698`), so it is not itself exploitable remotely \u2014 flagged here only for completeness, not as an action item.\n\n**Impact:** Low on its own (not broadcast, loopback-only consumption); becomes Medium only in combination with C1/H1, since `identity.json` sits in the same directory and backups as the password and gets swept into the same backup copies.\n\n**Remediation:** No urgent action; if `obs-tools` is ever made shareable (e.g. put in a public git repo), exclude `identity.json` same as the config file.\n\n**M4. `obs-backgroundremoval` is listed as an installed plugin but has no corresponding DLL under `C:\\Program Files\\obs-studio\\obs-plugins\\64bit\\`.**\n\nIts plugin-config folder exists (`AppData\\Roaming\\obs-studio\\plugin_config\\obs-backgroundremoval\\`), and it was copied into the baseline backup, but no `*background*`/`*remov*`-named `.dll` exists anywhere under `C:\\Program Files\\obs-studio\\` (checked recursively). Either the plugin was uninstalled without its config being cleaned up, or it is not actually providing background removal today (NVIDIA's bundled `nv-filters.dll`, which IS present and signed by OBS Project, may be covering that use case instead). This is a functionality/provenance gap, not a security hole \u2014 flagged so the plugin inventory used for planning matches reality.\n\n**Remediation:** Confirm whether background removal is expected to work; if the plugin is gone, delete its orphaned config folder; if it's expected, reinstall it and re-run the signature check below.\n\n---\n\n### LOW\n\n**L1. `input-overlay`'s WebSocket server is configured to bind `0.0.0.0` (latent, currently disabled).**\n\n`C:\\Users\\Srini\\AppData\\Roaming\\obs-studio\\global.ini`, `[input-overlay]` section:\n```\nwss_address=0.0.0.0\nwss_port=16899\nenable_wss=false\n```\n`enable_wss=false`, so this is not currently listening \u2014 but if a future \"enable remote input display\" toggle is flipped, it will bind to all interfaces by default. Note also that the bundled preset `input-overlay-presets-510\\input-history-windows\\input-history-windows.html:1059` connects to `ws://localhost:16899/`, consistent with this being meant for local use only; there's no reason the server itself needs `0.0.0.0`.\n\n**Remediation:** Change `wss_address` to `127.0.0.1` now, while it's inert, so a future \"turn it on\" doesn't silently reopen a LAN-facing port.\n\n**L2. `autopilot-launcher.lua:27` uses `os.execute()` with string-concatenated, double-quoted paths.**\n\n```lua\nos.execute('start \"\" \"' .. PYTHONW .. '\" \"' .. AUTOPILOT .. '\"')\n```\nBoth `PYTHONW` and `AUTOPILOT` are hardcoded constants in the same file (lines 15-16), not derived from any external/untrusted input, so there is no injection vector today. Flagged only because the pattern (building a shell command by string concatenation) is fragile \u2014 if either path constant is ever parameterized from OBS script settings or an environment variable, this becomes an injection point. No action required unless the script is changed to take configurable paths.\n\n**L3. No other `shell=True`, `os.system`, `eval`, or `exec` usage found.**\n\nSearched every `.py` file in `obs-tools\\` (`add_input_overlay.py`, `add_overlays.py`, `audit-audio.py`, `audit-full-files.py`, `audit-full.py`, `audit.py`, `layout.py`, `layout_inputs.py`, `migrate-drop-agent-select.py`, `obs-autopilot.py`, `rename_scenes.py`, `scan_orphans.py`, `sharpen_overlays.py`) for `subprocess`, `os.system`, `shell=True`, `eval(`, `exec(`, `os.popen`. The only `subprocess.run` call (`audit-full-files.py:112`) passes a list of literal arguments (no `shell=True`) and takes no runtime/untrusted input \u2014 safe. No path-traversal issues found in file writes; all file paths written by `obs-autopilot.py` (`autopilot.config.json`, `identity.json`, daily-stats JSON, clip log, Minecraft stats) are built from fixed constants (`HERE`, `os.path.join`) or from the Minecraft log path derived from `%LOCALAPPDATA%`/`.minecraft` conventions in `_mc_log_path(cfg)`, not from untrusted network input.\n\n---\n\n## Network Service Audit \u2014 Definitive Answers\n\n| Service | Configured/intended | Actual bind, as evidenced | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| OBS WebSocket (obs-websocket) | `autopilot.config.json` dials `127.0.0.1:4456` | OBS log, `2026-10-31 01-34-10.txt`: *\"Not locked to IPv4 bindings\"* / *\"Possible connect address: 192.168.0.4\"* on port 4456 | **LAN-exposed.** obs-websocket always binds all interfaces; there is no bind-address setting in its config.json or UI. `auth_required: true` + `server_password` set (plugin_config\\obs-websocket\\config.json) is the only control. |\n| Custom HUD HTTP server (obs-autopilot.py, port 4466) | \u2014 | `obs-autopilot.py:1315`: `srv = ThreadingHTTPServer(('127.0.0.1', cfg['hud_port']), Handler)` | **Loopback-only, by explicit code.** Confirmed directly from source \u2014 the bind tuple is hardcoded to `'127.0.0.1'`. |\n\nNeither service was running at the time of this review (`tasklist` showed no `obs64.exe`/`pythonw.exe`, and `netstat -ano | findstr LISTENING` showed nothing on 4456/4466), so live-port verification wasn't possible; the above is based on source code (HUD server) and the most recent OBS session's own startup log (obs-websocket), which is first-party evidence of actual bind behavior, not speculation.\n\nOther listening ports found via `netstat -ano | findstr LISTENING` at review time: standard Windows services only (135/445 RPC/SMB, ephemeral 49664-49671 RPC, 192.168.0.4:139 NetBIOS) plus two unrelated loopback services on 9010/9180/45654 (PID 12264/3340 \u2014 not identified as streaming-related, likely a background app; out of scope but noted in case the lead wants to check PID 12264/3340 ownership). Nothing streaming-related was listening because nothing was running.\n\n**Is there authentication on the HUD server?** No. `do_GET` (obs-autopilot.py:1287-1301) serves `/state.json` and `/ws.json` to any caller with no check at all. `/ws.json` returns the obs-websocket password in cleartext (H1). Input validation is minimal: the only dynamic routing is `self.path.split('?')[0]` string comparison against two literal routes, falling through to `SimpleHTTPRequestHandler`'s static file serving (which itself guards against `..` path traversal as part of Python's stdlib) for everything else \u2014 no user-controlled path construction was found.\n\n**Could a malicious page reach these via CSRF/DNS rebinding?** For the HUD server: a cross-origin page can send a GET to `/ws.json` (no preflight needed), and while it cannot *read* the JSON response due to lack of CORS headers (the browser treats it as an opaque response), this does not fully rule out side-channel leakage and does nothing to stop a page that IS same-origin (any OBS browser source) or a local process. For obs-websocket: see H2 \u2014 the real protection is password secrecy, not network topology, since the plugin binds all interfaces and WebSocket connections aren't subject to the same-origin/CORS model fetch() uses.\n\n---\n\n## Code Review \u2014 Injection & Unsafe Execution\n\nReviewed: `obs-autopilot.py`, `autopilot-launcher.lua`, `add_overlays.py`, `layout.py`, plus every other `.py` in `obs-tools\\`.\n\n- **No** `subprocess(..., shell=True)`, **no** `os.system`, **no** `eval(`/`exec(` found anywhere in the reviewed scripts (L3 above has the full file list and detail).\n- **No** command built from untrusted HTTP-response or Valorant-local-API data. The Valorant client's responses (match state, `puuid`, `game_name`) only ever flow into: in-memory dict fields, `json.dump()` calls to fixed local paths (`identity.json`, daily-stats files), and string formatting for *log messages* (`log(\"valorant state: %s\" % ...)` etc.) \u2014 never into a filename, a shell command, or a `eval`/`exec` call.\n- `autopilot-launcher.lua:27` (L2) and `audit-full-files.py:112` (L3) are the only process-launching code paths; both use fixed, non-attacker-controlled arguments.\n- TLS verification is deliberately disabled for the Riot local API only (`obs-autopilot.py:688-692`), scoped to `127.0.0.1` requests (M3) \u2014 a known-necessary pattern for that API, not a general weakening.\n- File writes are all to fixed, hardcoded paths under `obs-tools\\` or user-derived standard locations (`%LOCALAPPDATA%\\VALORANT\\...`, Minecraft's own `latest.log`), with no user/network-supplied path segments \u2014 no path-traversal vector found.\n\n**Conclusion:** the Python/Lua automation is written defensively with respect to injection; the real issues in this review are credential handling (C1/C2/H1) and front-end escaping (M1), not command/code injection.\n\n---\n\n## Overlay HTML Review\n\nReviewed: `overlay.html`, `cam-bg.html`, `chatbox.html`, `mc-hud.html`, `minecraft.html`, `obs-meter.js`, plus `reactive.html`, `scoreboard.html`, `crosshair.html` for the same patterns.\n\n- **chatbox.html** \u2014 the one page rendering untrusted third-party (viewer) content via `innerHTML`. Message bodies and display names are correctly HTML-escaped (M1-info); the `color` IRC tag is not (M1) \u2014 fix recommended but low likelihood of exploitation since it depends on Twitch's own tag sanitization holding.\n- **obs-meter.js** \u2014 deliberately avoids putting the obs-websocket password in any browser-source URL, fetching it instead from the loopback-only HUD server's `/ws.json` (see its own code comment, lines 14-19); this is the *right* pattern and is exactly what M2's YouTube-key gap and C2's StreamElements-URL gap should be changed to match.\n- `scoreboard.html:292`, `reactive.html:128`, `crosshair.html:92` use `innerHTML`/clearing with fixed template strings built from the app's own `LATEST` state (sourced from the loopback HUD server, not directly from any remote/third-party input) \u2014 no escaping gap found there (the risky external-input path is specifically the Twitch IRC feed in chatbox.html).\n- No hardcoded credentials found in any overlay HTML/JS file.\n- No remote `<script src=\"...\">` tags were found loading code from third-party CDNs in any of the reviewed overlay files (chatbox.html's only remote network calls are `fetch()`/`WebSocket` to Twitch IRC and the YouTube Data API, both expected and user/viewer-facing, not script-loading).\n\n---\n\n## Third-Party Plugin Signature Summary\n\n`Get-AuthenticodeSignature` run against every `.dll` in `C:\\Program Files\\obs-studio\\obs-plugins\\64bit\\`:\n\n**Signed (Valid):**\n- All core OBS Project plugins (obs-browser, obs-websocket, obs-filters, obs-transitions, win-capture, win-dshow, win-wasapi, rtmp-services, image-source, text-freetype2, obs-ffmpeg, obs-x264, obs-nvenc, obs-qsv11, obs-vst, obs-webrtc, nv-filters, frontend-tools, libcef, libEGL, libGLESv2, chrome_elf, coreaudio-encoder, vlc-video) \u2014 signed `CN=\"OBS Project, LLC\"`.\n- **aitum-multistream.dll** \u2014 `CN=Aitum Ltd`\n- **audio-monitor.dll, move-transition.dll, source-clone.dll, source-record.dll, scene-collection-manager.dll** \u2014 all `CN=Exeldro` (the well-known prolific OBS plugin author; consistent single-publisher signing across this set is a reasonable provenance signal)\n- **logi_obs_plugin_x64.dll** \u2014 `CN=Logitech Inc`\n- Microsoft ONNX runtime DLLs \u2014 `CN=Microsoft Corporation`\n\n**Not signed:**\n- **downstream-keyer.dll**, **obs-shaderfilter.dll**, **input-overlay.dll**, **obs-localvocal.dll**, **streamup-scene-as-transition.dll**\n- Supporting libraries for obs-localvocal's local Whisper model: `whisper.dll`, `ggml*.dll` (8 variants), `icudt76.dll`, `icuin76.dll`, `icuuc76.dll`, `libopenblas.dll`, `ctranslate2.dll`, `SDL2.dll`\n\n**Assessment (not alarmist):** an unsigned DLL is common and not inherently malicious \u2014 many well-regarded OBS community plugins (including some from the same authors who DO sign other plugins here, e.g. `obs-shaderfilter` is a long-standing, widely-used community plugin) ship unsigned because Authenticode certificates cost money and most small OBS-plugin authors don't carry one. The pattern here \u2014 core OBS and the larger/commercial-adjacent plugins (Aitum, Logitech, Exeldro's own suite) signed, smaller single-purpose or ML-bundling plugins unsigned \u2014 matches the OBS plugin ecosystem norm and isn't itself a red flag. The one thing worth independently verifying is **provenance of the binaries, not just the signature**: confirm `downstream-keyer.dll`, `obs-shaderfilter.dll`, `input-overlay.dll`, and `obs-localvocal.dll` were downloaded from their respective official GitHub release pages (not a third-party mirror), since lack of a signature means Windows SmartScreen/AV reputation won't catch a tampered copy the way it would for a signed-and-revoked one.\n\n**obs-backgroundremoval** could not be assessed \u2014 see M4; no DLL found to check.\n\n---\n\n## Backup & Rollback Validation\n\n### What `backup-obs.ps1` actually captures (verified against `20261031-160039-baseline-pre-overhaul`)\n\nConfirmed present and non-empty: `basic\\` (897 files total under the backup, 58 MB) including `basic\\scenes\\Main.json` (+ `.bak`) and all 4 profile folders with their `service.json`/`streamEncoder.json`/`recordEncoder.json`; `plugin_config\\` (9 subfolders incl. `obs-websocket`, `aitum-multistream`, `obs-browser`); `global.ini` (709 bytes); `user.ini` (2868 bytes); `obs-tools\\` (full copy of the tools, including `autopilot.config.json`, `identity.json`); `obs-overlays\\` (full copy); `installed-plugins.txt` (1059 bytes \u2014 plugin DLL *names* only); `obs-version.txt` (8 bytes \u2014 OBS product version string). All key files were non-zero-byte at inspection time; the backup is real and usable, not a stub.\n\n### What's MISSING for a true restore\n\n1. **The plugin DLLs themselves are not backed up \u2014 only their filenames.** `installed-plugins.txt` records *what was installed*, but `backup-obs.ps1` never copies `C:\\Program Files\\obs-studio\\obs-plugins\\64bit\\*.dll` or any installer. If a plugin update breaks something, there is nothing in this backup to roll the binary back to \u2014 you'd need to re-download the exact prior version from the vendor, which the filename list alone doesn't version-pin (no version string per plugin, no hash).\n2. **`AppData\\Roaming\\obs-studio\\plugin_manager\\`** (referenced by `global.ini`'s `[Locations] PluginManagerSettings=`) is not backed up. This holds the OBS Plugin Manager's own install/update bookkeeping; without it, a restore may show plugins as needing re-registration with the Plugin Manager even though the DLLs are back in place.\n3. **No OBS installer / version-pinned binary is saved.** `obs-version.txt` records the version string but not an installer; if OBS itself needs reinstalling (not just its config), you're dependent on downloading whatever is currently \"latest\" from obs project, which may not match the backed-up config/plugin set.\n4. **No restore verification step.** The script reports file/byte counts for the *backup* but there's no corresponding script that re-applies it and confirms OBS actually launches and loads the scene collection. (This is normal for a backup script, but worth calling out since the review asked specifically whether a *restore* is validated \u2014 it isn't, by anything currently in the repo.)\n5. The backup, by copying `obs-tools` and `plugin_config` wholesale, also perpetuates C1/C2/H3 forward \u2014 not a missing *capability*, but a missing *safeguard* (no secret-scrubbing step).\n\n### Rollback Procedure\n\n*(Document only \u2014 nothing below was executed as part of this review.)*\n\n#### A. Restore scene collection only\n1. Close OBS completely.\n2. Copy `<backup>\\basic\\scenes\\Main.json` \u2192 `C:\\Users\\Srini\\AppData\\Roaming\\obs-studio\\basic\\scenes\\Main.json`, overwriting the live file. (Optional safety: first copy the *current* live `Main.json` to `Main.json.pre-rollback` so today's state isn't lost if the backup turns out to be the wrong one.)\n3. Start OBS, open Scene Collection menu, confirm \"Main\" loads without an \"unsafe scene file\" warning. If OBS shows a recovery prompt instead, choose \"Load Backup\" pointing at the restored file, not the autosave.\n4. Spot-check: scene list matches expected names, each browser source's Properties dialog shows the expected `http://127.0.0.1:4466/...` URLs (no blank/broken sources), and downstream-keyer/aitum-multistream settings (if scene-collection-scoped) are present.\n\n#### B. Restore a single profile only\n1. Close OBS.\n2. Identify the target profile folder name under `<backup>\\basic\\profiles\\` (one of: `Archive - High Quality`, `Fallback - Lightweight`, `Stream and Record`, `Stream Only - No Record`).\n3. Copy that one folder \u2192 `C:\\Users\\Srini\\AppData\\Roaming\\obs-studio\\basic\\profiles\\<same name>`, overwriting in place. Do not touch the other three profiles.\n4. Start OBS, Profile menu \u2192 select the restored profile, confirm video/output settings match expectations (canvas 1920x1080, 60fps, the encoder settings the project's own `audit-full-files.py` checks for).\n5. If the profile's `service.json` has a stream key configured, verify it still matches what's expected on the streaming platform's dashboard \u2014 restoring an old profile can silently restore an old/revoked key.\n\n#### C. Full restore (disaster recovery)\n1. Close OBS; if it won't close cleanly, end `obs64.exe` via Task Manager.\n2. Rename the live `C:\\Users\\Srini\\AppData\\Roaming\\obs-studio` folder to `obs-studio.broken` (don't delete \u2014 keeps a fallback).\n3. Create a fresh `obs-studio` folder at that path.\n4. Copy from `<backup>\\` into the new `obs-studio\\` folder: `basic\\` (whole tree), `plugin_config\\` (whole tree), `global.ini`, `user.ini`.\n5. Copy `<backup>\\obs-tools\\` \u2192 `C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-tools\\` and `<backup>\\obs-overlays\\` \u2192 `C:\\Users\\Srini\\obs-overlays\\` (overwrite live copies; if live has newer work not in this backup, diff first).\n6. Reinstall OBS Studio itself if it's not already present/working, matching (as closely as possible) the version in `<backup>\\obs-version.txt`.\n7. Reinstall each plugin listed in `<backup>\\installed-plugins.txt` from its vendor source (this backup cannot do this step for you \u2014 see Missing #1). Re-run the signature check in this review against the freshly installed DLLs to confirm you got genuine builds.\n8. Start OBS. Confirm: Scene Collection = Main loads, all 4 Profiles are present and selectable, Tools \u2192 WebSocket Server Settings shows the expected port/auth state, Tools \u2192 Scripts shows `autopilot-launcher.lua` loaded (re-add it via \"+\" if the scripts list doesn't survive \u2014 OBS script registration under newer versions is scene-collection-scoped in `Main.json`, which was restored in step 4, so this should already be present; verify rather than assume).\n9. Run `run-autopilot.bat` (or let the Lua script auto-launch it) and confirm `autopilot.log` shows a clean startup and the