    "content": "# Besecstasy#GOST\u30b8 \u2014 Gotham Broadcast Overhaul: Creative Concepts\n\nPrepared as design work only. No file in `obs-overlays/` or the OBS scene collection was modified to produce this document. Scene/source names below were cross-checked against the live collection at `C:\\Users\\Srini\\AppData\\Roaming\\obs-studio\\basic\\scenes\\Main.json` (read-only) so the naming is exact, not guessed.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Visual Identity Assessment\n\n### What's genuinely strong \u2014 preserve this, don't touch it\n\n**The palette discipline is the single best asset in this build.** `gotham.css` states the rule explicitly and the rest of the codebase actually obeys it: every colour is sampled from the emblem \u2014 black, burnt orange, amber, gold, brass \u2014 and the comment calls out, correctly, that a \"neutral\" grey with its blue channel highest reads as blue on a dark ground. `scoreboard.html` even has an inline comment fixing exactly that bug (`--accent2 ... was a cool steel`). This is a team that already caught and corrected its own cool-tint drift once. Any new overlay must pass the same test: sample from the emblem, vary by value/tint only, never by hue.\n\n**The chrome-bevel + ink-panel system (`.chrome`, `.panel`) is reused everywhere** \u2014 `chatbox.html`, `titlebar.html`, `screens.html` \u2014 via one shared gradient recipe (padded gradient, not border-image, so the corner radius and highlight track together). That's a real design system, not copy-pasted CSS. New overlays should extend `.chrome`, not invent a second frame language.\n\n**The spotlight/fog/rain/grain primitives read as one cinematic language**, and they're built cheaply: CSS gradients and `repeating-linear-gradient`, no video textures, no canvas. `screens.html`'s 3D-spun emblem (`#spin`, `.face.front/.back`, the Z-stacked `.slice` extrusion) is a genuinely impressive touch for pure CSS \u2014 a licensed 2D logo reads as a machined medallion rotating in space, lit by a `--lit` custom property keyed to the same timeline as the spin. This is above the bar for \"just an overlay.\"\n\n**`obs-meter.js` is architecturally correct.** It pulls real levels from OBS's own obs-websocket meters rather than doing browser-side audio capture (which browser sources can't do anyway), degrades silently to `--glow: 0` if the socket never connects, and resolves its password from the autopilot's `/ws.json` instead of embedding a credential in every browser-source URL (which would otherwise get saved in cleartext into the scene collection). Every reactive surface \u2014 title bar, screens, cam frame, chat accent \u2014 reads off one `--glow` value set on `<html>`. One signal, many consumers. That's the right pattern for all future audio-reactive work.\n\n**`stinger.html`'s timing model is the most technically careful file in the set.** The comment block explaining *why* the animation (`?ms=2000`) is deliberately shorter than the transition duration (2600ms) \u2014 to absorb browser-source reload latency as \"tail\" slack after the visual is already fully transparent \u2014 shows real production debugging, not guesswork. The asymmetric intro/outro (soft shutter-close vs. hard snap-open with a light-streak bleed) is a genuine piece of motion design. Nobody should \"simplify\" this file without re-reading that comment first.\n\n**The naming convention (`CAP-/CAM-/HUD-/IN-/FX-/AUD-/ALR-/SCR-/GRP-`) is followed with zero exceptions** across all 33 sources in the live collection. This alone makes the whole rig legible at a glance in the OBS source list, and it's the thing most streamers never bother to do. Every proposal below keeps it intact.\n\n**Minecraft gets a genuinely separate visual language (`minecraft.html`) rather than a recolour** \u2014 hard pixel grid, stepped-block border via `repeating-linear-gradient` conic steps, corner torches with per-torch flicker offsets so they never pulse in sync, embers that respect the game's own safe-area (bottom-centre hotbar, bottom-left chat are explicitly left untouched). That safe-area discipline is also why the crosshair is correctly left alone \u2014 it's a precision tool, not decoration, and the existing rig already treats the game's own UI as sacred ground to design around rather than over.\n\n**Per-game accent variables (`[data-accent=\"valorant\"]`, `\"finals\"`, `\"solo\"`) stay inside the same hue family** and differ only by value/tint \u2014 `titlebar.html`, `chatbox.html`, `screens.html`, `reactive.html` all implement this identically. Consistent, extensible, and it means a fifth game (GeoGuessr, iRacing) is a four-line CSS addition, not a redesign.\n\n### What's dated or inconsistent\n\n- **No accent exists yet for GeoGuessr or iRacing.** The `[data-accent]` pattern is ready for them but nobody's written the two extra rules. Trivial to fix, flagged here so it doesn't get missed when those games actually get scenes.\n- **The licensed-emblem fallback chain is duplicated by hand in six different files** (`stinger.html`, `screens.html`, `titlebar.html`, `reactive.html`, `cam-bg.html`, `overlay.html`) \u2014 each one reimplements the same `img.onerror` cascade over `['assets/emblem.svg', 'assets/emblem.png', 'assets/emblem-fallback.svg']`. It works, but it's the one piece of real duplication in an otherwise disciplined codebase, and a future emblem-path change means editing six files instead of one. Not urgent, but worth a shared tiny `emblem.js` eventually.\n- **`scoreboard.html` has no `<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"gotham.css\">`** \u2014 it reimplements its own `:root` palette, its own font-face, its own body reset. It still lands in the right palette by hand, but it's drifted out of the shared system syntactically (it even redefines `--bat`/`--ink`/`--bone`, which `gotham.css` already defines identically). Low risk today because the values match, but it's the one file that would silently diverge if the shared palette ever changed.\n- **Three stale `.pre-*` backup files sit in the live folder** (`minecraft.html.pre-margin`, `overlay.html.pre-margin`, `screens.html.pre-margin`, `scoreboard.html.pre-finalsremove`). Harmless as long as nothing references them, but worth clearing out during any housekeeping pass so a future editor doesn't accidentally open the wrong one.\n- **`overlay.html`'s vignette/watermark layer and `minecraft.html`'s theme layer are two separate \"ambient shading\" systems that never talk to each other** \u2014 a scene with both active (none currently does) would need care about z-order and double-darkening. Not a bug today, just a seam to be aware of before anything gets layered.\n- **The emblem's licensed artwork is explicitly NOT present** per `assets/README.md`'s own description (it describes what *would* be dropped in) \u2014 the system is designed to run entirely on `emblem-fallback.svg` and the CSS-only skyline fallback. That's a legitimate design decision (licensed art stays local-only, never shipped), but it does mean the \"signature\" element of the identity right now is the drawn fallback bat, not licensed art. Worth confirming that's intentional and permanent vs. a placeholder waiting on an asset drop.\n\n### What's missing\n\n- **No Competitive/Just-Chatting/Cinematic/Recording/Emergency-Fallback scene variants** \u2014 only game-specific scenes (`1 - Gaming`, `2 - Minecraft`) plus the three screen states and the alert/stinger scenes exist today. That's Task 4 below.\n- **No GeoGuessr or iRacing capture/HUD sources at all** \u2014 content the streamer plays regularly has zero overlay support right now.\n- **No clip/highlight pipeline** \u2014 `source-record` is available but unused in the collection; nothing currently isolates game capture for post-stream editing.\n- **No viewer-facing audience-interaction surface beyond `ALR - StreamElements`** \u2014 no in-house alert styling that matches the chrome/amber language as tightly as the rest of the rig (StreamElements alerts are presumably still in that service's default skin, not Gotham-skinned).\n- **No use of `downstream-keyer`, `backgroundremoval`, `source-clone` beyond the one `CAM - Ambient Glow` clone, `input-overlay` beyond keyboard/mouse, or `obs-shaderfilter` anywhere** \u2014 half the installed plugin roster is idle. That's not a flaw, it's headroom; several concepts below spend it.\n\n**crosshair.html was not opened for editing and no change is proposed to it anywhere in this document** \u2014 confirmed untouched, per instruction.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Concept Bank (34 concepts, 8 categories)\n\nScoring key (1\u201310, Difficulty inverted: 10 = hardest):\n`Orig` Originality \u00b7 `Eng` Viewer Engagement \u00b7 `Use` Practical Usefulness \u00b7 `PerfEff` Performance Efficiency (10 = cheapest) \u00b7 `Diff` Implementation Difficulty (10 = hardest)\n\n### A. Visual\n\n**A1. Round-Outcome Spotlight Flare (full-capture)**\nExtends the round `#flare` pulse already coded into `scoreboard.html` from a HUD strip accent into a brief full-screen amber/signal-gold wash over the whole canvas on round win/loss, reusing the same `doFlare(who)` colour logic.\n- Viewer sees: a half-second warm or cold light wash across the entire frame at the instant a Valorant round is won or lost \u2014 readable even with sound off.\n- Implementation: new `FX - Round Flare (Full)` browser source, full-canvas, placed above `CAP - Valorant` and below HUD strips in `1 - Gaming`; driven by the same `/state.json` `round.at`/`round.who` fields the HUD strip already polls, so autopilot needs zero new code.\n- Required software: none beyond what's installed.\n- Custom dev needed: small (one new HTML file, ~60 lines, copy the flare keyframes out of `scoreboard.html`).\n- Hardware overhead: ~0.5\u20131% GPU, only during the ~1.5s animation window; a CSS radial-gradient opacity fade, no shaders.\n- Complexity: Low. Security: none (reads only local `/state.json`, no new network surface).\n- Priority: High.\n- Scores: Orig 5, Eng 7, Use 7, PerfEff 10, Diff 2.\n\n**A2. Weather-by-Performance (reactive rain density)**\nTies the existing `.rain`/`.fog` opacity in `gotham.css` to live K/D from `/state.json` instead of a fixed constant \u2014 rain gets heavier as the session's K/D worsens, thins out on a hot streak.\n- Viewer sees: the ambient Gotham rain subtly intensifying during a rough match and clearing during a win streak \u2014 an atmospheric scoreline nobody has to read as text.\n- Implementation: a small poll script added only to scenes that already include `.rain`/`.fog` (currently none visibly do outside `screens.html`'s BRB/Starting Soon cards) \u2014 more realistically ship this as an optional variant of A1/A3 rather than a new source.\n- Required software: none. Custom dev: small (pure JS fetch loop + CSS variable set).\n- Hardware overhead: negligible, <0.5% CPU for a 1s-interval fetch.\n- Complexity: Low. Security: none.\n- Priority: Medium \u2014 nice texture, but it's a subtlety easy to miss live; pair with A1, don't ship standalone.\n- Scores: Orig 6, Eng 5, Use 4, PerfEff 10, Diff 2.\n\n**A3. Searchlight Sweep (idle BRB/Starting Soon variant)**\nReplaces the static breathing spotlight in `screens.html` with an occasional slow sweep across the card during long BRB/Starting-Soon holds, so the screen doesn't look frozen on a 10-minute break.\n- Viewer sees: the spotlight cone slowly arcing side to side every 20\u201330s instead of sitting still.\n- Implementation: extend `.spotlight`'s existing `gothamBreathe` keyframe with an additional `translate`/rotate pass; pure CSS, same `SCR - BRB` / `SCR - Starting Soon` sources.\n- Required software: none. Custom dev: small (CSS-only edit).\n- Hardware overhead: negligible \u2014 same element, same compositing cost as today.\n- Complexity: Low. Security: none.\n- Priority: Medium.\n- Scores: Orig 4, Eng 5, Use 5, PerfEff 10, Diff 1.\n\n**A4. Flash/Damage Glitch Pass (shaderfilter)**\nA one-shot chromatic-aberration/glitch pulse on the cam frame when autopilot detects the player got flashed in Valorant (state already tracked for match polling can be extended to ability events if the API exposes them; if not, trigger on round-loss only as a safe substitute).\n- Viewer sees: the cam frame glitches briefly in sync with getting flashed \u2014 a reaction-shot gag that reads instantly on clips.\n- Implementation: `obs-shaderfilter` on `CAM - Frame`, enabled/disabled via `obs-websocket` `SetSourceFilterEnabled` from autopilot.\n- Required software: obs-shaderfilter (installed).\n- Custom dev: medium \u2014 needs autopilot to expose a flash/ability event, which may not exist in the current match-state poll; verify before committing to the flash trigger specifically.\n- Hardware overhead: ~2\u20134% GPU, but only during the ~300ms pulse, not continuous.\n- Complexity: Medium. Security: none (local filter toggle only).\n- Priority: Medium \u2014 compelling but gated on an autopilot feature that may not exist yet.\n- Scores: Orig 7, Eng 7, Use 4, PerfEff 8, Diff 5.\n\n**A5. IRL/Variety Day Weather Toggle**\nPackages the existing `.rain`/`.fog`/`.grain` classes as an explicit optional layer any new \"Cinematic\" or IRL-adjacent scene can turn on/off per occasion (e.g., a rainy real-world day, a moodier VOD review session) without writing new CSS.\n- Viewer sees: the signature Gotham weather applied consistently to non-gameplay scenes when the streamer wants the mood, not just BRB/Starting Soon.\n- Implementation: no new code \u2014 just a documented convention of including the existing classes in new `SCR-`/`FX-` sources.\n- Required software: none. Custom dev: none (reuse only).\n- Hardware overhead: same as current rain/fog (already proven cheap).\n- Complexity: Trivial. Security: none.\n- Priority: Low \u2014 it's already possible, just flagged so Cinematic Mode (Task 4) reuses it deliberately.\n- Scores: Orig 2, Eng 4, Use 5, PerfEff 10, Diff 1.\n\n### B. Gameplay\n\n**B1. Rank-Delta Progress Bar**\nAdds an RR-gain/loss progress bar under the existing `Rank` cell in `HUD - Minecraft Run`'s sibling for Valorant \u2014 i.e., extends `HUD - Mic Bars`'s neighbour, the match strip in `scoreboard.html`, with a thin bar showing progress toward rank-up, sourced from the same official rank string already polled.\n- Viewer sees: a small amber-fill bar ticking up toward the next rank alongside the existing Rank text.\n- Implementation: edit `scoreboard.html`'s `.cell` for rank to add a `<div class=\"bar\">` filled via a new `/state.json` field (`rr_progress`), which autopilot computes from its existing rank polling.\n- Required software: none new. Custom dev: small (one HTML/CSS addition + one autopilot field).\n- Hardware overhead: negligible \u2014 same polling loop, one more DOM node.\n- Complexity: Low. Security: none.\n- Priority: High.\n- Scores: Orig 4, Eng 6, Use 8, PerfEff 10, Diff 3.\n\n**B2. Minecraft Boss-Fight Timer**\nExtends `mc-hud.html`'s log-tailing approach (already reading `latest.log` for deaths/advancements with no mod required) to flag known boss-encounter log lines (Ender Dragon perch, Wither summon) and show an elapsed fight timer row.\n- Viewer sees: a new \"Boss Fight: 1:42\" row appears the moment a dragon/wither fight starts, ticking live.\n- Implementation: new row in `HUD - Minecraft Run`, fed by an autopilot log-pattern addition; follows the existing `.row`/`.k`/`.v` block style exactly.\n- Required software: none new. Custom dev: medium (autopilot needs new log-pattern matching; the overlay-side change is small).\n- Hardware overhead: negligible \u2014 log tailing is already happening.\n- Complexity: Medium. Security: none (local file read only).\n- Priority: Medium.\n- Scores: Orig 6, Eng 6, Use 5, PerfEff 10, Diff 4.\n\n**B3. GeoGuessr Round HUD**\nA small `HUD - GeoGuessr Round` strip showing round timer and last-guess distance, fed not by memory-reading (GeoGuessr has no local process to read) but by a tiny, purpose-built browser extension that reads the GeoGuessr tab's own DOM and posts JSON to autopilot's existing local HTTP server.\n- Viewer sees: round countdown and \"last guess: 340km off\" distance readout styled identically to the Valorant match strip.\n- Implementation: new `CAP - GeoGuessr` (window/display capture of the browser tab) + `HUD - GeoGuessr Round` browser source; a small unpacked Chrome/Firefox extension (content script + `fetch` POST) is the only new \"software.\"\n- Required software: a hand-written browser extension (not a game mod, not memory access \u2014 DOM scraping of a website you're voluntarily browsing). No anti-cheat exposure since GeoGuessr has none.\n- Custom dev: medium (extension + one new HUD page + one new autopilot endpoint).\n- Hardware overhead: negligible \u2014 extension runs inside the browser you already have open; no extra GPU render pass beyond a normal `HUD-` browser source.\n- Complexity: Medium. Security: a content script with host permissions on geoguessr.com only \u2014 scoped narrowly, reviewed before install, zero other sites touched. **Flag for approval**: new third-party-adjacent code (a browser extension) running with page access, even scoped.\n- Priority: Medium-High \u2014 content you play regularly with zero current overlay support.\n- Scores: Orig 8, Eng 7, Use 7, PerfEff 9, Diff 5.\n\n**B4. iRacing Telemetry Strip**\nA `HUD - iRacing Telemetry` bar (speed/gear/lap-delta) using iRacing's own official shared-memory telemetry SDK \u2014 a first-party, intentionally-exposed read API, not memory injection, not anything Vanguard-equivalent would flag (iRacing has no comparable kernel anti-cheat and explicitly documents this interface for exactly this use).\n- Viewer sees: live speed, gear, and lap delta styled with the same chrome-bevel HUD language as the Valorant strip.\n- Implementation: a small Python or C# bridge reading iRacing's shared memory block, writing it into the same `state.json` shape autopilot already produces, consumed by a new HTML HUD styled off `scoreboard.html`.\n- Required software: `pyirsdk` (or equivalent) \u2014 a well-established, iRacing-sanctioned library.\n- Custom dev: medium (bridge script + one HUD page).\n- Hardware overhead: negligible \u2014 a background Python process polling shared memory at low frequency, well under 1% CPU.\n- Complexity: Medium. Security: none (local shared memory, no network, no injection).\n- Priority: Medium \u2014 real but lower-frequency content than Valorant/Minecraft; build once the higher-priority items land.\n- Scores: Orig 6, Eng 6, Use 7, PerfEff 9, Diff 5.\n\n### C. Audience Interaction\n\n**C1. Redeem-Triggered Mini Bat-Signal**\nA shortened variant of `stinger.html` (`?ms=700` instead of 2000) fired as a brief non-transition overlay flash on big cheer/sub events, without swapping scenes \u2014 just the shutter-and-emblem flash layered momentarily over whatever scene is live.\n- Viewer sees: the bat emblem flares briefly over the current scene on a big sub/bits event, distinct from the full scene-change stinger.\n- Implementation: `Streamer.bot` action \u2192 `obs-websocket` \u2192 show/hide a dedicated `FX - Mini Signal` browser source (same HTML as `stinger.html` with `restart_when_active` so it replays on every show).\n- Required software: Streamer.bot (listed as \"soon\"). Custom dev: small (reuse existing stinger HTML with different params).\n- Hardware overhead: ~1\u20132% GPU for well under a second, matching the existing stinger's cost profile.\n- Complexity: Low-Medium (depends on Streamer.bot being installed first). Security: none beyond normal Streamer.bot/Twitch EventSub scopes.\n- Priority: High \u2014 cheap, on-brand, immediately legible as \"this stream's thing.\"\n- Scores: Orig 7, Eng 8, Use 6, PerfEff 9, Diff 4.\n\n**C2. Chat-Voted Accent for BRB Screens**\nDuring BRB, chat votes (via channel-point redeem or simple chat command tally in Streamer.bot) between the existing `[data-accent]` presets (arena/valorant/finals/solo) for that break's screen colour.\n- Viewer sees: their vote visibly changes the BRB card's accent colour for that break.\n- Implementation: Streamer.bot tallies votes, writes the winning accent to a small local file; `SCR - BRB` (already a browser source) polls that file every few seconds via a tiny added fetch loop and re-applies `document.documentElement.dataset.accent`.\n- Required software: Streamer.bot. Custom dev: small (poll loop addition to `screens.html`, ~15 lines).\n- Hardware overhead: negligible.\n- Complexity: Low. Security: local file read only, no exposed write endpoint from chat directly (Streamer.bot mediates).\n- Priority: High.\n- Scores: Orig 6, Eng 7, Use 5, PerfEff 10, Diff 3.\n\n**C3. Case File Shoutout Card**\nA new-follower/raid alert styled as a Gotham \"case file\" \u2014 a manila-and-chrome card with the viewer's name stamped in the amber display type \u2014 composited cleanly over whatever's live via `downstream-keyer` instead of a flat alert box.\n- Viewer sees: raids/follows trigger a styled case-file card that slides/stamps in, on-brand rather than a generic StreamElements popup.\n- Implementation: new `ALR - Case File` browser source + `downstream-keyer` for clean compositing in `DSK - Alerts`; triggered by the existing StreamElements/Streamer.bot event webhook.\n- Required software: downstream-keyer (installed), Streamer.bot (soon) or continued StreamElements webhook.\n- Custom dev: medium (new HTML card, ~150 lines borrowing `.chrome`/`.amber-text`).\n- Hardware overhead: ~1% GPU, alert-duration only.\n- Complexity: Medium. Security: none beyond existing alert-service exposure.\n- Priority: High \u2014 directly replaces the one place in the current rig (`ALR - StreamElements`) that visibly doesn't match the rest of the identity.\n- Scores: Orig 6, Eng 7, Use 8, PerfEff 9, Diff 5.\n\n**C4. Fog/Rain Intensity Redeem**\nA bits-gated channel-point redeem that temporarily cranks the BRB/Starting-Soon fog and rain opacity up for a few minutes \u2014 a cheap, funny, low-stakes redeem.\n- Viewer sees: the atmosphere visibly \"storms\" for a few minutes after someone redeems it.\n- Implementation: Streamer.bot writes a temporary override value to the same local file C2 reads; `screens.html`'s existing fog/rain opacity gets one more query-string-equivalent override read from that file.\n- Required software: Streamer.bot. Custom dev: small.\n- Hardware overhead: negligible (same elements, different opacity).\n- Complexity: Low. Security: none; redeem is cosmetic-only and time-boxed.\n- Priority: Medium \u2014 fun but overlaps functionally with C2; ship one or the other first, not both at once.\n- Scores: Orig 5, Eng 6, Use 3, PerfEff 10, Diff 3.\n\n**C5. Interrogation Mode Q&A Lower-Third**\nA chat-submitted-question queue (via Streamer.bot command, e.g. `!ask`) displayed as a Gotham \"interrogation room\" lower-third during Just Chatting segments, composited via `downstream-keyer`.\n- Viewer sees: their submitted question appears on-screen, held in queue order, cleared by streamer command.\n- Implementation: new `IN - Interrogation Queue` browser source polling a small Streamer.bot-maintained queue file; styled off `.chrome` + `.amber-text`.\n- Required software: Streamer.bot. Custom dev: medium (queue logic lives in Streamer.bot C# or JS; overlay is a straightforward poll-and-render page).\n- Hardware overhead: negligible.\n- Complexity: Medium. Security: needs basic text sanitization before render (same `esc()` pattern already used in `chatbox.html`) to avoid any injected HTML from a chat-submitted string \u2014 reuse that existing escaping function.\n- Priority: Medium-High \u2014 strong Just Chatting Mode asset.\n- Scores: Orig 7, Eng 8, Use 6, PerfEff 10, Diff 5.\n\n### D. Automation\n\n**D1. Smoother Auto-Switch Transitions**\nAutopilot already detects game and switches scenes; layer `move-transition` (installed, currently only used for the `Move` transition on manual scene changes per the live collection) onto the automated switches too, so cam/HUD elements glide into their new positions instead of a hard cut when autopilot swaps scenes.\n- Viewer sees: smoother, more polished automatic scene changes between games.\n- Implementation: no new source \u2014 just ensure autopilot's `obs-websocket` scene-switch calls go through the existing `Move` transition rather than bypassing it (verify current trigger path; may already work).\n- Required software: none new (move-transition already installed and already the default transition). Custom dev: small \u2014 likely a one-line check in autopilot's switch call, possibly zero dev if it already routes through the default transition.\n- Hardware overhead: same as any transition \u2014 negligible, GPU-composited crossfade/move, sub-1% for the transition duration.\n- Complexity: Low. Security: none.\n- Priority: High \u2014 likely near-zero-cost polish.\n- Scores: Orig 3, Eng 5, Use 6, PerfEff 10, Diff 2.\n\n**D2. Clip-Worthy Moment Auto-Flagging**\nAutopilot, which already polls Valorant match state and tails the Minecraft log, writes a timestamped marker (ace, clutch round, rare advancement, boss kill) to a local log file; paired with a replay buffer or `source-record`, those timestamps become a ready-made cut list for later editing \u2014 entirely a background-process change, no render cost.\n- Viewer sees: nothing live \u2014 this pays off in faster highlight turnaround.\n- Implementation: extend autopilot's existing event detection to also append to `highlights.log`; separately enable OBS's built-in replay buffer or `source-record` on `CAP -` sources.\n- Required software: none new (source-record already installed). Custom dev: medium (autopilot needs event-significance heuristics, e.g. what counts as \"clip-worthy\").\n- Hardware overhead: `source-record` writing an isolated capture stream to disk costs encode overhead \u2014 budget ~3\u20136% CPU/GPU depending on codec choice (prefer NVENC HEVC/AV1 to keep it off the CPU encode path entirely, since NVENC is available and otherwise idle during this work).\n- Complexity: Medium. Security: local files only.\n- Priority: High \u2014 directly serves the Content Creation category below with near-zero live-stream risk.\n- Scores: Orig 6, Eng 3, Use 9, PerfEff 7, Diff 5.\n\n**D3. Automated Health-Triggered Fallback**\n`scoreboard.html`'s `#health` badge already surfaces dropped-frame/render-lag warnings from `/state.json`; extend autopilot to automatically switch to the new Emergency Fallback Mode scene (Task 4) via `obs-websocket` once the `bad` threshold is sustained for N seconds, rather than only displaying the badge.\n- Viewer sees: if the stream is genuinely struggling, it drops to a lightweight, stable fallback automatically instead of staying on an overloaded scene.\n- Implementation: autopilot already reads the health numbers for the badge; add a threshold+debounce check that calls `SetCurrentProgramScene`.\n- Required software: none new. Custom dev: small-medium (debounce logic to avoid flapping).\n- Hardware overhead: negative \u2014 this concept *reduces* load during exactly the moments load is already too high.\n- Complexity: Medium. Security: none; **flag for approval only because it's an autonomous scene-switch action** \u2014 the streamer should set and confirm the exact threshold/debounce values before it's allowed to act without a human in the loop.\n- Priority: High.\n- Scores: Orig 7, Eng 3, Use 9, PerfEff 10, Diff 5.\n\n**D4. Auto-Populated End-of-Stream Recap**\nAutopilot aggregates the day's K/D, deaths, advancements, and uptime, then writes them into `SCR - Stream Ended`'s subtitle/handle text via query-string-equivalent fields it already has the data for (the HUD strips compute these numbers live already \u2014 this just also pushes them into the sign-off screen).\n- Viewer sees: the sign-off card shows a real one-line recap of the day instead of a static \"thanks for watching.\"\n- Implementation: `screens.html`'s `ended` preset gets one more field read from `/state.json` at load time.\n- Required software: none. Custom dev: small.\n- Hardware overhead: negligible.\n- Complexity: Low. Security: none.\n- Priority: Medium.\n- Scores: Orig 5, Eng 5, Use 6, PerfEff 10, Diff 2.\n\n### E. Audio Experiences\n\n**E1. Ducked Ambient Soundscape**\nA very low-volume rain/thunder ambience bed (royalty-free) that sidechains down automatically whenever the mic is open, using `localvocal`'s voice-activity signal as the duck trigger rather than a manual noise gate.\n- Viewer sees/hears: a faint Gotham rain ambience under quiet moments that disappears the instant the streamer talks, reinforcing the mood without ever competing with voice.\n- Implementation: new `AUD - Ambience` media source; a small script (or Streamer.bot audio action) reduces its OBS volume via `obs-websocket` `SetInputVolume` when `localvocal`'s VAD output is active.\n- Required software: localvocal (installed).\n- Custom dev: medium (the duck-trigger glue code is the only new piece; localvocal and obs-websocket already exist).\n- Hardware overhead: localvocal's voice-activity-only mode (not full transcription) is cheap \u2014 well under 2% CPU; the ambience track itself costs nothing extra (it's just another audio source).\n- Complexity: Medium. Security: none.\n- Priority: Medium.\n- Scores: Orig 6, Eng 4, Use 5, PerfEff 8, Diff 5.\n\n**E2. Round-Outcome Audio Stabs**\nShort, signature-amber audio stingers (a rising chime on round win, a low thud on loss) fired off the same `round.at`/`round.who` event A1 and the existing `#flare` already key off.\n- Viewer sees/hears: an instantly recognizable audio sting on every round outcome, reinforcing the visual flare.\n- Implementation: a `media_source` added/removed or restarted via `obs-websocket`, triggered from the same autopilot event feed.\n- Required software: none new. Custom dev: small.\n- Hardware overhead: negligible \u2014 short audio clip playback.\n- Complexity: Low. Security: none.\n- Priority: High \u2014 cheap, pairs naturally with A1, strong identity reinforcement.\n- Scores: Orig 5, Eng 7, Use 6, PerfEff 10, Diff 2.\n\n**E3. Gotham-Styled Live Captions**\n`localvocal`'s local ASR output rendered as a subtitle card using the same `.chrome`/`amber-text` language as the rest of the rig, for accessibility \u2014 explicitly a Just Chatting / Cinematic Mode feature, toggled off during Competitive Mode.\n- Viewer sees: live captions in the stream's own visual language instead of a generic caption box.\n- Implementation: new `HUD - Captions` browser source consuming localvocal's output (localvocal already supports writing to a local text/websocket sink); style per `.chrome`.\n- Required software: localvocal (installed).\n- Custom dev: medium (styling pass + a small bridge if localvocal's output format needs adapting to the HUD's expected JSON).\n- Hardware overhead: continuous local ASR is the heaviest line item in this whole document \u2014 realistically 8\u201315% CPU on the 5700X depending on model size, which is why it's scoped explicitly OFF for Competitive Mode and the FPS-priority instruction up top. Fine for Just Chatting/Cinematic where there's render headroom; not fine layered under ranked Valorant.\n- Complexity: Medium. Security: none (fully local ASR, no cloud call).\n- Priority: Medium \u2014 valuable for accessibility, but gate it hard to non-competitive modes.\n- Scores: Orig 5, Eng 5, Use 7, PerfEff 4, Diff 5.\n\n**E4. Mic-Open Ambient Pulse**\nA near-invisible amber pulse already possible from the existing `--glow` pipeline \u2014 tie a one-time soft glow bloom to the mic open/close transition itself (not continuous level, just the on/off edge), reusing `obs-meter.js`'s existing output with zero new signal path.\n- Viewer sees: a barely-there warm pulse the instant the mic opens \u2014 subliminal \"on air\" cue.\n- Implementation: a few lines added to any `onLevel` consumer watching for a 0\u2192nonzero mic-input transition specifically (as distinct from Desktop Audio, which is what `obs-meter.js` currently defaults to \u2014 this needs it pointed at the mic input instead/also).\n- Required software: none new. Custom dev: small.\n- Hardware overhead: negligible \u2014 reuses the exact existing `--glow` CSS pipeline.\n- Complexity: Low. Security: none.\n- Priority: Low \u2014 subtle enough that it may not be worth the attention, but essentially free if bundled with other `obs-meter.js` work.\n- Scores: Orig 4, Eng 3, Use 3, PerfEff 10, Diff 2.\n\n### F. Content Creation\n\n**F1. Auto-Compiled \"Case File\" Highlight Reel**\nBuilds directly on D2: once `source-record` has an isolated capture stream and autopilot has a `highlights.log` of flagged timestamps, a short local `ffmpeg` script (run post-stream, off-stream, zero live cost) cuts a montage automatically.\n- Viewer sees: nothing live; this is a between-stream deliverable (clips/VOD highlights) that previously required manual scrubbing.\n- Implementation: a Python/ffmpeg script reading `highlights.log` and the isolated `source-record` file, cutting N-second windows around each timestamp.\n- Required software: ffmpeg (free, local). Custom dev: medium.\n- Hardware overhead: zero during the stream \u2014 runs entirely after, when the GPU/CPU budget isn't shared with Valorant.\n- Complexity: Medium. Security: none, local files only.\n- Priority: High \u2014 meaningfully valuable, depends only on D2 landing first.\n- Scores: Orig 6, Eng 4, Use 9, PerfEff 10, Diff 5.\n\n**F2. Weekly \"Case Board\" Recap Graphic**\nA Python script renders the week's aggregated stats (from the same `/state.json` history autopilot already tallies for \"Today\") into a static Gotham-styled image for socials \u2014 literally reusing `gotham.css` in a headless-rendered HTML page, screenshotted to PNG.\n- Viewer sees: nothing live; a social-media asset for Twitter/Discord, visually on-brand automatically.\n- Implementation: a small headless-browser screenshot script (e.g., Playwright) of a new static `recap.html` that borrows `.chrome`/`.amber-text`.\n- Required software: a headless browser tool (Playwright or similar) \u2014 one new local dependency.\n- Custom dev: medium.\n- Hardware overhead: zero during stream; runs offline.\n- Complexity: Medium. Security: none.\n- Priority: Medium.\n- Scores: Orig 6, Eng 3, Use 6, PerfEff 10, Diff 5.\n\n**F3. Clutch-Round Replay Inset**\nA `source-clone` of `CAP - Valorant` feeding a short rolling buffer; on a manual hotkey (or D2's auto-flag), a `IN - Replay Inset` card \u2014 styled as a pinned case-file photo in the corner \u2014 shows the last ~10 seconds again inset over the live feed.\n- Viewer sees: an instant corner-replay of the clutch they just watched, without cutting away from the live action.\n- Implementation: `source-clone` (installed) + a short local buffer; triggered by hotkey via `obs-websocket` or Streamer.bot.\n- Required software: source-clone (installed). Custom dev: medium-high (buffer/trigger logic is the fiddly part).\n- Hardware overhead: a second encode of the same capture is the real cost here \u2014 budget ~5\u20138% GPU while the clone is actively buffering, since it's a second NVENC/CPU path on top of the main capture. Worth testing headroom specifically during Valorant before committing, given the FPS-priority constraint.\n- Complexity: High. Security: none.\n- Priority: Medium \u2014 cool feature, but it's the single most GPU-expensive idea in this whole list and conflicts directly with the \"Valorant FPS is the top priority\" constraint if not very carefully budgeted.\n- Scores: Orig 8, Eng 8, Use 6, PerfEff 4, Diff 8.\n\n**F4. Autopilot VOD Chapter Markers**\nAutopilot's round/boss/advancement events get written to a sidecar chapter file (standard VOD chapter format) alongside the recording, ready for YouTube chapters once that platform is reactivated, and useful for local VOD navigation today.\n- Viewer sees: nothing live; a future-platform convenience, built now while the event data is already flowing.\n- Implementation: append to a `.chapters` file alongside whatever D2/F1 already write.\n- Required software: none new. Custom dev: small.\n- Hardware overhead: zero.\n- Complexity: Low. Security: none.\n- Priority: Low \u2014 genuinely useful but tied to the explicitly-deferred YouTube platform; don't prioritize ahead of Twitch-facing work.\n- Scores: Orig 4, Eng 2, Use 5, PerfEff 10, Diff 2.\n\n### G. Community Engagement\n\n**G1. Rogues Gallery Top-Chatter Card**\nA between-game `ALR - Rogues Gallery` card showing the week's top chat contributors as styled \"mugshot\" boxes (initials/name in chrome frames, amber captions), sourced from Streamer.bot's point/loyalty system.\n- Viewer sees: recognition for top chatters shown briefly between matches or during a break.\n- Implementation: new browser source polling a Streamer.bot-exported leaderboard file; styled per `.chrome`.\n- Required software: Streamer.bot. Custom dev: medium.\n- Hardware overhead: negligible, shown briefly and not continuously.\n- Complexity: Medium. Security: displays usernames only \u2014 no PII beyond what's already public in chat.\n- Priority: Medium-High \u2014 strong community-engagement value, low cost.\n- Scores: Orig 6, Eng 7, Use 6, PerfEff 10, Diff 4.\n\n**G2. Case-File Goals Tracker**\nA thin `HUD - Goals` strip (sub/follower-goal progress) styled with the chrome bevel, pulling from Streamer.bot, shown persistently in Just Chatting Mode and briefly during transitions elsewhere.\n- Viewer sees: a visible, on-brand goal bar instead of a third-party widget's default look.\n- Implementation: new browser source, data via Streamer.bot's existing goal-tracking features (if present) or a simple local counter file.\n- Required software: Streamer.bot. Custom dev: small-medium.\n- Hardware overhead: negligible.\n- Complexity: Low-Medium. Security: none.\n- Priority: Medium.\n- Scores: Orig 4, Eng 6, Use 6, PerfEff 10, Diff 3.\n\n**G3. Night Shift Schedule Card**\n`SCR - Stream Ended` gets an additional block showing the next 2\u20133 scheduled stream dates/times, pulled from a simple local schedule file the streamer edits by hand (no calendar API integration needed).\n- Viewer sees: \"Next case file opens: Thu 8PM\" instead of having to check Discord/Twitter for the next stream time.\n- Implementation: one more text field on the existing sign-off screen, reading a local `schedule.txt`.\n- Required software: none. Custom dev: small.\n- Hardware overhead: negligible.\n- Complexity: Low. Security: none.\n- Priority: Medium.\n- Scores: Orig 3, Eng 5, Use 7, PerfEff 10, Diff 2.\n\n**G4. Minecraft Build-Vote Row**\nChannel points let chat vote on what to build next; the running tally shows as one more row in `HUD - Minecraft Run`, following the exact `.row`/`.k`/`.v` styling already established there.\n- Viewer sees: their build vote tallying live in the same HUD they already watch deaths/advancements in.\n- Implementation: Streamer.bot tallies channel-point redeems into a small local file; `mc-hud.html` gets one more row reading it.\n- Required software: Streamer.bot (or Twitch channel-point API directly). Custom dev: small.\n- Hardware overhead: negligible.\n- Complexity: Low. Security: none.\n- Priority: Medium.\n- Scores: Orig 5, Eng 7, Use 6, PerfEff 10, Diff 3.\n\n### H. Experimental Broadcasting\n\n**H1. Multistream Groundwork (Twitch-only, YouTube-ready)**\nConfigure `aitum-multistream` (already installed) purely as plumbing \u2014 a single Twitch output today, structured so adding a YouTube destination later is a config change, not a re-architecture of scenes or overlays.\n- Viewer sees: nothing \u2014 zero visual change. This is pure infrastructure.\n- Implementation: verify aitum-multistream's existing config targets Twitch cleanly and isn't fighting with any other output path; document the YouTube-activation steps for whenever that platform comes back into scope.\n- Required software: none new (already installed). Custom dev: none \u2014 configuration only.\n- Hardware overhead: a second simultaneous encode is the real cost *if and when* a second destination is turned on \u2014 budget a full second NVENC session (AV1 or HEVC, since both are available) only at that future point; today, with Twitch-only, there's no overhead at all.\n- Complexity: Low today. Security: none today.\n- Priority: Low right now (explicitly deferred per the brief) but worth a 20-minute config check so it's not a surprise later.\n- Scores: Orig 2, Eng 1, Use 6, PerfEff 10, Diff 2.\n\n**H2. \"Detective Vision\" Key-Moment Shader Pass**\nA one-shot desaturate-and-rim-light shader pass (obs-shaderfilter) applied briefly to the full capture during a flagged key moment (clutch round, Minecraft near-death) \u2014 a stylized \"focus mode\" rather than a continuous filter.\n- Viewer sees: the whole frame briefly desaturates except for a amber rim-light pulse during the game's most dramatic seconds \u2014 a deliberate, noticeable stylistic beat, used sparingly.\n- Implementation: `obs-shaderfilter` on the relevant `CAP -` source, toggled by the same autopilot event feed as D2/A1.\n- Required software: obs-shaderfilter (installed). Custom dev: medium (writing/tuning the actual shader).\n- Hardware overhead: ~4\u20136% GPU, but strictly for the few-second duration of the effect, not continuously \u2014 needs testing against the Valorant FPS budget specifically before being allowed to fire mid-competitive-round rather than only between rounds.\n- Complexity: High. Security: none.\n- Priority: Low-Medium \u2014 striking but risky against the FPS-priority constraint if it ever fires mid-round rather than at round-boundaries; if built, gate it strictly to round-end/death moments, never mid-combat.\n- Scores: Orig 8, Eng 7, Use 4, PerfEff 5, Diff 7.\n\n**H3. Interrogation Room Alt-Angle Swap**\nA second camera (room-wide shot) toggled in via `source-clone`/manual switch during Just Chatting for visual variety, swapping with the primary `CAM - Webcam` on a hotkey.\n- Viewer sees: an occasional wide \"room\" shot instead of always the tight face cam during chat segments.\n- Implementation: a second `dshow_input` source + a `GRP -`-style group swap, or a scene-item visibility toggle; no GPU cost beyond a second capture device decode.\n- Required software: a second USB camera (hardware purchase, not software) \u2014 **flag for approval**: this is the one concept here that needs a hardware buy, not just config/code.\n- Custom dev: small (swap logic). Hardware overhead: a second camera decode is cheap on this GPU (USB UVC capture, not GPU-side); the actual constraint is USB bandwidth if both cameras are high-res/high-framerate on the same controller \u2014 budget accordingly.\n- Complexity: Low (software) / requires hardware. Security: none.\n- Priority: Low \u2014 nice-to-have, gated on a hardware purchase decision that's outside the scope of this design pass.\n- Scores: Orig 4, Eng 6, Use 5, PerfEff 9, Diff 4.\n\n**H4. Evidence Board Viewer-Submitted Corkboard**\nA channel-point redeem lets viewers submit an image (via a small upload form) that gets pinned, corkboard-style, onto an `IN - Evidence Board` overlay shown during Just Chatting.\n- Viewer sees: their submitted image pinned on a Gotham-styled corkboard on stream.\n- Implementation: a tiny local web form (new, custom) writes uploaded files into a watched folder; a browser source polls that folder and renders new pins.\n- Required software: a small custom upload server (new code, new local network surface) and some file-type/size validation.\n- Custom dev: high (upload handling, validation, and crucially moderation \u2014 this is the one concept here that puts *unvetted viewer-submitted content* directly on stream).\n- Hardware overhead: negligible for render; the overhead is a standing local HTTP upload endpoint.\n- Complexity: High. Security: **significant \u2014 flag for explicit approval.** Any viewer-upload-to-stream pipeline needs a moderation step (manual approval queue, not instant-publish) and file-type/size limits, or it's an open door for someone to put arbitrary or offensive imagery live on stream. Do not ship without an approve-before-display gate.\n- Priority: Low \u2014 the moderation burden and new attack surface outweigh the novelty; see shortlist rejection rationale below.\n- Scores: Orig 8, Eng 7, Use 4, PerfEff 8, Diff 8.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Score Summary\n\n| # | Concept | Orig | Eng | Use | PerfEff | Diff |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A1 | Round-Outcome Spotlight Flare | 5 | 7 | 7 | 10 | 2 |\n| A2 | Weather-by-Performance | 6 | 5 | 4 | 10 | 2 |\n| A3 | Searchlight Sweep (idle) | 4 | 5 | 5 | 10 | 1 |\n| A4 | Flash/Damage Glitch Pass | 7 | 7 | 4 | 8 | 5 |\n| A5 | IRL Weather Toggle | 2 | 4 | 5 | 10 | 1 |\n| B1 | Rank-Delta Progress Bar | 4 | 6 | 8 | 10 | 3 |\n| B2 | Minecraft Boss-Fight Timer | 6 | 6 | 5 | 10 | 4 |\n| B3 | GeoGuessr Round HUD | 8 | 7 | 7 | 9 | 5 |\n| B4 | iRacing Telemetry Strip | 6 | 6 | 7 | 9 | 5 |\n| C1 | Redeem-Triggered Mini Signal | 7 | 8 | 6 | 9 | 4 |\n| C2 | Chat-Voted BRB Accent | 6 | 7 | 5 | 10 | 3 |\n| C3 | Case File Shoutout Card | 6 | 7 | 8 | 9 | 5 |\n| C4 | Fog/Rain Intensity Redeem | 5 | 6 | 3 | 10 | 3 |\n| C5 | Interrogation Q&A Lower-Third | 7 | 8 | 6 | 10 | 5 |\n| D1 | Smoother Auto-Switch Transitions | 3 | 5 | 6 | 10 | 2 |\n| D2 | Clip-Worthy Auto-Flagging | 6 | 3 | 9 | 7 | 5 |\n| D3 | Automated Health Fallback | 7 | 3 | 9 | 10 | 5 |\n| D4 | Auto Recap Screen | 5 | 5 | 6 | 10 | 2 |\n| E1 | Ducked Ambient Soundscape | 6 | 4 | 5 | 8 | 5 |\n| E2 | Round-Outcome Audio Stabs | 5 | 7 | 6 | 10 | 2 |\n| E3 | Gotham Live Captions | 5 | 5 | 7 | 4 | 5 |\n| E4 | Mic-Open Ambient Pulse | 4 | 3 | 3 | 10 | 2 |\n| F1 | Auto-Compiled Highlight Reel | 6 | 4 | 9 | 10 | 5 |\n| F2 | Weekly Recap Graphic | 6 | 3 | 6 | 10 | 5 |\n| F3 | Clutch Replay Inset | 8 | 8 | 6 | 4 | 8 |\n| F4 | VOD Chapter Markers | 4 | 2 | 5 | 10 | 2 |\n| G1 | Rogues Gallery Top-Chatter | 6 | 7 | 6 | 10 | 4 |\n| G2 | Case-File Goals Tracker | 4 | 6 | 6 | 10 | 3 |\n| G3 | Night Shift Schedule Card | 3 | 5 | 7 | 10 | 2 |\n| G4 | Minecraft Build-Vote Row | 5 | 7 | 6 | 10 | 3 |\n| H1 | Multistream Groundwork | 2 | 1 | 6 | 10 | 2 |\n| H2 | Detective Vision Shader Pass | 8 | 7 | 4 | 5 | 7 |\n| H3 | Interrogation Room Alt-Angle | 4 | 6 | 5 | 9 | 4 |\n| H4 | Evidence Board Corkboard | 8 | 7 | 4 | 8 | 8 |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Recommended Shortlist (ranked)\n\n**Curation principle:** favor concepts that extend infrastructure already proven in this codebase (`/state.json` polling, `obs-meter.js`'s `--glow` pipeline, the `.chrome`/`.amber-text` CSS system, autopilot's event detection) over anything requiring a new standing service, a new input surface from strangers, or GPU budget that competes with Valorant frame time.\n\n1. **C3 \u2014 Case File Shoutout Card.** Fixes the one visibly off-brand element in the current live rig (`ALR - StreamElements`'s default skin) with real viewer-facing payoff every follow/raid. High usefulness, reuses existing plugin (downstream-keyer) that's installed and currently idle.\n2. **D3 \u2014 Automated Health-Triggered Fallback.** Directly protects the hardware-ceiling priority stated in the brief: if frames start dropping, the stream gets *lighter*, automatically, instead of staying on an overloaded scene until a human notices. This is the single highest-leverage reliability concept here.\n3. **D2 \u2014 Clip-Worthy Moment Auto-Flagging.** Near-zero live cost (it's a background logging change), unlocks F1 for free, and turns \"editing the VOD\" from a scrubbing chore into a markers list.\n4. **A1 \u2014 Round-Outcome Spotlight Flare.** Cheapest possible visual payoff (reuses existing flare logic from `scoreboard.html` almost verbatim) with real engagement value \u2014 readable with sound off, which matters for clips.\n5. **E2 \u2014 Round-Outcome Audio Stabs.** Ship alongside A1; same trigger, same event, doubles the signature-moment payoff for almost no extra work.\n6. **B1 \u2014 Rank-Delta Progress Bar.** Small, cheap, genuinely useful \u2014 the kind of HUD improvement that compounds every single competitive session rather than a one-off gag.\n7. **C1 \u2014 Redeem-Triggered Mini Bat-Signal.** The strongest \"this stream has a signature move\" audience-interaction idea in the bank, and it's a parameter variant of a file that already exists and already works (`stinger.html`).\n8. **C2 \u2014 Chat-Voted BRB Accent.** Cheap, funny, directly reuses the `[data-accent]` system that's already built into three files \u2014 this is \"spend five minutes of chat's attention on a feature we basically already have.\"\n9. **G1 \u2014 Rogues Gallery Top-Chatter Card.** The best community-recognition idea for the lowest build cost; meaningfully different from a generic leaderboard widget because it's styled in-identity.\n10. **F1 \u2014 Auto-Compiled Highlight Reel.** Pure payoff once D2 exists; zero live-stream risk since it runs entirely post-stream.\n11. **D1 \u2014 Smoother Auto-Switch Transitions.** Likely near-zero additional work (may already be close to working via the existing `Move` transition) for a real polish gain on every single automatic scene change.\n12. **B3 \u2014 GeoGuessr Round HUD.** The strongest \"fills an actual content gap\" pick \u2014 GeoGuessr currently has zero overlay support despite being regular content \u2014 but ranked last because it needs a small custom browser extension (new, narrow, but real attack surface) and should get explicit sign-off before building, not quiet default-yes treatment like the rest of this list.\n\n### Explicitly NOT worth building (and why)\n\n- **F3 \u2014 Clutch Replay Inset.** The idea is good (instant corner-replay of a clutch) but it's the single worst performance-efficiency score in the whole bank (4/10) precisely because it demands a second simultaneous encode of the game capture via `source-clone`, on the hardware whose explicit top priority is Valorant frame time. This is the textbook case of a flashy feature that directly fights the stated constraint. Revisit only if a future GPU upgrade changes the budget math \u2014 don't build it on the 4060 Ti.\n- **H4 \u2014 Evidence Board Corkboard.** Letting strangers push arbitrary images directly onto a live broadcast is a moderation and security problem first and a feature second. Even with an approval queue, it's a standing custom upload server (new network surface) for a novelty that a Discord channel already covers better and more safely. Not recommended at all, approval-gated or not.\n- **H2 \u2014 Detective Vision Shader Pass.** Visually the most striking idea here, but it needs a hand-tuned shader running during exactly the moments (clutch rounds) when frame time matters most, and the only safe version of it (\"never mid-combat, round-boundary only\") sharply limits when it can actually fire \u2014 a lot of shader-dev effort for a narrow window of legal use. Worth a second look only after Competitive Mode's frame budget is proven comfortable with margin to spare.\n- **E3 \u2014 Gotham Live Captions.** Accessibility value is real, but continuous local ASR is the single heaviest CPU item in this entire bank (8\u201315%) on a chip that's also running OBS's encode and the game. Fine for Just Chatting; not worth building as a priority item given it must be hard-gated off during the content (Valorant) the brief says matters most \u2014 the gating logic adds complexity for a feature that's then unavailable during primary content anyway.\n- **H1 \u2014 Multistream Groundwork.** Not \"bad,\" just explicitly out of scope \u2014 YouTube is deferred per the brief, so spending build time on multistream plumbing today is effort against a priority that doesn't exist yet. A 20-minute later-check is enough; it doesn't need a project slot now.\n- **A2 \u2014 Weather-by-Performance** and **C4 \u2014 Fog/Rain Intensity Redeem** overlap heavily with each other and with A1/C2 respectively \u2014 both are real but neither earns a separate build slot; fold A2 into A1 as an optional variant, and pick C2 over C4 since accent-voting has more replay value than a one-shot intensity bump.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Broadcast Mode Specs\n\nAll five modes are new scenes, named and ordered per the existing convention. Sources marked **(existing)** are taken verbatim from the live `Main.json` collection; sources marked **(new)** need to be created, following the same naming prefix rules observed throughout the current collection.\n\n### Competitive Mode \u2014 minimal, Valorant-only, FPS-first\n\n**Scene name:** `1a - Competitive`\n\nReuses almost everything from `1 - Gaming` but strips every non-essential render pass to protect frame time, and removes the cam-card/input-overlay visual weight down to the smallest footprint that still shows mic status and match state.\n\nOrdered source list (top = front):\n1. `ALR - StreamElements` **(existing)** \u2014 alerts still need to reach the viewer even in competitive play.\n2. `HUD - Mic Muted` **(existing)**\n3. `HUD - Valorant Match` **(new)** \u2014 a slimmed, score-and-map-only variant of `scoreboard.html`'s `#bar`, with the season-stats panel (`#stats`) and round-flare (`#flare`) both disabled by default to keep this mode's render cost at the floor.\n4. `CAM - Frame` **(existing)** \u2014 kept small/corner, no `CAM - Card` backdrop, no `CAM - Ambient Glow` clone (that's the one `source-clone` instance already in the collection \u2014 skip it here specifically because it's a second encode pass).\n5. `CAM - Webcam` **(existing)**\n6. `CAP - Valorant` **(existing)**, with `CAP - Valorant (Window Fallback)` **(existing)** behind it, unchanged.\n7. `AUD - Game` / `AUD - Discord` / `AUD - Music (Spotify)` / `AUD - Music (Browser)` **(existing, all four)**\n8. `FX - Rim (Mic)` **(existing)**\n\nExplicitly dropped vs. `1 - Gaming`: `IN - Keyboard`, `IN - Mouse`, `GRP - Cam + Inputs`, `CAM - Card`, `CAM - Ambient Glow`. Input overlays are genuinely useful for coaching-style content but cost render time for information most competitive viewers don't need second-to-second; cut them here, keep them in `1 - Gaming` for non-ranked sessions.\n\nTransition in/out: `Move` **(existing default)** for normal switches; `Bat Signal` (`scene_as_transition`, **existing**) only when entering/leaving the session, not between round states.\n\n### Just Chatting Mode \u2014 large cam\n\n**Scene name:** `6 - Just Chatting`\n\nCam becomes the primary subject; game capture is dropped entirely.\n\nOrdered source list:\n1. `ALR - Case File` **(new, from C3)** \u2014 or `ALR - StreamElements` **(existing)** if C3 hasn't been built yet.\n2. `IN - Interrogation Queue` **(new, from C5)**\n3. `HUD - Captions` **(new, from E3)** \u2014 the one mode where the ASR CPU cost is acceptable, per the assessment above.\n4. `HUD - Goals` **(new, from G2)**\n5. `CAM - Card` **(existing)** \u2014 resized large, centred, as the dominant frame instead of a corner element.\n6. `CAM - Webcam` **(existing)**\n7. `CAM - Frame` **(existing)**\n8. `AUD - Discord` / `AUD - Music (Spotify)` / `AUD - Music (Browser)` **(existing)** \u2014 `AUD - Game` omitted since no game is running.\n9. `FX - Rim (Mic)` **(existing)**\n\nTransition: `Move` **(existing)** \u2014 the large-cam resize benefits visibly from `move-transition`'s interpolation rather than a hard cut.\n\n### Cinematic Mode \u2014 mood/variety, atmosphere-forward\n\n**Scene name:** `7 - Cinematic`\n\nFor VOD review, music sessions, or any segment where the Gotham atmosphere itself is the point rather than gameplay or chat density.\n\nOrdered source list:\n1. `FX - Weather (Cinematic)` **(new, packaging A5)** \u2014 the existing `.rain`/`.fog`/`.grain` classes from `gotham.css`, deliberately turned on as a full-scene layer rather than confined to the BRB/Starting-Soon cards.\n2. `HUD - Mic Bars` **(existing)** \u2014 reused from the screen-state scenes rather than the full match strip; this mode isn't about live match data.\n3. `CAM - Card` **(existing)**\n4. `CAM - Webcam` **(existing)**\n5. `CAM - Frame` **(existing)**\n6. `AUD - Music (Spotify)` / `AUD - Music (Browser)` / `AUD - Discord` **(existing)**\n7. `FX - Rim (Music)` **(existing)** \u2014 already the correct rim variant for non-mic-driven scenes, per its use in `3 - Starting Soon` / `4 - BRB` / `5 - Stream Ended`.\n\nTransition: `Bat Signal` **(existing `scene_as_transition`)** on entry/exit, since Cinematic Mode is a deliberate mood shift the stinger is built for; `Move` for anything internal.\n\n### Recording Mode \u2014 clean, no overlay, for off-platform capture\n\n**Scene name:** `8 - Recording (Clean)`\n\nFor local recording intended for editing (tutorial footage, montage source, anything that shouldn't carry the on-stream HUD).\n\nOrdered source list:\n1. `CAM - Frame` **(existing)** \u2014 kept, since a clean recording of gameplay commentary still usually wants the cam.\n2. `CAM - Webcam` **(existing)**\n3. `CAP - Valorant` / `CAP - Minecraft` / `CAP - The Finals` / `CAP - Any Game` **(existing, whichever is active)**\n4. `AUD - Game` / `AUD - Discord` (muted) / `AUD - Music (Spotify)` **(existing)**\n\nEverything else \u2014 every `HUD -`, `ALR -`, `IN -`, `FX -` source \u2014 is deliberately absent. This is the one mode that is cheaper than every other mode in the document, by design: it exists specifically so `source-record`/replay-buffer captures used for later editing (feeding F1/F2) aren't burning render budget on overlay elements that get cropped out anyway.\n\nTransition: `Cut` (no stinger, no move) \u2014 a recording-only scene has no viewer-facing transition moment to perform for.\n\n### Emergency Fallback Mode \u2014 low overhead, known-good audio\n\n**Scene name:** `9 - Fallback`\n\nThe automatic target for D3. Must render reliably even when the rest of the PC is under load \u2014 this is the scene that has to work when everything else is struggling, so it uses the fewest possible browser sources (each one is a Chromium process OBS has to keep live) and no `source-clone`/shader/ASR anywhere in it.\n\nOrdered source list:\n1. `SCR - BRB` **(existing)** \u2014 reused as-is; it's already the lightest full-screen card in the collection (no live match polling, just the static/animated screen).\n2. `HUD - Mic Bars` **(existing)** \u2014 the only HUD element kept, since confirming audio is still alive is the one piece of information that matters most when something's gone wrong.\n3. `AUD - Discord` **(existing)** \u2014 kept so the streamer can communicate with viewers/mods about what's happening even if game audio is the thing that broke.\n4. `FX - Rim (Music)` **(existing)**\n\nEverything else \u2014 all `CAP -`, `CAM -`, `IN -`, and match-polling `HUD -` sources \u2014 is dropped. `ALR - StreamElements` is also dropped here deliberately: if the stream is unstable enough to need the fallback, a new alert popping in is one more thing that can go wrong or distract from fixing the actual problem.\n\nTransition: `Cut`, triggered programmatically by D3's `obs-websocket` call \u2014 a transition animation during a reliability incident is exactly the kind of thing that should not be competing for frame time. **This is the one mode-level item that needs streamer sign-off on specifics before autopilot is allowed to invoke it unattended**, per D3's note above: confirm the exact dropped-frame/render-lag threshold and the debounce window before wiring the auto-switch live.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Items Needing Explicit Approval Before Building\n\nFlagged throughout, collected here for visibility:\n\n- **D3 (Automated Health-Triggered Fallback)** \u2014 autonomous scene-switching needs the exact trigger threshold and debounce window confirmed by the streamer before it's allowed to act without a human in the loop.\n- **B3 (GeoGuessr Round HUD)** \u2014 requires writing and installing a small custom browser extension with page access to geoguessr.com. Narrowly scoped and reviewable, but it's new third-party-adjacent code with host permissions, not just an OBS source \u2014 approve the extension's exact permission scope before installing.\n- **H3 (Interrogation Room Alt-Angle)** \u2014 requires an actual hardware purchase (second camera), not just software/config.\n- **H4 (Evidence Board Corkboard)** \u2014 not recommended at all, but if reconsidered later: never ship without a manual approval queue between upload and on-stream display; flagged as a hard requirement, not a suggestion.\n- **C5 (Interrogation Q&A) / any chat-submitted-text surface** \u2014 must reuse `chatbox.html`'s existing `esc()` sanitization pattern before rendering any chat-submitted string as HTML; confirm this is actually wired in before launch, not just assumed.\n"
  }
}
