## The camera fit — two real bugs, both fixed

Your Brio 100 exposes exactly one mode, **1920×1080 @ 30fps**, so the camera itself is clean 16:9. The misfit was on my side:

1. **Frame aspect was wrong.** I built each frame by adding a flat 12px to width *and* height, which gave 1.738–1.751 instead of 16:9. Now inflated proportionally (2.5% per edge).
2. **The real culprit — browser canvas pillarboxing.** The `Reactive Frame` sources had a **620×420** internal canvas (aspect 1.476). Scaled into a 16:9 box with "scale to inner bounds", OBS letterboxed it, so the frame rendered ~531px wide inside a 651px box — it shrank *inside* the camera instead of wrapping it. Canvas is now **640×360**.

I also pinned the capture mode explicitly (`res_type: 1`, 1920×1080, 30fps) so OBS stops renegotiating, and fixed the warmup bar strip's box, which had the same aspect mismatch (420×110 → 420×66).

Verified by render: the frame now sits evenly just outside the camera on all four edges, and your handcam is live.

## The VAL scenes — what each is for

They're all the same Valorant capture with different **handcam size, crop, and layout**, because a handcam wants to be small during play and large when mechanics are the point.

| Key | Scene | Handcam | Use it when |
|---|---|---|---|
| **F2** | Valorant | 400×225, bottom-right | Default. Normal rounds — cam small, HUD fully clear |
| **F8** | VAL - Agent Select | 540×304, larger | Agent select / buy phase. No minimap to protect, so the cam grows and live labels appear |
| **F9** | VAL - Clutch Cam | 470×264 | **1vX clutches.** Gameplay is center-cropped 2×, so viewers see the duel plus your mouse hand |
| **F10** | VAL - Warmup Split | 560×315 | Range / aim warmup. Gameplay boxed left, big handcam right, mic bars — built for talking through mechanics |
| **F11** | VAL - Replay | 400×225 | Shows the saved replay clip |

**Switching:** just press the F-key — OBS hotkeys are global, so they work while Valorant has focus. `Ctrl+M` toggles mic mute.

**The replay workflow:** press **F12** during a play → the script saves the last 30s from the replay buffer and loads it into `Replay Player`. Then **F11** to cut to the replay scene, and **F2** back to live when it ends. Practical pattern: clutch happens → F9 for the zoom → F12 as it ends → F11 to show it → F2.

One caveat from the research: a few games grab the keyboard exclusively and swallow OBS hotkeys. Test F2/F9 with Valorant focused before you rely on them mid-match; if they don't register, the fallback is the system tray or a Stream Deck.

